  acl2023-chi-plue.pdf  language en  blocks 1378
  0  p1  paragraph  [p1-l4]  PLUE: Language Understanding Evaluation Benchmark
  1  p1  paragraph  [p1-l5]  for Privacy Policies in English
  2  p1  paragraph  [p1-l6]  Jianfeng Chi1,2
  3  p1  paragraph  [p1-l7]  Wasi Uddin Ahmad3∗ Yuan Tian3
  4  p1  paragraph  [p1-l8]  Kai-Wei Chang3
  5  p1  paragraph  [p1-l9]  1Meta AI, 2University of Virginia, 3University of California, Los Angeles
  6  p1  paragraph  [p1-l10]  jianfengchi@meta.com,{wasiahmad,yuant,kwchang}@ucla.edu
  7  p1  paragraph  [p1-l11]  Abstract
  8  p1  paragraph  [p1-l12]  Privacy policies provide individuals with infor-
  9  p1  paragraph  [p1-l13]  mation about their rights and how their personal
  10  p1  paragraph  [p1-l14]  information is handled. Natural language un-
  11  p1  paragraph  [p1-l15]  derstanding (NLU) technologies can support
  12  p1  paragraph  [p1-l16]  individuals and practitioners to understand bet-
  13  p1  paragraph  [p1-l17]  ter privacy practices described in lengthy and
  14  p1  paragraph  [p1-l18]  complex documents. However, existing ef-
  15  p1  paragraph  [p1-l19]  forts that use NLU technologies are limited
  16  p1  paragraph  [p1-l20]  by processing the language in a way exclusive
  17  p1  paragraph  [p1-l21]  to a single task focusing on certain privacy
  18  p1  paragraph  [p1-l22]  practices. To this end, we introduce the Pri-
  19  p1  paragraph  [p1-l23]  vacy Policy Language Understanding Evalua-
  20  p1  paragraph  [p1-l24]  tion (PLUE) benchmark, a multi-task bench-
  21  p1  paragraph  [p1-l25]  mark for evaluating the privacy policy lan-
  22  p1  paragraph  [p1-l26]  guage understanding across various tasks. We
  23  p1  paragraph  [p1-l27]  also collect a large corpus of privacy policies
  24  p1  paragraph  [p1-l28]  to enable privacy policy domain-specific lan-
  25  p1  paragraph  [p1-l29]  guage model pre-training. We evaluate several
  26  p1  paragraph  [p1-l30]  generic pre-trained language models and con-
  27  p1  paragraph  [p1-l31]  tinue pre-training them on the collected corpus.
  28  p1  paragraph  [p1-l32]  We demonstrate that domain-specific continual
  29  p1  paragraph  [p1-l33]  pre-training offers performance improvements
  30  p1  paragraph  [p1-l34]  across all tasks. The code and models are re-
  31  p1  paragraph  [p1-l35]  leased at https://github.com/JFChi/PLUE.
  32  p1  paragraph  [p1-l36]  1 Introduction
  33  p1  paragraph  [p1-l37]  Privacy policies are documents that outline how a
  34  p1  paragraph  [p1-l38]  company or organization collects, uses, shares, and
  35  p1  paragraph  [p1-l39]  protects individuals’ personal information. Without
  36  p1  paragraph  [p1-l40]  a clear understanding of privacy policies, individu-
  37  p1  paragraph  [p1-l41]  als may not know how their personal information is
  38  p1  paragraph  [p1-l42]  being used or who it is being shared with. The pri-
  39  p1  paragraph  [p1-l43]  vacy violation might cause potential harm to them.
  40  p1  paragraph  [p1-l44]  However, privacy policies are lengthy and complex,
  41  p1  paragraph  [p1-l45]  prohibiting users from reading and understanding
  42  p1  paragraph  [p1-l46]  them in detail (Commission et al., 2012; Gluck
  43  p1  paragraph  [p1-l47]  et al., 2016; Marotta-Wurgler, 2015).
  44  p1  paragraph  [p1-l48]  Various natural language understanding (NLU)
  45  p1  paragraph  [p1-l49]  technologies have recently been developed to un-
  46  p1  paragraph  [p1-l50]  derstand privacy policies (Wilson et al., 2016a;
  47  p1  paragraph  [p1-l51]  Harkous et al., 2018; Ravichander et al., 2019;
  48  p1  figure  [p1-d1]  
  49  p1  paragraph  [p1-l52]  ∗Work done while at UCLA.
  50  p1  paragraph  [p1-l53]  Ahmad et al., 2020; Parvez et al., 2022; Ahmad
  51  p1  paragraph  [p1-l54]  et al., 2021; Bui et al., 2021). These tasks focus
  52  p1  paragraph  [p1-l55]  on understanding specific privacy practices at dif-
  53  p1  paragraph  [p1-l56]  ferent syntax or semantics levels and require sig-
  54  p1  paragraph  [p1-l57]  nificant effort for data annotations (e.g., domain
  55  p1  paragraph  [p1-l58]  experts). It is hard to develop generic pre-trained
  56  p1  paragraph  [p1-l59]  language models (e.g., BERT (Devlin et al., 2019))
  57  p1  paragraph  [p1-l60]  with task-specific fine-tuning using limited anno-
  58  p1  paragraph  [p1-l61]  tated data. Besides, the unique characteristics of
  59  p1  paragraph  [p1-l62]  privacy policies, such as reasoning over ambiguity
  60  p1  paragraph  [p1-l63]  and vagueness, modality, and document structure
  61  p1  paragraph  [p1-l64]  (Ravichander et al., 2021), make it challenging to
  62  p1  paragraph  [p1-l65]  directly apply generic pre-trained language models
  63  p1  paragraph  [p1-l66]  to the privacy policy domain.
  64  p1  paragraph  [p1-l67]  To address these problems and encourage re-
  65  p1  paragraph  [p1-l68]  search to develop NLU technologies in the pri-
  66  p1  paragraph  [p1-l69]  vacy policy domain, we introduce the Privacy Pol-
  67  p1  paragraph  [p1-l70]  icy Language Understanding Evaluation (PLUE)
  68  p1  paragraph  [p1-l71]  benchmark, to evaluate the privacy policy language
  69  p1  paragraph  [p1-l72]  understanding across six tasks, including text clas-
  70  p1  paragraph  [p1-l73]  sification, question answering, semantic parsing,
  71  p1  paragraph  [p1-l74]  and named-entity recognition. PLUE also includes
  72  p1  paragraph  [p1-l75]  a pre-training privacy policy corpus that we crawl
  73  p1  paragraph  [p1-l76]  from the websites to enable privacy policy domain-
  74  p1  paragraph  [p1-l77]  specific language model pre-training. We use this
  75  p1  paragraph  [p1-l78]  corpus to pre-train BERT (Devlin et al., 2019),
  76  p1  paragraph  [p1-l79]  RoBERTa (Liu et al., 2019), Electra (Clark et al.,
  77  p1  paragraph  [p1-l80]  2020), and SpanBERT (Joshi et al., 2020) and fine-
  78  p1  paragraph  [p1-l81]  tune them on the downstream tasks. We demon-
  79  p1  paragraph  [p1-l82]  strate that domain-specific continual pre-training
  80  p1  paragraph  [p1-l83]  offers performance improvements across all tasks.
  81  p1  paragraph  [p1-l84]  We will release the benchmark to assist natural
  82  p1  paragraph  [p1-l85]  language processing (NLP) researchers and practi-
  83  p1  paragraph  [p1-l86]  tioners in future exploration.
  84  p1  paragraph  [p1-l87]  2 Policy Language Understanding
  85  p1  paragraph  [p1-l88]  Evaluation (PLUE) Benchmark
  86  p1  paragraph  [p1-l89]  PLUE is centered on six English privacy policy lan-
  87  p1  paragraph  [p1-l90]  guage understanding tasks. The datasets and tasks
  88  p1  paragraph  [p1-l91]  are selected based on the following principles: (1)
  89  p1  paragraph  [p1-l92]  usefulness: the selected tasks can help practitioners
