  158  p2  paragraph  [p2-l59]  Table 1: Statistics of the PLUE datasets and tasks.
  159  p2  paragraph  [p2-l60]  in the domain quickly understand privacy practices
  160  p2  paragraph  [p2-l61]  without reading the whole privacy policy; (2) task
  161  p2  paragraph  [p2-l62]  diversity: the selected tasks focus on different se-
  162  p2  paragraph  [p2-l63]  mantic levels, e.g., words (phrases), sentences, and
  163  p2  paragraph  [p2-l64]  paragraphs; (3) task difficulty: the selected tasks
  164  p2  paragraph  [p2-l65]  should be adequately challenging for more room
  165  p2  paragraph  [p2-l66]  for improvement; (4) training efficiency: all tasks
  166  p2  paragraph  [p2-l67]  can be trainable on a single moderate GPU (e.g.,
  167  p2  paragraph  [p2-l68]  GeForce GTX 1080 Ti) for no more than ten hours;
  168  p2  paragraph  [p2-l69]  (5) accessibility: all datasets are publicly available
  169  p2  paragraph  [p2-l70]  under licenses that allow usage and redistribution
  170  p2  paragraph  [p2-l71]  for research purposes.
  171  p2  paragraph  [p2-l72]  2.1
  172  p2  paragraph  [p2-l73]  Datasets and Tasks
  173  p2  paragraph  [p2-l74]  PLUE includes six tasks in four categories. Ta-
  174  p2  paragraph  [p2-l75]  ble 1 presents an overview of the datasets and tasks
  175  p2  paragraph  [p2-l76]  within PLUE, and Table 4 in the Appendix gives
  176  p2  paragraph  [p2-l77]  an example for each task.
  177  p2  paragraph  [p2-l78]  OPP-115
  178  p2  paragraph  [p2-l79]  Wilson et al. (2016a) presented 115
  179  p2  paragraph  [p2-l80]  Online Privacy Policies (OPP-115). The dataset
  180  p2  paragraph  [p2-l81]  comprises website privacy policies with text seg-
  181  p2  paragraph  [p2-l82]  ments annotated with one or more privacy practices
  182  p2  paragraph  [p2-l83]  from ten categories (see Appendix A.1). We train a
  183  p2  paragraph  [p2-l84]  multi-label classifier to predict the privacy practices
  184  p2  paragraph  [p2-l85]  given a sentence from a policy document.
  185  p2  paragraph  [p2-l86]  APP-350
  186  p2  paragraph  [p2-l87]  Zimmeck et al. (2019) presented APP-
  187  p2  paragraph  [p2-l88]  350, a collection of mobile application privacy poli-
  188  p2  paragraph  [p2-l89]  cies annotating what types of users’ data mobile
  189  p2  paragraph  [p2-l90]  applications collect or share. Like OPP-115, each
  190  p2  paragraph  [p2-l91]  text segment in a policy document is annotated
  191  p2  paragraph  [p2-l92]  with zero or more privacy practices (listed in Ap-
  192  p2  paragraph  [p2-l93]  pendix A.2). In total, there are 30 data-type-related
  193  p2  paragraph  [p2-l94]  classes in APP-350, and we assign one more class,
  194  p2  paragraph  [p2-l95]  No_Mention, to those text segments that do not
  195  p2  paragraph  [p2-l96]  pertain to such practices.
  196  p2  paragraph  [p2-l97]  PrivacyQA
  197  p2  paragraph  [p2-l98]  Ravichander et al. (2019) proposed a
  198  p2  paragraph  [p2-l99]  question-answering dataset, PrivacyQA, comprised
  199  p2  paragraph  [p2-l100]  of 35 mobile application privacy policies. Given
  200  p2  paragraph  [p2-l101]  a question from a mobile application user and a
  201  p2  paragraph  [p2-l102]  sentence from a privacy policy, the task is to predict
  202  p2  paragraph  [p2-l103]  whether the sentence is relevant to the question.
  203  p2  paragraph  [p2-l104]  PrivacyQA includes unanswerable and subjective
  204  p2  paragraph  [p2-l105]  questions and formulates the QA task as a binary
  205  p2  paragraph  [p2-l106]  sentence classification task.
  206  p2  paragraph  [p2-l107]  PolicyQA
  207  p2  paragraph  [p2-l108]  Ahmad et al. (2020) proposed a read-
  208  p2  paragraph  [p2-l109]  ing comprehension (Rajpurkar et al., 2016) style
  209  p2  paragraph  [p2-l110]  dataset, PolicyQA. The dataset is derived from
  210  p2  paragraph  [p2-l111]  OPP-115 annotations that include a set of fine-
  211  p2  paragraph  [p2-l112]  grained attributes and evidence text spans that sup-
  212  p2  paragraph  [p2-l113]  port the annotations. Considering the annotated
  213  p2  paragraph  [p2-l114]  spans as the answer spans, PolicyQA generates
  214  p2  paragraph  [p2-l115]  diverse questions relating to the corresponding pri-
  215  p2  paragraph  [p2-l116]  vacy practices and attributes. The task is to predict
  216  p2  paragraph  [p2-l117]  the answer text span given the corresponding text
  217  p2  paragraph  [p2-l118]  segment and question.
  218  p2  paragraph  [p2-l119]  PolicyIE
  219  p2  paragraph  [p2-l120]  Ahmad et al. (2021) proposed a seman-
  220  p2  paragraph  [p2-l121]  tic parsing dataset composed of two tasks: intent
  221  p2  paragraph  [p2-l122]  classification and slot filling. Given a sentence in a
  222  p2  paragraph  [p2-l123]  privacy policy, the task is to predict the sentence’s
  223  p2  paragraph  [p2-l124]  intent (i.e., privacy practice) and identify the seman-
  224  p2  paragraph  [p2-l125]  tic concepts associated with the privacy practice.
  225  p2  paragraph  [p2-l126]  Based on the role of the slots in privacy practices,
  226  p2  paragraph  [p2-l127]  PolicyIE groups them into type-I and type-II slots.
  227  p2  paragraph  [p2-l128]  In total, there are four intent labels and 14 type-I
  228  p2  paragraph  [p2-l129]  and four type-II slot labels. We individually train
  229  p2  paragraph  [p2-l130]  a text classifier and sequence taggers to perform
  230  p2  paragraph  [p2-l131]  intent classification and slot filling, respectively.
  231  p2  paragraph  [p2-l132]  PI-Extract
  232  p2  paragraph  [p2-l133]  Bui et al. (2021) presented PI-Extract,
  233  p2  paragraph  [p2-l134]  a named-entity recognition (NER) dataset. It aims
  234  p2  paragraph  [p2-l135]  to identify what types of user data are (not) col-
  235  p2  paragraph  [p2-l136]  lected or shared mentioned in the privacy policies.
  236  p2  paragraph  [p2-l137]  It contains 4 types of named entities: COLLECT,
  237  p2  paragraph  [p2-l138]  NOT_COLLECT, SHARE and NOT_SHARE. Note that
  238  p2  paragraph  [p2-l139]  the named entities of different types may overlap.
  239  p2  paragraph  [p2-l140]  Thus, we report the results for collection-related
  240  p2  paragraph  [p2-l141]  and share-related entities, respectively.
  241  p2  paragraph  [p2-l142]  2.2
  242  p2  paragraph  [p2-l143]  Pre-training Corpus Collection
  243  p2  paragraph  [p2-l144]  The existing pre-trained language models (PLMs)
  244  p2  paragraph  [p2-l145]  mostly use data from BooksCorpus (Zhu et al.,
  245  p2  furniture  [p2-l146]  353
  246  p3  paragraph  [p3-l1]  2015) and English Wikipedia. Language models
  247  p3  paragraph  [p3-l2]  pre-trained on text from those sources might not
  248  p3  paragraph  [p3-l3]  perform well on the downstream privacy policy lan-
  249  p3  paragraph  [p3-l4]  guage understanding tasks, as privacy policies are
  250  p3  paragraph  [p3-l5]  composed of text written by domain experts (e.g.,
  251  p3  paragraph  [p3-l6]  lawyers). Gururangan et al. (2020) suggested that
  252  p3  paragraph  [p3-l7]  adapting to the domain’s unlabeled data (domain-
  253  p3  paragraph  [p3-l8]  adaptive pre-training) improves the performance
  254  p3  paragraph  [p3-l9]  of domain-specific tasks. Therefore, we collect
  255  p3  paragraph  [p3-l10]  a large privacy policy corpus for language model
  256  p3  paragraph  [p3-l11]  pre-training. In order to achieve broad coverage
  257  p3  paragraph  [p3-l12]  across privacy practices written in privacy policies
  258  p3  paragraph  [p3-l13]  (William, 2020; Ahmad et al., 2021), we collect the
  259  p3  paragraph  [p3-l14]  privacy policies from two sources: mobile applica-
  260  p3  paragraph  [p3-l15]  tion privacy policies and website privacy policies.
  261  p3  paragraph  [p3-l16]  Appendix B provides more details about how we
  262  p3  paragraph  [p3-l17]  collect these two types of privacy policies.
  263  p3  paragraph  [p3-l18]  2.3
  264  p3  paragraph  [p3-l19]  Models & Training
  265  p3  paragraph  [p3-l20]  Baselines
  266  p3  paragraph  [p3-l21]  We benchmark pre-trained language
  267  p3  paragraph  [p3-l22]  models (PLMs), BERT (Devlin et al., 2019),
  268  p3  paragraph  [p3-l23]  RoBERTa (Liu et al., 2019), SpanBERT (Joshi
  269  p3  paragraph  [p3-l24]  et al., 2020), Electra (Clark et al., 2020), and
  270  p3  paragraph  [p3-l25]  LEGAL-BERT (Chalkidis et al., 2020). We present
  271  p3  paragraph  [p3-l26]  the details of the PLMs in Appendix C.
  272  p3  paragraph  [p3-l27]  Domain-specific Continual Pre-training
  273  p3  paragraph  [p3-l28]  In or-
  274  p3  paragraph  [p3-l29]  der to adapt PLMs to the privacy policy domain,
  275  p3  paragraph  [p3-l30]  we continue to train BERT, Electra, SpanBERT,
  276  p3  paragraph  [p3-l31]  and RoBERTa on the pre-training corpus described
  277  p3  paragraph  [p3-l32]  in Section 2.2. We refer to them as PP-BERT, PP-
  278  p3  paragraph  [p3-l33]  RoBERTa, PP-SpanBERT, and PP-Electra, respec-
  279  p3  paragraph  [p3-l34]  tively.1 We present details in Appendix D.1.
  280  p3  paragraph  [p3-l35]  Task-specific Fine-tuning
  281  p3  paragraph  [p3-l36]  We fine-tune PLMs
  282  p3  paragraph  [p3-l37]  for each PLUE task. We only tune the learning
  283  p3  paragraph  [p3-l38]  rate for each task, as we found in the preliminary
  284  p3  paragraph  [p3-l39]  experiments that model performances are highly
  285  p3  paragraph  [p3-l40]  sensitive to the learning rate. We present more
  286  p3  paragraph  [p3-l41]  details in Appendix D.2.
  287  p3  paragraph  [p3-l42]  3 Experiment Results
  288  p3  paragraph  [p3-l43]  Tables 2 and 3 present the results for all the ex-
  289  p3  paragraph  [p3-l44]  periment models for PLUE tasks. Rows 2-9 show
  290  p3  paragraph  [p3-l45]  the results of the base PLMs and their correspond-
  291  p3  paragraph  [p3-l46]  ing variants with privacy policy domain-specific
  292  p3  paragraph  [p3-l47]  continual pre-training. Similar to GLUE (Wang
  293  p3  paragraph  [p3-l48]  et al., 2019), we also provide the average scores
  294  p3  paragraph  [p3-l49]  of all PLUE tasks in the last column of Table 3.
  295  p3  paragraph  [p3-l50]  We observe that the language models (PP-BERT,
  296  p3  figure  [p3-d1]  
  297  p3  paragraph  [p3-l51]  1We continually pre-train only the base models to mitigate
  298  p3  paragraph  [p3-l52]  the environmental impact of our experiments, but our code
  299  p3  paragraph  [p3-l53]  supports continual pre-training of large PLMs too.
  300  p3  paragraph  [p3-l54]  PP-SpanBERT, PP-Electra, PP-RoBERTa) adapted
  301  p3  paragraph  [p3-l55]  to the privacy policy domain outperform the gen-
  302  p3  paragraph  [p3-l56]  eral language models consistently in all the tasks,
  303  p3  paragraph  [p3-l57]  and PP-RoBERTa performs the best among all base
  304  p3  paragraph  [p3-l58]  models in terms of the average scores of all PLUE
  305  p3  paragraph  [p3-l59]  tasks. In particular, PP-RoBERTa performs the
  306  p3  paragraph  [p3-l60]  best for OPP-115, APP-350, PrivacyQA,2 and PI-
  307  p3  paragraph  [p3-l61]  Extract, among all base models. PP-BERT and
  308  p3  paragraph  [p3-l62]  PP-RoBERTa perform the best for PolicyQA; PP-
  309  p3  paragraph  [p3-l63]  Electra and PP-RoBERTa achieve the best perfor-
  310  p3  paragraph  [p3-l64]  mance for PolicyIE. In contrast, LEGAL-BERT
  311  p3  paragraph  [p3-l65]  (row 10) performs comparably or shows moder-
  312  p3  paragraph  [p3-l66]  ate improvements over BERT, indicating that pre-
  313  p3  paragraph  [p3-l67]  training on the general legal corpus does not neces-
  314  p3  paragraph  [p3-l68]  sarily help privacy policy language understanding.
  315  p3  paragraph  [p3-l69]  It is interesting to see that continual pre-training
  316  p3  paragraph  [p3-l70]  of the language models using the privacy policy
  317  p3  paragraph  [p3-l71]  domain data benefits them differently. For exam-
  318  p3  paragraph  [p3-l72]  ple, in the text classification tasks (i.e., OPP-115
  319  p3  paragraph  [p3-l73]  and APP-350), the performance difference between
  320  p3  paragraph  [p3-l74]  SpanBERT and PP-SpanBERT are most significant,
  321  p3  paragraph  [p3-l75]  while models using MLM (BERT and RoBERTa)
  322  p3  paragraph  [p3-l76]  already shows relatively high performance before
  323  p3  paragraph  [p3-l77]  continual pre-training and continual pre-training
  324  p3  paragraph  [p3-l78]  brings moderate gains to BERT and RoBERTa.
  325  p3  paragraph  [p3-l79]  We further investigate the improvement of large
  326  p3  paragraph  [p3-l80]  variants of PLMs over base variants of PLMs on
  327  p3  paragraph  [p3-l81]  PLUE tasks. Since PP-RoBERTaBASE performs the
  328  p3  paragraph  [p3-l82]  best among all base models, we also continue pre-
  329  p3  paragraph  [p3-l83]  train RoBERTaLARGE (PP-RoBERTaLARGE). As
  330  p3  paragraph  [p3-l84]  shown in the last five rows in Tables 2 and 3,
  331  p3  paragraph  [p3-l85]  the large pre-trained language models mostly out-
  332  p3  paragraph  [p3-l86]  perform their base counterparts. Noticeably, PP-
  333  p3  paragraph  [p3-l87]  RoBERTaLARGE is the best-performing model in
  334  p3  paragraph  [p3-l88]  APP-350, PolicyQA, PI-Extract, and sub-tasks in
  335  p3  paragraph  [p3-l89]  PolicyIE, and it also achieves the highest average
  336  p3  paragraph  [p3-l90]  scores of all PLUE tasks among all models.
  337  p3  paragraph  [p3-l91]  Lastly, even though domain-specific pre-training
  338  p3  paragraph  [p3-l92]  and large PLMs help boost the performance for all
  339  p3  paragraph  [p3-l93]  tasks, the performance of some tasks and datasets
  340  p3  paragraph  [p3-l94]  (e.g., APP-350, PrivacyQA, slot filling in PolicyIE)
  341  p3  paragraph  [p3-l95]  remains low, which indicates much potential for
  342  p3  paragraph  [p3-l96]  further work on NLP for the privacy policy domain.
  343  p3  paragraph  [p3-l97]  4 Related Work
  344  p3  paragraph  [p3-l98]  Privacy Policy Benchmarks
  345  p3  paragraph  [p3-l99]  The Usable Privacy
  346  p3  paragraph  [p3-l100]  Policy Project (Sadeh et al., 2013) is the most sig-
  347  p3  paragraph  [p3-l101]  nificant effort to date, resulting in a large pool of
  348  p3  paragraph  [p3-l102]  works (Wilson et al., 2016a,b; Sathyendra et al.,
  349  p3  figure  [p3-d2]  
  350  p3  paragraph  [p3-l103]  2Ravichander et al. (2019) reported 39.8% F1 score for
  351  p3  paragraph  [p3-l104]  BERT model; however, we are able to achieve 36.3%.
