  623  p4  paragraph  [p4-l255]  average scores of all PLUE tasks. We fine-tune all the models three times with different seeds and report average
  624  p4  paragraph  [p4-l256]  performances. Human performances are reported from the respective works.
  625  p4  paragraph  [p4-l257]  2016; Mysore Sathyendra et al., 2017; Bhatia and
  626  p4  paragraph  [p4-l258]  Breaux, 2015; Bhatia et al., 2016; Hosseini et al.,
  627  p4  paragraph  [p4-l259]  2016; Zimmeck et al., 2019) to facilitate the au-
  628  p4  paragraph  [p4-l260]  tomation of privacy policy analysis. A wide range
  629  p4  paragraph  [p4-l261]  of NLP techniques have been explored accordingly
  630  p4  paragraph  [p4-l262]  (Liu et al., 2014; Ramanath et al., 2014; Wilson
  631  p4  paragraph  [p4-l263]  et al., 2016a; Harkous et al., 2018; Zimmeck et al.,
  632  p4  paragraph  [p4-l264]  2019; Shvartzshanider et al., 2018; Harkous et al.,
  633  p4  paragraph  [p4-l265]  2018; Ravichander et al., 2019; Ahmad et al., 2020;
  634  p4  paragraph  [p4-l266]  Bui et al., 2021; Ahmad et al., 2021).
  635  p4  paragraph  [p4-l267]  Pre-trained Language Models
  636  p4  paragraph  [p4-l268]  In the last few
  637  p4  paragraph  [p4-l269]  years, NLP research has witnessed a radical change
  638  p4  paragraph  [p4-l270]  with the advent of PLMs like ELMo (Peters et al.,
  639  p4  paragraph  [p4-l271]  2018) and BERT (Devlin et al., 2019). PLMs
  640  p4  paragraph  [p4-l272]  achieved state-of-the-art results in many language
  641  p4  paragraph  [p4-l273]  understanding benchmarks. Consequently, PLMs
  642  p4  paragraph  [p4-l274]  have been developed for a wide range of domains,
  643  p4  paragraph  [p4-l275]  e.g., scientific (Beltagy et al., 2019), medical (Lee
  644  p4  paragraph  [p4-l276]  et al., 2020; Rasmy et al., 2021; Alsentzer et al.,
  645  p4  paragraph  [p4-l277]  2019), legal (Chalkidis et al., 2020), and cybersecu-
  646  p4  paragraph  [p4-l278]  rity (Ranade et al., 2021; Bayer et al., 2022). This
  647  p4  furniture  [p4-l279]  355
  648  p5  paragraph  [p5-l1]  work investigates the adaptation of PLMs to facili-
  649  p5  paragraph  [p5-l2]  tate NLP research in the privacy policy domain.
  650  p5  paragraph  [p5-l3]  5 Conclusion and Future work
  651  p5  paragraph  [p5-l4]  Reliable aggregation of datasets and benchmark-
  652  p5  paragraph  [p5-l5]  ing foundation models on them facilitate future
  653  p5  paragraph  [p5-l6]  research. This work presents PLUE, a benchmark
  654  p5  paragraph  [p5-l7]  for training and evaluating new security and pri-
  655  p5  paragraph  [p5-l8]  vacy policy models. PLUE will help researchers
  656  p5  paragraph  [p5-l9]  benchmark policy language understanding under a
  657  p5  paragraph  [p5-l10]  unified setup and facilitate reliable comparison.
  658  p5  paragraph  [p5-l11]  PLUE also presents some challenges in lan-
  659  p5  paragraph  [p5-l12]  guage understanding evaluation for privacy poli-
  660  p5  paragraph  [p5-l13]  cies. For example, the imbalance data issue for
  661  p5  paragraph  [p5-l14]  privacy practices is a major challenge in the Priva-
  662  p5  paragraph  [p5-l15]  cyQA task (Parvez et al., 2022). Data efficiency is
  663  p5  paragraph  [p5-l16]  also a challenge for continual pre-training as the
  664  p5  paragraph  [p5-l17]  amount of unlabeled data is also small for this do-
  665  p5  paragraph  [p5-l18]  main. Approaches such as (Qin et al., 2022) could
  666  p5  paragraph  [p5-l19]  be investigated to continually adapt LMs for the
  667  p5  paragraph  [p5-l20]  emerging data in this domain.
  668  p5  paragraph  [p5-l21]  Limitations
  669  p5  paragraph  [p5-l22]  The pre-training privacy policy corpus and the
  670  p5  paragraph  [p5-l23]  downstream task datasets are unlikely to contain
  671  p5  paragraph  [p5-l24]  toxic or biased content. Therefore, they should
  672  p5  paragraph  [p5-l25]  not magnify toxicity or bias in the pre-trained
  673  p5  paragraph  [p5-l26]  and fine-tuned models, although the models may
  674  p5  paragraph  [p5-l27]  exhibit such behavior due to their original pre-
  675  p5  paragraph  [p5-l28]  training. The pre-training and benchmark datasets
  676  p5  paragraph  [p5-l29]  are formed based on privacy policies crawled in
  677  p5  paragraph  [p5-l30]  the past; as a result, they could be outdated by now.
  678  p5  paragraph  [p5-l31]  This work focuses on the English language only,
  679  p5  paragraph  [p5-l32]  and the findings may not apply to other languages.
  680  p5  paragraph  [p5-l33]  Ethics Statement
  681  p5  paragraph  [p5-l34]  License
  682  p5  paragraph  [p5-l35]  The OPP-115 and APP-350 datasets
  683  p5  paragraph  [p5-l36]  are made available for research, teaching, and
  684  p5  paragraph  [p5-l37]  scholarship purposes only, with further parameters
  685  p5  paragraph  [p5-l38]  in the spirit of a Creative Commons Attribution-
  686  p5  paragraph  [p5-l39]  NonCommercial License (CC BY-NC). The Pol-
  687  p5  paragraph  [p5-l40]  icyQA and PI-Extract datasets are derived from
  688  p5  paragraph  [p5-l41]  OPP-115 datasets. The PrivacyQA and PolicyIE
  689  p5  paragraph  [p5-l42]  datasets are released under an MIT license. The
  690  p5  paragraph  [p5-l43]  pre-training corpus, MAPS Policies Dataset, is re-
  691  p5  paragraph  [p5-l44]  leased under CC BY-NC. We strictly adhere to
  692  p5  paragraph  [p5-l45]  these licenses and will release the PLUE bench-
  693  p5  paragraph  [p5-l46]  mark resources under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0.
  694  p5  paragraph  [p5-l47]  Carbon Footprint
  695  p5  paragraph  [p5-l48]  We only use RoBERTa large
  696  p5  paragraph  [p5-l49]  models for continual training on the privacy pol-
  697  p5  paragraph  [p5-l50]  icy domain to reduce the environmental impacts
  698  p5  paragraph  [p5-l51]  of training large models.
  699  p5  paragraph  [p5-l52]  The PP-BERT, PP-
  700  p5  paragraph  [p5-l53]  SpanBERT, PP-Electra, and PP-RoBERTa models
  701  p5  paragraph  [p5-l54]  were trained for 100k steps on Tesla V100 GPUs
  702  p5  paragraph  [p5-l55]  that took 1-2 days. Therefore, the training would
  703  p5  paragraph  [p5-l56]  emit only 9kg of carbon into the environment.3
  704  p5  paragraph  [p5-l57]  All fine-tuning experiments were very lightweight
  705  p5  paragraph  [p5-l58]  due to the small size of the datasets, resulting in
  706  p5  paragraph  [p5-l59]  approximately 12kg of carbon emission.
