  1146  p10  figure  [p10-d7]  
  1147  p10  paragraph  [p10-l41]  Table 4: Examples from the tasks in PLUE.
  1148  p10  paragraph  [p10-l42]  and uses more training data to achieve remarkable
  1149  p10  paragraph  [p10-l43]  performance improvements. Note that while BERT,
  1150  p10  paragraph  [p10-l44]  Electra, and SpanBERT use the same vocabulary,
  1151  p10  paragraph  [p10-l45]  RoBERTa uses a different vocabulary resulting in
  1152  p10  paragraph  [p10-l46]  15M more parameters in the model.
  1153  p10  paragraph  [p10-l47]  LEGAL-BERT
  1154  p10  paragraph  [p10-l48]  Chalkidis et al. (2020) pre-
  1155  p10  paragraph  [p10-l49]  trained BERT using 12 GB of the English text
  1156  p10  paragraph  [p10-l50]  (over 351K documents) from several legal fields
  1157  p10  paragraph  [p10-l51]  (e.g., contracts, legislation, court cases) scraped
  1158  p10  paragraph  [p10-l52]  from publicly available resources. Since privacy
  1159  p10  paragraph  [p10-l53]  policies serve as official documents to protect the
  1160  p10  paragraph  [p10-l54]  company and consumers’ privacy rights and might
  1161  p10  paragraph  [p10-l55]  contain contents in response to privacy law (e.g.,
  1162  p10  paragraph  [p10-l56]  GDPR), we study LEGAL-BERT’s effectiveness
  1163  p10  paragraph  [p10-l57]  on the PLUE tasks.
  1164  p10  furniture  [p10-l58]  361
  1165  p11  paragraph  [p11-l1]  D More Implementation Details
  1166  p11  paragraph  [p11-l2]  D.1
  1167  p11  paragraph  [p11-l3]  Domain-specific Continual Pre-training
  1168  p11  paragraph  [p11-l4]  Since BERT, Electra, and SpanBERT share the
  1169  p11  paragraph  [p11-l5]  same model architectures, we use almost the same
  1170  p11  paragraph  [p11-l6]  hyperparameters (e.g., learning rate, train steps,
  1171  p11  paragraph  [p11-l7]  batch size) for them following the original papers.
  1172  p11  paragraph  [p11-l8]  We scale down the train steps by the same factor,
  1173  p11  paragraph  [p11-l9]  as the size of our pre-training corpus is roughly
  1174  p11  paragraph  [p11-l10]  1/10 the size of the pre-training corpus of BERT.
  1175  p11  paragraph  [p11-l11]  We adhere to the guidelines outlined in Liu et al.
  1176  p11  paragraph  [p11-l12]  (2019) to train RoBERTa with larger batch size,
  1177  p11  paragraph  [p11-l13]  higher learning rate, and fewer train steps. Table 5
  1178  p11  paragraph  [p11-l14]  presents the training hyperparameters for PLMs.
  1179  p11  paragraph  [p11-l15]  D.2
  1180  p11  paragraph  [p11-l16]  Task-specific Fine-tuning
  1181  p11  paragraph  [p11-l17]  We fine-tune the models for each task using the
  1182  p11  paragraph  [p11-l18]  Adam (Kingma and Ba, 2015) optimizer with a
  1183  p11  paragraph  [p11-l19]  batch size of 32. We fine-tune the models on the
  1184  p11  paragraph  [p11-l20]  QA tasks for 3 epochs and other tasks for 20 epochs
  1185  p11  paragraph  [p11-l21]  and perform a grid search on the learning rate for
  1186  p11  paragraph  [p11-l22]  each task with validation examples. We chose the
  1187  p11  paragraph  [p11-l23]  learning rate for tasks without validation examples
  1188  p11  paragraph  [p11-l24]  based on our findings from the tasks with validation
  1189  p11  paragraph  [p11-l25]  examples. Table 6 lists the hyperparameters for all
  1190  p11  paragraph  [p11-l26]  the downstream tasks.
  1191  p11  paragraph  [p11-l27]  In OPP-115 and APP-350, we compute the class
  1192  p11  paragraph  [p11-l28]  weights (the class weights are inversely propor-
  1193  p11  paragraph  [p11-l29]  tional to the occurrences of the classes) and apply
  1194  p11  paragraph  [p11-l30]  them in fine-tuning, as we find out both datasets
  1195  p11  paragraph  [p11-l31]  have the class-imbalance problem and using class
  1196  p11  paragraph  [p11-l32]  weights brings gains to overall performance. We
  1197  p11  paragraph  [p11-l33]  also report the human performances for PrivacyQA
  1198  p11  paragraph  [p11-l34]  and PolicyIE from the original works.
  1199  p11  paragraph  [p11-l35]  D.3
  1200  p11  paragraph  [p11-l36]  Software Tools
  1201  p11  paragraph  [p11-l37]  To facilitate using PLUE, we release our implemen-
  1202  p11  paragraph  [p11-l38]  tation, which is built with Pytorch (Paszke et al.,
  1203  p11  paragraph  [p11-l39]  2019) and the Huggingface transformers5 pack-
  1204  p11  paragraph  [p11-l40]  age. Our implementation includes the continual
  1205  p11  paragraph  [p11-l41]  pre-training of our baselines and the evaluation
  1206  p11  paragraph  [p11-l42]  of any PLMs supported by the Huggingface trans-
  1207  p11  paragraph  [p11-l43]  formers package on the PLUE benchmark tasks.
  1208  p11  paragraph  [p11-l44]  In addition to PLUE datasets, we release the pre-
  1209  p11  paragraph  [p11-l45]  training corpus and all data pre-processing scripts,
  1210  p11  paragraph  [p11-l46]  including the pre-training corpus crawling scripts,
  1211  p11  paragraph  [p11-l47]  to assist future research in this area.
