# name: test_golden_file[acl2023-hardt-ellipsis]
  '''
  acl2023-hardt-ellipsis.pdf  language en  blocks 624
  0  p1  paragraph  [p1-l4]  Ellipsis-Dependent Reasoning: a New Challenge for Large Language
  1  p1  paragraph  [p1-l5]  Models
  2  p1  paragraph  [p1-l6]  Daniel Hardt
  3  p1  paragraph  [p1-l7]  Copenhagen Business School
  4  p1  paragraph  [p1-l8]  dha.msc@cbs.dk
  5  p1  paragraph  [p1-l9]  Abstract
  6  p1  paragraph  [p1-l10]  We propose a novel challenge for large lan-
  7  p1  paragraph  [p1-l11]  guage models: ellipsis-dependent reasoning.
  8  p1  paragraph  [p1-l12]  We define several structures of paired exam-
  9  p1  paragraph  [p1-l13]  ples, where an ellipsis example is matched to
  10  p1  paragraph  [p1-l14]  its non-ellipsis counterpart, and a question is
  11  p1  paragraph  [p1-l15]  posed which requires resolution of the ellipsis.
  12  p1  paragraph  [p1-l16]  Test results show that the best models perform
  13  p1  paragraph  [p1-l17]  well on non-elliptical examples but struggle
  14  p1  paragraph  [p1-l18]  with all but the simplest ellipsis structures.
  15  p1  paragraph  [p1-l19]  1 Introduction
  16  p1  paragraph  [p1-l20]  Ellipsis is a fundamental feature of human lan-
  17  p1  paragraph  [p1-l21]  guage, occurring in all registers, where parts of
  18  p1  paragraph  [p1-l22]  sentences are omitted, although the missing parts
  19  p1  paragraph  [p1-l23]  are essential for understanding the meaning. The
  20  p1  paragraph  [p1-l24]  following is an example of Verb Phrase Ellipsis
  21  p1  paragraph  [p1-l25]  (VPE)(Bos and Spenader, 2011):
  22  p1  paragraph  [p1-l26]  (1)
  23  p1  paragraph  [p1-l27]  William went running. Harold did too.
  24  p1  paragraph  [p1-l28]  (1) is understood as asserting that Harold went run-
  25  p1  paragraph  [p1-l29]  ning; that is, the hearer or reader naturally fills
  26  p1  paragraph  [p1-l30]  in the missing material. This is done by identify-
  27  p1  paragraph  [p1-l31]  ing the antecedent VP, went running, in the first
  28  p1  paragraph  [p1-l32]  sentence. The following is the non-elliptical coun-
  29  p1  paragraph  [p1-l33]  terpart of (1):
  30  p1  paragraph  [p1-l34]  (2)
  31  p1  paragraph  [p1-l35]  William went running. Harold went run-
  32  p1  paragraph  [p1-l36]  ning too.
  33  p1  paragraph  [p1-l37]  With such examples, we can test understanding
  34  p1  paragraph  [p1-l38]  of ellipsis by targeting the ellipsis phrase with a
  35  p1  paragraph  [p1-l39]  simple Yes/No question:
  36  p1  paragraph  [p1-l40]  (3)
  37  p1  paragraph  [p1-l41]  Did Harold go running?
  38  p1  paragraph  [p1-l42]  If a system answers the question incorrectly for (1),
  39  p1  paragraph  [p1-l43]  the ellipsis example, but answers correctly for (2),
  40  p1  paragraph  [p1-l44]  the non-elliptical counterpart of (1), we can ascribe
  41  p1  paragraph  [p1-l45]  the result specifically to the challenge of ellipsis,
  42  p1  paragraph  [p1-l46]  since the examples are otherwise identical.
  43  p1  paragraph  [p1-l47]  As with pronominal anaphora and other dis-
  44  p1  paragraph  [p1-l48]  course processes, there is great flexibility in the
  45  p1  paragraph  [p1-l49]  way ellipsis can occur in discourse. Example (1)
  46  p1  paragraph  [p1-l50]  involves two simple adjacent sentences. It is also
  47  p1  paragraph  [p1-l51]  possible for ellipsis or the antecedent to occur in
  48  p1  paragraph  [p1-l52]  embedded clauses. Furthermore, ellipsis can occur
  49  p1  paragraph  [p1-l53]  either before or after the antecedent. Finally, an
  50  p1  paragraph  [p1-l54]  arbitrary amount of material can intervene between
  51  p1  paragraph  [p1-l55]  the antecedent and the ellipsis occurrence.
  52  p1  paragraph  [p1-l56]  In this paper, we propose the challenge of
  53  p1  paragraph  [p1-l57]  ellipsis-dependent reasoning. This challenge con-
  54  p1  paragraph  [p1-l58]  sists of examples involving an ellipsis clause, the
  55  p1  paragraph  [p1-l59]  target. Each ellipsis example is paired with its non-
  56  p1  paragraph  [p1-l60]  elliptical counterpart, where the target clause is
  57  p1  paragraph  [p1-l61]  overt rather than elliptical. We then pose a question
  58  p1  paragraph  [p1-l62]  whose answer is dependent on the target clause. A
  59  p1  paragraph  [p1-l63]  key aspect of the challenge is that ellipsis occur-
  60  p1  paragraph  [p1-l64]  rences are possible in a variety of diverse structural
  61  p1  paragraph  [p1-l65]  configurations. We test a series of GPT-3 models
  62  p1  paragraph  [p1-l66]  (GPT) on several such ellipsis structures.
  63  p1  paragraph  [p1-l67]  2 Related Work
  64  p1  paragraph  [p1-l68]  There is a large literature concerning the prob-
  65  p1  paragraph  [p1-l69]  ing of language models from a variety of perspec-
  66  p1  paragraph  [p1-l70]  tives. Furthermore, there has been substantial work
  67  p1  paragraph  [p1-l71]  specifically addressing ellipsis in NLP. In this pa-
  68  p1  paragraph  [p1-l72]  per, we are proposing the challenge of ellipsis-
  69  p1  paragraph  [p1-l73]  dependent reasoning. This proposal builds on var-
  70  p1  paragraph  [p1-l74]  ious strands of prior research; below we consider
  71  p1  paragraph  [p1-l75]  some particularly relevant aspects of this literature.
  72  p1  paragraph  [p1-l76]  2.1
  73  p1  paragraph  [p1-l77]  Probing Models for Knowledge
  74  p1  paragraph  [p1-l78]  The Winograd Schema (Kocijan et al. (2022);
  75  p1  paragraph  [p1-l79]  Levesque et al. (2012)) involves test examples that
  76  p1  paragraph  [p1-l80]  use the linguistic problem of pronoun resolution
  77  p1  paragraph  [p1-l81]  to gain insight into the commonsense reasoning
  78  p1  paragraph  [p1-l82]  abilities of an AI system. To do this, the Winograd
  79  p1  paragraph  [p1-l83]  Schema requires pairs of examples that differ only
  80  p1  paragraph  [p1-l84]  in one specific, small way, as in (4):
  81  p1  paragraph  [p1-l85]  (4)
  82  p1  paragraph  [p1-l86]  The city councilmen refused the demonstra-
  83  p1  paragraph  [p1-l87]  tors a permit because they feared/advocated
  84  p1  paragraph  [p1-l88]  violence.
  85  p1  furniture  [p1-l89]  39
  86  p1  furniture  [p1-l1]  Proceedings of the 61st Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics
  87  p1  furniture  [p1-l2]  Volume 2: Short Papers, pages 39–47
  88  p1  furniture  [p1-l3]  July 9-14, 2023 ©2023 Association for Computational Linguistics
  89  p2  paragraph  [p2-l1]  With “feared", the pronoun “they" refers to the city
  90  p2  paragraph  [p2-l2]  councilmen, while with “advocated", it refers to the
  91  p2  paragraph  [p2-l3]  demonstrators. Humans understand this because
  92  p2  paragraph  [p2-l4]  of general, commonsense knowledge about what
  93  p2  paragraph  [p2-l5]  would reasonably explain the refusal of a permit
  94  p2  paragraph  [p2-l6]  in the two cases. It is difficult to ensure that such
  95  p2  paragraph  [p2-l7]  examples can only be solved through such sophis-
  96  p2  paragraph  [p2-l8]  ticated reasoning, and, according to Kocijan et al.
  97  p2  paragraph  [p2-l9]  (2022)[p. 8], “Solving Winograd schemas is not
  98  p2  paragraph  [p2-l10]  a surrogate for the ability to do commonsense rea-
  99  p2  paragraph  [p2-l11]  soning".
  100  p2  paragraph  [p2-l12]  A different approach is exemplified by Lin et al.
  101  p2  paragraph  [p2-l13]  (2019): here, examples are constructed which test
  102  p2  paragraph  [p2-l14]  specific aspects of linguistic knowledge of a sys-
  103  p2  paragraph  [p2-l15]  tem, namely, whether BERT embeddings “encode
  104  p2  paragraph  [p2-l16]  hierarchical information". For example, a task is
  105  p2  paragraph  [p2-l17]  defined to identify the main auxiliary verb in a
  106  p2  paragraph  [p2-l18]  sentence, even in cases where the main auxiliary
  107  p2  paragraph  [p2-l19]  is not the first auxiliary verb to appear. Training
  108  p2  paragraph  [p2-l20]  and testing datasets are automatically generated us-
  109  p2  paragraph  [p2-l21]  ing a context-free grammar for several such tasks
  110  p2  paragraph  [p2-l22]  involving hierarchical syntactic information.
  111  p2  paragraph  [p2-l23]  2.2
  112  p2  paragraph  [p2-l24]  Anaphora and Question Answering
  113  p2  paragraph  [p2-l25]  Quoref (Dasigi et al. (2019); Zhang and Zhao
  114  p2  paragraph  [p2-l26]  (2022)) is a question-answer dataset designed so
  115  p2  paragraph  [p2-l27]  that correct answers cannot be given unless a coref-
  116  p2  paragraph  [p2-l28]  erence relationship is correctly identified; that is,
  117  p2  paragraph  [p2-l29]  the reasoning involved in question answering is
  118  p2  paragraph  [p2-l30]  dependent on resolving coreference. This is, in a
  119  p2  paragraph  [p2-l31]  sense, the inverse of the Winograd schema, where
  120  p2  paragraph  [p2-l32]  resolving coreference is dependent upon reasoning.
  121  p2  paragraph  [p2-l33]  Just as with the Winograd schema, it is difficult to
  122  p2  paragraph  [p2-l34]  ensure that resolving this dependency is required
  123  p2  paragraph  [p2-l35]  for system success. (Dasigi et al., 2019)[p. 1] note
  124  p2  paragraph  [p2-l36]  that this is “challenging, because it is hard to avoid
  125  p2  paragraph  [p2-l37]  lexical cues that shortcut complex reasoning", and
  126  p2  paragraph  [p2-l38]  based on a random sample, found that coreference
  127  p2  paragraph  [p2-l39]  resolution was required for 78% of questions.
  128  p2  paragraph  [p2-l40]  2.3
  129  p2  paragraph  [p2-l41]  Ellipsis as a Task
  130  p2  paragraph  [p2-l42]  There has been substantial work on ellipsis as a
  131  p2  paragraph  [p2-l43]  discrete NLP task (Khullar (2020), Zhang et al.
  132  p2  paragraph  [p2-l44]  (2019); Kenyon-Dean et al. (2016); Bos and Spe-
  133  p2  paragraph  [p2-l45]  nader (2011)). Vanderlyn et al. (2022) surveys a
  134  p2  paragraph  [p2-l46]  variety of forms of what they call “implicit ref-
  135  p2  paragraph  [p2-l47]  erence", which includes ellipsis and related phe-
  136  p2  paragraph  [p2-l48]  nomena. Aralikatte et al. (2021) frame ellipsis as
  137  p2  paragraph  [p2-l49]  a question-answering task, i.e., a task of locating
  138  p2  paragraph  [p2-l50]  an antecedent, understood as a span of tokens in
  139  p2  paragraph  [p2-l51]  context. Aralikatte et al. (2021) report token F1
  140  p2  paragraph  [p2-l52]  scores of 78.66 for VPE and 86.01 for sluicing, an-
  141  p2  paragraph  [p2-l53]  other form of ellipsis. It’s important to note that the
  142  p2  paragraph  [p2-l54]  task here, of antecedent identification, is a sub-part
  143  p2  paragraph  [p2-l55]  of the ellipsis challenge. Before the antecedent is
  144  p2  paragraph  [p2-l56]  identified, an ellipsis occurrence must be identified,
  145  p2  paragraph  [p2-l57]  and after the antecedent is identified, it must be
  146  p2  paragraph  [p2-l58]  interpreted, or “reconstructed", at the ellipsis site.
  147  p2  paragraph  [p2-l59]  2.4
  148  p2  paragraph  [p2-l60]  Relevance for Ellipsis-Dependent
  149  p2  paragraph  [p2-l61]  Reasoning
  150  p2  paragraph  [p2-l62]  The specific task of ellipsis is addressed in work
  151  p2  paragraph  [p2-l63]  like that of Aralikatte et al. (2021), but the key
  152  p2  paragraph  [p2-l64]  difference here is that we are probing for a com-
  153  p2  paragraph  [p2-l65]  plete solution to the ellipsis problem. The proposed
  154  p2  paragraph  [p2-l66]  ellipsis-dependent reasoning task involves a ques-
  155  p2  paragraph  [p2-l67]  tion that can only be answered correctly if the el-
  156  p2  paragraph  [p2-l68]  lipsis is properly identified and interpreted. This
  157  p2  paragraph  [p2-l69]  combines aspects of the preceding works in a novel
  158  p2  paragraph  [p2-l70]  way: like the Winograd schema and the syntactic
  159  p2  paragraph  [p2-l71]  work by Lin et al. (2019), it probes for what we
  160  p2  paragraph  [p2-l72]  see as a specific type of psychologically-defined
  161  p2  paragraph  [p2-l73]  knowledge: namely, a representation of context
  162  p2  paragraph  [p2-l74]  that supports the resolution of ellipsis. Similarly to
  163  p2  paragraph  [p2-l75]  the work on Quoref, we use targeted questions to
  164  p2  paragraph  [p2-l76]  probe for discourse-related knowledge.
  165  p2  paragraph  [p2-l77]  There is an extensive literature on the contextual
  166  p2  paragraph  [p2-l78]  interpretation of natural language, resting on the
  167  p2  paragraph  [p2-l79]  idea of a dynamic, ongoing model of discourse. For
  168  p2  paragraph  [p2-l80]  example, Discourse Representation Theory (Kamp,
  169  p2  paragraph  [p2-l81]  1981) describes a semantic model supporting dis-
  170  p2  paragraph  [p2-l82]  course phenomena such as pronominal and tempo-
  171  p2  paragraph  [p2-l83]  ral anaphora, and Sag and Hankamer (1984) argue
  172  p2  paragraph  [p2-l84]  explicitly that ellipsis and other such phenomena
  173  p2  paragraph  [p2-l85]  are interpreted with respect to a discourse model
  174  p2  paragraph  [p2-l86]  (Garnham, 2010). As one study puts it, “Inter-
  175  p2  paragraph  [p2-l87]  preting a verb-phrase ellipsis (VP ellipsis) requires
  176  p2  paragraph  [p2-l88]  accessing an antecedent in memory, and then in-
  177  p2  paragraph  [p2-l89]  tegrating a representation of this antecedent into
  178  p2  paragraph  [p2-l90]  the local context" (Martin and McElree, 2008). In
  179  p2  paragraph  [p2-l91]  this paper, we seek to determine whether a large
  180  p2  paragraph  [p2-l92]  language model is capable of such an interpretive
  181  p2  paragraph  [p2-l93]  process.
  182  p2  paragraph  [p2-l94]  3 Data
  183  p2  paragraph  [p2-l95]  There is a great deal of variety in the structural
  184  p2  paragraph  [p2-l96]  configurations in which ellipsis can occur. In ta-
  185  p2  paragraph  [p2-l97]  bles 1 and 2 we define structures for ellipsis and
  186  p2  paragraph  [p2-l98]  antecedent occurrences.
  187  p2  paragraph  [p2-l99]  In all structures, there is an ellipsis occurrence,
  188  p2  paragraph  [p2-l100]  and the question targets the ellipsis occurrence. Fur-
  189  p2  paragraph  [p2-l101]  thermore, each ellipsis example is paired with a
  190  p2  paragraph  [p2-l102]  non-ellipsis version. The first two structures, Sep-
  191  p2  furniture  [p2-l103]  40
