  0  p1  paragraph  [p1-l4]  A Fast Algorithm for Computing Prefix Probabilities
  1  p1  paragraph  [p1-l5]  Franz Nowak
  2  p1  paragraph  [p1-l6]  Ryan Cotterell
  3  p1  paragraph  [p1-l7]  {franz.nowak, ryan.cotterell}@inf.ethz.ch
  4  p1  figure  [p1-i1..p1-d5 (6)]  
  5  p1  paragraph  [p1-l8]  Abstract
  6  p1  paragraph  [p1-l9]  Multiple algorithms are known for efficiently
  7  p1  paragraph  [p1-l10]  calculating the prefix probability of a string
  8  p1  paragraph  [p1-l11]  under a probabilistic context-free grammar
  9  p1  paragraph  [p1-l12]  (PCFG). Good algorithms for the problem have
  10  p1  paragraph  [p1-l13]  a runtime cubic in the length of the input string.
  11  p1  paragraph  [p1-l14]  However, some proposed algorithms are subop-
  12  p1  paragraph  [p1-l15]  timal with respect to the size of the grammar.
  13  p1  paragraph  [p1-l16]  This paper proposes a novel speed-up of Je-
  14  p1  paragraph  [p1-l17]  linek and Lafferty’s (1991) algorithm, whose
  15  p1  paragraph  [p1-l18]  original runtime is O(N3 |N |3 + |N |4 ), where
  16  p1  paragraph  [p1-l19]  N is the input length and |N | is the number of
  17  p1  paragraph  [p1-l20]  non-terminals in the grammar. In contrast, our
  18  p1  paragraph  [p1-l21]  speed-up runs in O(N2 |N |3 + N3 |N |2 ).
  19  p1  figure  [p1-i2]  
  20  p1  paragraph  [p1-l22]  https://github.com/rycolab/
  21  p1  paragraph  [p1-l23]  prefix-parsing
  22  p1  paragraph  [p1-l24]  1 Introduction
  23  p1  paragraph  [p1-l25]  Probabilistic context-free grammars (PCFGs) are
  24  p1  paragraph  [p1-l26]  an important formalism in NLP (Eisenstein, 2019,
  25  p1  paragraph  [p1-l27]  Chapter 10). One common use of PCFGs is to con-
  26  p1  paragraph  [p1-l28]  struct a language model. For instance, PCFGs form
  27  p1  paragraph  [p1-l29]  the backbone of many neural language models, e.g.,
  28  p1  paragraph  [p1-l30]  recurrent neural network grammars (RNNGs; Dyer
  29  p1  paragraph  [p1-l31]  et al., 2016; Dyer, 2017; Kim et al., 2019). How-
  30  p1  paragraph  [p1-l32]  ever, in order to use a PCFG as a language model,
  31  p1  paragraph  [p1-l33]  one needs to be able to compute prefix probabilities,
  32  p1  paragraph  [p1-l34]  i.e., the probability that the yield of a derivation
  33  p1  paragraph  [p1-l35]  starts with the given string. In notation, given a
  34  p1  paragraph  [p1-l36]  string w = w1 · · · wN , we seek the probability
  35  p1  paragraph  [p1-l37]  p(S ∗⇒ w · · ·) where S is the distinguished start
  36  p1  paragraph  [p1-l38]  symbol of the grammar and ∗⇒ is the closure over
  37  p1  paragraph  [p1-l39]  applications of derivation rules of the grammar.1
  38  p1  paragraph  [p1-l40]  Our paper gives a more efficient algorithm for the
  39  p1  paragraph  [p1-l41]  simultaneous computation of the prefix probabil-
  40  p1  paragraph  [p1-l42]  ities of all prefixes of a string w under a PCFG.
  41  p1  paragraph  [p1-l43]  The authors are aware of two existing efficient
  42  p1  paragraph  [p1-l44]  algorithms to compute prefix probabilities under
  43  p1  paragraph  [p1-l45]  a PCFG.2 The first is Jelinek and Lafferty’s (1991)
  44  p1  figure  [p1-d6]  
  45  p1  paragraph  [p1-l46]  1 Specifically, α ∗⇒ β means that there exists an n ≥ 0
  46  p1  paragraph  [p1-l47]  such that α ⇒ · · · ⇒
  47  p1  paragraph  [p1-l50]  β, where ⇒ marks a derivation step.
  48  p1  paragraph  [p1-l48]  | {z }
  49  p1  figure  [p1-d7]  
  50  p1  figure  [p1-d8]  
  51  p1  paragraph  [p1-l49]  n times
  52  p1  paragraph  [p1-l51]  2Upon publication of this work, the authors were made
  53  p1  paragraph  [p1-l52]  aware of two other algorithms for finding prefix probabilities
  54  p1  paragraph  [p1-l53]  in the special case of idempotent semirings (Corazza et al.
  55  p1  paragraph  [p1-l54]  1994; Sánchez and Benedí 1997). See App. B for a discussion
  56  p1  paragraph  [p1-l55]  of prefix parsing under a semiring.
  57  p1  paragraph  [p1-l56]  algorithm which is derived from CKY (Kasami,
  58  p1  paragraph  [p1-l57]  1965; Younger, 1967; Cocke and Schwartz, 1970)
  59  p1  paragraph  [p1-l58]  and, thus, requires the grammar to be in Chomsky
  60  p1  paragraph  [p1-l59]  normal form (CNF). Jelinek–Lafferty runs in
  61  p1  paragraph  [p1-l60]  O(N3 |N |3 + |N |4 ) time, where N is the length
  62  p1  paragraph  [p1-l61]  of the input and N is the number of non-terminals
  63  p1  paragraph  [p1-l62]  of the grammar, slower than the O(N3 |N |3 )
  64  p1  paragraph  [p1-l63]  required for parsing with CKY, when the number
  65  p1  paragraph  [p1-l64]  of non-terminals |N | is taken into account.
  66  p1  paragraph  [p1-l65]  The second, due to Stolcke (1995), is derived
  67  p1  paragraph  [p1-l66]  from Earley parsing (Earley, 1970) and can parse
  68  p1  paragraph  [p1-l67]  arbitrary PCFGs,3 with a runtime of O(N3 |N |3 ).
  69  p1  paragraph  [p1-l68]  Many previous authors have improved the runtime
  70  p1  paragraph  [p1-l69]  of Earley’s (Graham et al., 1980; Leermakers
  71  p1  paragraph  [p1-l70]  et al., 1992; Moore, 2000, inter alia), and Opedal
  72  p1  paragraph  [p1-l71]  et al. (2023) successfully applied this speed-up
  73  p1  paragraph  [p1-l72]  to computing prefix probabilities, achieving a
  74  p1  paragraph  [p1-l73]  runtime of O(N3 |G|), where |G| is the size of
  75  p1  paragraph  [p1-l74]  the grammar, that is, the sum of the number of
  76  p1  paragraph  [p1-l75]  symbols in all production rules.
  77  p1  paragraph  [p1-l76]  Our paper provides a more efficient version of
  78  p1  paragraph  [p1-l77]  Jelinek and Lafferty (1991) for the computation of
  79  p1  paragraph  [p1-l78]  prefix probabilities under a PCFG in CNF. Specif-
  80  p1  paragraph  [p1-l79]  ically, we give an O(N2 |N |3 + N3 |N |2 ) time al-
  81  p1  paragraph  [p1-l80]  gorithm, which is the fastest attested in the liter-
  82  p1  paragraph  [p1-l81]  ature for dense grammars in CNF,4 matching the
  83  p1  paragraph  [p1-l82]  complexity of CKY adapted for dense grammars
  84  p1  paragraph  [p1-l83]  by Eisner and Blatz (2007).5 We provide a full
  85  p1  paragraph  [p1-l84]  derivation and proof of correctness, as well as an
  86  p1  paragraph  [p1-l85]  open-source implementation on GitHub. We also
  87  p1  paragraph  [p1-l86]  briefly discuss how our improved algorithm can be
  88  p1  paragraph  [p1-l87]  extended to work for semiring-weighted CFGs.
  89  p1  paragraph  [p1-l88]  2 Preliminaries
  90  p1  paragraph  [p1-l89]  We start by introducing the necessary background
  91  p1  paragraph  [p1-l90]  on probabilistic context-free grammars.
  92  p1  figure  [p1-d9]  
  93  p1  paragraph  [p1-l91]  3Note that Earley’s and, by extension, Stolcke’s algorithms
  94  p1  paragraph  [p1-l92]  also implicitly binarize the grammar during execution by using
  95  p1  paragraph  [p1-l93]  dotted rules as additional non-terminals.
  96  p1  paragraph  [p1-l94]  4A PCFG in CNF is dense if for every X, Y, Z ∈ N , we
  97  p1  paragraph  [p1-l95]  have a production rule X → Y Z ∈ R.
  98  p1  paragraph  [p1-l96]  5Note that there exist approximate parsing algorithms with
  99  p1  paragraph  [p1-l97]  lower complexity bounds (Cohen et al., 2013). Moreover,
  100  p1  paragraph  [p1-l98]  there are parsing algorithms that asymptotically run in sub-
  101  p1  paragraph  [p1-l99]  cubic time in the input length using fast matrix multiplication
  102  p1  paragraph  [p1-l100]  (Valiant, 1975; Benedí and Sánchez, 2007). However, they are
  103  p1  paragraph  [p1-l101]  of limited practical use (Lee, 1997).
  104  p1  furniture  [p1-l102]  57
  105  p1  furniture  [p1-l1]  Proceedings of the 61st Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics
  106  p1  furniture  [p1-l2]  Volume 2: Short Papers, pages 57–67
  107  p1  furniture  [p1-l3]  July 9-14, 2023 ©2023 Association for Computational Linguistics
  108  p2  paragraph  [p2-l1]  Definition 1. A probabilistic context-free gram-
  109  p2  paragraph  [p2-l2]  mar (PCFG) is a five-tuple G = (N , Σ, S, R, p),
  110  p2  paragraph  [p2-l3]  made up of:
  111  p2  paragraph  [p2-l4]  • A finite set of non-terminal symbols N ;
  112  p2  paragraph  [p2-l5]  • A finite set of terminal symbols Σ, Σ ∩ N =∅;
  113  p2  paragraph  [p2-l6]  • A distinguished start symbol S ∈ N ;
  114  p2  paragraph  [p2-l7]  • A finite set of production rules R ⊂ N ×
  115  p2  paragraph  [p2-l8]  (N ∪ Σ)∗ where each rule is written as X −→
  116  p2  paragraph  [p2-l9]  α with X ∈ N and α ∈ (N ∪ Σ)∗ . Here, ∗
  117  p2  paragraph  [p2-l10]  denotes the Kleene closure;
  118  p2  paragraph  [p2-l11]  • A weighting function p: R → [0, 1] assigning
  119  p2  paragraph  [p2-l12]  each rule r ∈ R a probability such that p
  120  p2  paragraph  [p2-l13]  is locally normalized, meaning that for all
  121  p2  paragraph  [p2-l14]  X ∈ N that appear on the left-hand side of a
  122  p2  paragraph  [p2-l15]  rule,
  123  p2  paragraph  [p2-l16]  P
  124  p2  paragraph  [p2-l18]  p(X −→ α) = 1.
  125  p2  paragraph  [p2-l17]  X−→α∈R
  126  p2  paragraph  [p2-l19]  Note that not every locally normalized PCFG
  127  p2  paragraph  [p2-l20]  constitutes a valid distribution over Σ ∗ . Specifi-
  128  p2  paragraph  [p2-l21]  cally, some may place probability mass on infinite
  129  p2  paragraph  [p2-l22]  trees (Chi and Geman, 1998). PCFGs that do con-
  130  p2  paragraph  [p2-l23]  stitute a valid distribution over Σ ∗ are referred to as
  131  p2  paragraph  [p2-l24]  tight. Furthermore, if all non-terminals of the gram-
  132  p2  paragraph  [p2-l25]  mar can be reached from the start non-terminal via
  133  p2  paragraph  [p2-l26]  production rules, we say the PCFG is trim.
  134  p2  paragraph  [p2-l27]  Definition 2. A PCFG G = (N , Σ, S, R, p) is in
  135  p2  paragraph  [p2-l28]  Chomsky normal form (CNF) if each production
  136  p2  paragraph  [p2-l29]  rule in R is in one of the following forms:
  137  p2  paragraph  [p2-l30]  X −→ Y Z
  138  p2  paragraph  [p2-l31]  (1)
  139  p2  paragraph  [p2-l32]  X −→ a
  140  p2  paragraph  [p2-l33]  (2)
  141  p2  paragraph  [p2-l34]  S −→ ε
  142  p2  paragraph  [p2-l35]  (3)
  143  p2  paragraph  [p2-l36]  where X, Y, Z ∈ N such that Y, Z ̸= S, a ∈ Σ,
  144  p2  paragraph  [p2-l37]  and ε is the empty string.6
  145  p2  paragraph  [p2-l38]  Definition 3. A derivation step α ⇒ β is an appli-
  146  p2  paragraph  [p2-l39]  cation of the binary relation ⇒: (N ∪ Σ)∗ × (N ∪
  147  p2  paragraph  [p2-l40]  Σ)∗ , which rewrites the left-most non-terminal in α
  148  p2  paragraph  [p2-l41]  according to a rule in R from the left-hand side of
  149  p2  paragraph  [p2-l42]  that rule to its right-hand side, resulting in β. The
  150  p2  paragraph  [p2-l43]  probability of a derivation step is the probability of
  151  p2  paragraph  [p2-l44]  the applied rule: p(αXγ ⇒ αβγ) def
  152  p2  paragraph  [p2-l45]  = p(X −→ β).
  153  p2  paragraph  [p2-l46]  Definition 4. A derivation under a grammar
  154  p2  paragraph  [p2-l47]  G is a sequence α0, α1, · · · , αm, where α0 ∈
  155  p2  paragraph  [p2-l48]  N , α1, · · · , αm−1 ∈ (N ∪ Σ)∗ , and αm ∈ Σ ∗ ,
  156  p2  paragraph  [p2-l49]  in which each αi+1 is formed by applying a deriva-
  157  p2  paragraph  [p2-l50]  tion step to αi . αm = w1 · · · wN ∈ Σ ∗ is called
  158  p2  paragraph  [p2-l51]  the yield of the derivation. If α0 is not the start
  159  p2  figure  [p2-d1]  
  160  p2  paragraph  [p2-l52]  6Note that any PCFG can be converted to an equivalent
  161  p2  paragraph  [p2-l53]  PCFG in CNF (Smith and Johnson, 2007).
  162  p2  paragraph  [p2-l54]  symbol S, we call it a partial derivation. We define
  163  p2  paragraph  [p2-l55]  p(α ∗⇒ β) as the sum of probabilities of all sub-
  164  p2  paragraph  [p2-l56]  sequences from α to β, where each subsequence
  165  p2  paragraph  [p2-l57]  probability is the product of the individual deriva-
  166  p2  paragraph  [p2-l58]  tion step probabilities defined in Def. 3.
  167  p2  paragraph  [p2-l59]  We represent derivations as trees whose structure
  168  p2  paragraph  [p2-l60]  corresponds to production rules, where any parent
  169  p2  paragraph  [p2-l61]  node is the non-terminal on the left-hand side of
  170  p2  paragraph  [p2-l62]  a rule and its children are the symbols from the
  171  p2  paragraph  [p2-l63]  right-hand side. The leaves of the tree, when read
  172  p2  paragraph  [p2-l64]  from left to right, form the yield. Such a tree, when
  173  p2  paragraph  [p2-l65]  rooted S, is called a derivation tree. Otherwise, it
  174  p2  paragraph  [p2-l66]  is called a derivation subtree.
  175  p2  paragraph  [p2-l67]  Definition 5. The probability of a derivation tree
  176  p2  paragraph  [p2-l68]  (or derivation subtree) τ is the product of the prob-
  177  p2  paragraph  [p2-l69]  abilities of all its corresponding production rules:
  178  p2  paragraph  [p2-l70]  p(τ ) def
  179  p2  paragraph  [p2-l71]  =
  180  p2  paragraph  [p2-l72]  Y
  181  p2  paragraph  [p2-l74]  p(α −→ β)
  182  p2  paragraph  [p2-l75]  (4)
  183  p2  paragraph  [p2-l73]  (α−→β)∈τ
  184  p2  paragraph  [p2-l76]  Definition 6. TX(wi · · · wk) is the set of all deriva-
  185  p2  paragraph  [p2-l77]  tion subtrees τ rooted at X with yield wi · · · wk.
  186  p2  paragraph  [p2-l78]  Definition 7. Given a PCFG G = (N , Σ, S, R, p),
  187  p2  paragraph  [p2-l79]  a string w = w1 · · · wN ∈ Σ ∗ , and a non-terminal
  188  p2  paragraph  [p2-l80]  X ∈ N , the inside probability of X between indices
  189  p2  paragraph  [p2-l81]  i and k (where 0 ≤ i ≤ k ≤ N) is defined as:
  190  p2  paragraph  [p2-l82]  β(i, X, k) def
  191  p2  paragraph  [p2-l83]  = p(X ∗⇒ wi+1 · · · wk)
  192  p2  paragraph  [p2-l84]  (5)
  193  p2  paragraph  [p2-l85]  =
  194  p2  paragraph  [p2-l86]  X
  195  p2  paragraph  [p2-l88]  p(τ )
  196  p2  paragraph  [p2-l89]  (6)
  197  p2  paragraph  [p2-l87]  τ∈TX(wi+1···wk)
  198  p2  paragraph  [p2-l90]  That is, the sum of the probabilities of all derivation
  199  p2  paragraph  [p2-l91]  trees τ starting at X that have yield wi · · · wk.
  200  p2  paragraph  [p2-l92]  Definition 8. Given a PCFG G = (N , Σ, S, R, p),
  201  p2  paragraph  [p2-l93]  a string w = w1 · · · wN ∈ Σ ∗ , and a non-terminal
  202  p2  paragraph  [p2-l94]  X ∈ N , we define the prefix probability π, i.e., the
  203  p2  paragraph  [p2-l95]  probability of w being a prefix under G, as:
  204  p2  paragraph  [p2-l96]  π(w | X) def
  205  p2  paragraph  [p2-l97]  = X
  206  p2  paragraph  [p2-l99]  p(X ∗⇒ wu)
  207  p2  paragraph  [p2-l100]  (7)
  208  p2  paragraph  [p2-l98]  u∈Σ∗
  209  p2  paragraph  [p2-l101]  In words, π is the probability of deriving w with
  210  p2  paragraph  [p2-l102]  an arbitrary continuation from X, that is, the sum
  211  p2  paragraph  [p2-l103]  of probabilities of deriving wu from X over all
  212  p2  paragraph  [p2-l104]  possible suffixes u ∈ Σ ∗ . In the following, we
  213  p2  paragraph  [p2-l105]  write the prefix probability of deriving prefix w =
  214  p2  paragraph  [p2-l106]  wi+1 · · · wk from X as π(i, X, k).
  215  p2  paragraph  [p2-l107]  Definition 9. Let G be a PCFG in CNF. Then for
  216  p2  paragraph  [p2-l108]  non-terminals X, Y, Z ∈ N , the left-corner expec-
  217  p2  paragraph  [p2-l109]  tations ξ(Y | X) and ξ(Y Z | X) are defined as:
  218  p2  paragraph  [p2-l110]  ξ(Y | X) def
  219  p2  paragraph  [p2-l111]  = X
  220  p2  paragraph  [p2-l113]  p(X ∗⇒ Yu)
  221  p2  paragraph  [p2-l114]  (8)
  222  p2  paragraph  [p2-l112]  u∈Σ∗
  223  p2  paragraph  [p2-l115]  ξ(Y Z | X) def
  224  p2  paragraph  [p2-l116]  = X
  225  p2  paragraph  [p2-l118]  ξ(X′ | X) · p(X′−→Y Z) (9)
  226  p2  paragraph  [p2-l117]  X′∈N
