  353  p3  paragraph  [p3-l111]  Figure 2: Visualization of left-corner expectations
  354  p3  paragraph  [p3-l112]  The left-corner expectation ξ(Y | X) is hence
  355  p3  paragraph  [p3-l113]  the sum of the probabilities of partial derivation
  356  p3  paragraph  [p3-l114]  subtrees rooted in X that have Y as the left-most
  357  p3  paragraph  [p3-l115]  leaf; see Fig. 2a for a visualization. Similarly,
  358  p3  paragraph  [p3-l116]  ξ(Y Z | X) is the sum of the probabilities of
  359  p3  paragraph  [p3-l117]  partial derivation subtrees that have Y and Z as the
  360  p3  paragraph  [p3-l118]  leftmost leaves; see Fig. 2b.
  361  p3  paragraph  [p3-l119]  3 Jelinek and Lafferty (1991)
  362  p3  paragraph  [p3-l120]  We now give a derivation of the Jelinek–Lafferty
  363  p3  paragraph  [p3-l121]  algorithm. The first step is to derive an expression
  364  p3  paragraph  [p3-l122]  for the prefix probability in PCFG terms.
  365  p3  paragraph  [p3-l123]  Lemma 1. Given a tight, trim PCFG in CNF and
  366  p3  paragraph  [p3-l124]  a string w = w1 · · · wN , the prefix probability of a
  367  p3  paragraph  [p3-l125]  substring wi+1 · · · wk of w being derived from X
  368  p3  paragraph  [p3-l126]  can be defined recursively as follows:
  369  p3  paragraph  [p3-l127]  π(i, X, k) = X
  370  p3  paragraph  [p3-l129]  ξ(Y Z | X)
  371  p3  paragraph  [p3-l128]  Y,Z∈N
  372  p3  paragraph  [p3-l131]  k−1
  373  p3  paragraph  [p3-l134]  (10)
  374  p3  paragraph  [p3-l130]  · X
  375  p3  paragraph  [p3-l133]  β(i, Y, j) · π(j, Z, k)
  376  p3  paragraph  [p3-l132]  j=i+1
  377  p3  paragraph  [p3-l135]  Base case (for all k ∈ 0 . . . N − 1 and all X ∈ N ):
  378  p3  paragraph  [p3-l136]  π(k, X, k+1) = X
  379  p3  paragraph  [p3-l138]  ξ(Y|X)·p(Y−→wk+1) (11)
  380  p3  paragraph  [p3-l137]  Y∈N
  381  p3  paragraph  [p3-l139]  Proof. A proof of Lem. 1 is given in App. A.
  382  p3  paragraph  [p3-l140]  The above formulation of the prefix probability is
  383  p3  paragraph  [p3-l141]  closely related to that of the inside probability from
  384  p3  paragraph  [p3-l142]  Baker’s (1979) inside–outside algorithm, which
  385  p3  paragraph  [p3-l143]  can be efficiently computed using CKY, see Alg. 1.
  386  p3  paragraph  [p3-l144]  Next, the left-corner expectations ξ as defined by
  387  p3  paragraph  [p3-l145]  Eq. (8) can be computed efficiently as follows. Let
  388  p3  paragraph  [p3-l146]  P denote the square matrix of dimension |N |, with
  389  p3  paragraph  [p3-l147]  rows and columns indexed by the non-terminals N
  390  p3  paragraph  [p3-l148]  (in some fixed order), where the entry at the i th row
  391  p3  paragraph  [p3-l149]  and the j th column corresponds to p(Xi −→ Xj _),
  392  p3  paragraph  [p3-l150]  i.e., the probability of deriving Xj on the left corner
  393  p3  furniture  [p3-l151]  59
  394  p4  paragraph  [p4-l1]  from Xi in one step (we use _ as a wildcard):
  395  p4  paragraph  [p4-l2]  p(Xi −→ Xj _) def
  396  p4  paragraph  [p4-l3]  = X
  397  p4  paragraph  [p4-l5]  p(Xi −→ Xj Y)
  398  p4  paragraph  [p4-l6]  (12)
  399  p4  paragraph  [p4-l4]  Y∈N
  400  p4  paragraph  [p4-l7]  We can find the probability of getting to non-
  401  p4  paragraph  [p4-l8]  terminal Xj after k derivation steps starting from
  402  p4  paragraph  [p4-l9]  Xi by multiplying P with itself k times:
  403  p4  paragraph  [p4-l10]  p(Xi k −→ Xj _) = (P k )ij
  404  p4  paragraph  [p4-l11]  (13)
  405  p4  paragraph  [p4-l12]  We can hence get the matrix P ∗ , whose entries cor-
  406  p4  paragraph  [p4-l13]  respond to deriving Xj from Xi after any number
  407  p4  paragraph  [p4-l14]  of derivation steps, by summing over all the powers
  408  p4  paragraph  [p4-l15]  of the matrix P: 7
  409  p4  paragraph  [p4-l18]  ∞
  410  p4  paragraph  [p4-l16]  P ∗ def
  411  p4  paragraph  [p4-l17]  = I + P + P 2 + P 3 + · · · = X
  412  p4  paragraph  [p4-l20]  P n
  413  p4  paragraph  [p4-l21]  (14)
  414  p4  paragraph  [p4-l19]  n=0
  415  p4  paragraph  [p4-l23]  ∞
  416  p4  paragraph  [p4-l22]  = I + P X
  417  p4  paragraph  [p4-l25]  P n = I + P P∗ = (I − P) −1
  418  p4  paragraph  [p4-l24]  n=0
  419  p4  paragraph  [p4-l26]  Note that the entry at the i th row and j th col-
  420  p4  paragraph  [p4-l27]  umn of P ∗ is exactly the left-corner expectation
  421  p4  paragraph  [p4-l28]  ξ(Xj | Xi). Finally, we can compute the left-corner
  422  p4  paragraph  [p4-l29]  expectations ξ(Y Z | X) using Eq. (9):
  423  p4  paragraph  [p4-l30]  ξ(Y Z | X) def
  424  p4  paragraph  [p4-l31]  = X
  425  p4  paragraph  [p4-l33]  ξ(X′ | X) · p(X′−→Y Z)
  426  p4  paragraph  [p4-l32]  X′∈N
  427  p4  paragraph  [p4-l34]  Lastly, for completeness, we also compute the pre-
  428  p4  paragraph  [p4-l35]  fix probability of the empty string, ε. For proba-
  429  p4  paragraph  [p4-l36]  bilistic PCFGs, this probability is simply 1 because
  430  p4  paragraph  [p4-l37]  ε is a prefix of any string:8
  431  p4  paragraph  [p4-l38]  π(k, X, k) def
  432  p4  paragraph  [p4-l39]  = π(ε | X) = 1
  433  p4  paragraph  [p4-l40]  (15)
  434  p4  paragraph  [p4-l41]  We can now combine the quantities derived
  435  p4  paragraph  [p4-l42]  above to obtain an efficient algorithm for the com-
  436  p4  paragraph  [p4-l43]  putation of prefix probabilities π(i, S, k). For the
  437  p4  paragraph  [p4-l44]  full algorithm, see Alg. 2.
  438  p4  paragraph  [p4-l45]  Proposition 1. The time complexity of the CKY
  439  p4  paragraph  [p4-l46]  algorithm as presented in Alg. 1 is O(N3 |N |3 ).
  440  p4  paragraph  [p4-l47]  Proof. Clearly, the computationally critical part is
  441  p4  paragraph  [p4-l48]  in lines 11–18, where we iterate over all indices
  442  p4  paragraph  [p4-l49]  of w for i, j, and k, as well as over the whole
  443  p4  paragraph  [p4-l50]  set of grammar rules, thus taking O(N3 |R|). In a
  444  p4  paragraph  [p4-l51]  PCFG in CNF, with the size of Σ taken as constant,
  445  p4  paragraph  [p4-l52]  the number of rules, |R|, is O(|N |3 ), making the
  446  p4  paragraph  [p4-l53]  overall complexity of CKY O(N3 |N |3 ).
  447  p4  paragraph  [p4-l54]  ■
  448  p4  figure  [p4-d1]  
  449  p4  paragraph  [p4-l55]  7Note that this sum converges if the PCFG is tight and trim
  450  p4  paragraph  [p4-l56]  since infinite derivation (sub)trees have zero probability mass.
  451  p4  paragraph  [p4-l57]  8 For a generalization of the algorithm that does not require
  452  p4  paragraph  [p4-l58]  locally normalized rule weights, see App. B.
  453  p4  paragraph  [p4-l59]  Proposition 2. The total time complexity of Jelinek–
  454  p4  paragraph  [p4-l60]  Lafferty is O(N3 |N |3 + |N |4 ):
  455  p4  paragraph  [p4-l61]  Proof. First, we precompute any values that are
  456  p4  paragraph  [p4-l62]  independent of the input. In lines 1–3, we pre-
  457  p4  paragraph  [p4-l63]  compute all the left-corner expectations ξ(Y | X)
  458  p4  paragraph  [p4-l64]  using Eq. (14), which has the complexity of in-
  459  p4  paragraph  [p4-l65]  verting the matrix P, i.e., O(|N |3 ), and move the
  460  p4  paragraph  [p4-l66]  values into a map of left-corner expectations in
  461  p4  paragraph  [p4-l67]  O(|N |2 ) (this is just for readability). In lines 4–
  462  p4  paragraph  [p4-l68]  5, we then use Eq. (9) to compute ξ(Y Z | X),
  463  p4  paragraph  [p4-l69]  iterating once over all non-terminals X for each
  464  p4  paragraph  [p4-l70]  rule, which takes O(|R||N |), that is, O(|N |4 ). Af-
  465  p4  paragraph  [p4-l71]  ter initializing the probabilities in lines 7–10 in
  466  p4  paragraph  [p4-l72]  O(N2 |N |), we begin by pre-computing all the in-
  467  p4  paragraph  [p4-l73]  side probabilities β in line 11 of Alg. 2, which takes
  468  p4  paragraph  [p4-l74]  O(N3 |N |3 ) by Prop. 1. Computing π(k, X, k + 1)
  469  p4  paragraph  [p4-l75]  for all X ∈ N by Eq. (11) in lines 12–14 takes
  470  p4  paragraph  [p4-l76]  O(N|N |2 ) as we iterate over all positions k ∈ N
  471  p4  paragraph  [p4-l77]  and over all Y ∈ N for each X ∈ N . And fi-
  472  p4  paragraph  [p4-l78]  nally, computing the π chart in lines 15–19 takes
  473  p4  paragraph  [p4-l79]  O(N3 |N |3 ) since we iterate over all ℓ, i, j ≤ N
  474  p4  paragraph  [p4-l80]  and X, Y, Z ∈ N . This yields an overall time com-
  475  p4  paragraph  [p4-l81]  plexity of O(N3 |N |3 + |N |4 ).
  476  p4  paragraph  [p4-l82]  ■
  477  p4  paragraph  [p4-l83]  4 Our Speed-up
  478  p4  paragraph  [p4-l84]  We now turn to our development of a faster dy-
  479  p4  paragraph  [p4-l85]  namic program to compute all prefix probabilities.
  480  p4  paragraph  [p4-l86]  The speed-up comes from a different way to
  481  p4  paragraph  [p4-l87]  factorize π(i, X, k), which allows additional mem-
  482  p4  paragraph  [p4-l88]  oization. Starting with the definition of the prefix
  483  p4  paragraph  [p4-l89]  probability in Eq. (16a), we first expand ξ(Y Z | X)
  484  p4  paragraph  [p4-l90]  by Eq. (9), as seen in Eq. (16b). Then, we factor
  485  p4  paragraph  [p4-l91]  out all terms that depend on the left-corner non-
  486  p4  paragraph  [p4-l92]  terminal Y in Eq. (16c), which we store in a chart
  487  p4  paragraph  [p4-l93]  γ, see Eq. (16e). We then do the same for all terms
  488  p4  paragraph  [p4-l94]  depending on X′ , factoring them out in Eq. (16d)
  489  p4  paragraph  [p4-l95]  and storing them in another chart δ, see Eq. (16f).
  490  p4  paragraph  [p4-l96]  Our improved algorithm for computing all prefix
  491  p4  paragraph  [p4-l97]  probabilities is shown in Alg. 3.
  492  p4  paragraph  [p4-l98]  Proposition 3. The complexity of our improved
  493  p4  paragraph  [p4-l99]  algorithm is O(N2 |N |3 + N3 |N |2 ).
  494  p4  paragraph  [p4-l100]  Proof. As before, Alg. 3 starts by precomputing
  495  p4  paragraph  [p4-l101]  and assigning the left-corner expectations in lines 1–
  496  p4  paragraph  [p4-l102]  3, which takes O(|N |3 ) and O(|N |2 ), respectively.
  497  p4  paragraph  [p4-l103]  We then initialize the prefix probabilities and com-
  498  p4  paragraph  [p4-l104]  pute the inside probabilities in lines 5–8, taking
  499  p4  paragraph  [p4-l105]  O(N2 |N |). As Eisner and Blatz (2007) show, one
  500  p4  paragraph  [p4-l106]  can compute β (line 9) in O(N2 |N |3 + N3 |N |2 ),
  501  p4  paragraph  [p4-l107]  thus improving the runtime of Alg. 1 for dense
  502  p4  paragraph  [p4-l108]  grammars. Pre-computing γ and δ in lines 10–14
