  621  p5  paragraph  [p5-l115]  ■
  622  p5  paragraph  [p5-l116]  5 Generalization to Semirings
  623  p5  paragraph  [p5-l117]  It turns out that Jelinek–Lafferty, and, by extension,
  624  p5  paragraph  [p5-l118]  our improved algorithm, can be generalized to
  625  p5  paragraph  [p5-l119]  work for semiring-weighted CFGs, with the same
  626  p5  paragraph  [p5-l120]  time complexity, under the condition that the
  627  p5  paragraph  [p5-l121]  semiring is closed, i.e., it has a Kleene star. This
  628  p5  paragraph  [p5-l122]  follows from the fact that the only operations used
  629  p5  paragraph  [p5-l123]  by the algorithm are addition and multiplication
  630  p5  paragraph  [p5-l124]  if we use Lehmann’s (1977) algorithm for the
  631  p5  paragraph  [p5-l125]  computation of left-corner expectations, ξ. We
  632  p5  paragraph  [p5-l126]  can even relax the condition of local normalization
  633  p5  paragraph  [p5-l127]  by changing how left-corner expectations and the
  634  p5  paragraph  [p5-l128]  weight of the prefix ε are computed. The relevant
  635  p5  paragraph  [p5-l129]  definitions and derivation of the adapted algorithm
  636  p5  paragraph  [p5-l130]  can be found in App. B.
  637  p5  paragraph  [p5-l131]  6 Conclusion
  638  p5  paragraph  [p5-l132]  In this paper, we have shown how to efficiently
  639  p5  paragraph  [p5-l133]  compute prefix probabilities for PCFGs in CNF,
  640  p5  paragraph  [p5-l134]  adapting Jelinek–Lafferty to use additional memo-
  641  p5  paragraph  [p5-l135]  ization, thereby reducing the time complexity from
  642  p5  paragraph  [p5-l136]  O(N3 |N |3 + |N |4 ) to O(N2 |N |3 + N3 |N |2 ).
  643  p5  paragraph  [p5-l137]  We thereby addressed one of the main limitations
  644  p5  paragraph  [p5-l138]  of the original formulation, of being slow for large
  645  p5  paragraph  [p5-l139]  grammar sizes.
  646  p5  furniture  [p5-l140]  61
  647  p6  paragraph  [p6-l1]  Limitations
  648  p6  paragraph  [p6-l2]  While we have improved the asymptotic running
  649  p6  paragraph  [p6-l3]  time of a classic algorithm with regard to grammar
  650  p6  paragraph  [p6-l4]  size, the time complexity of our algorithm is still
  651  p6  paragraph  [p6-l5]  cubic in the length of the input. Our result follows
  652  p6  paragraph  [p6-l6]  the tradition of dynamic programming algorithms
  653  p6  paragraph  [p6-l7]  that trade time for space by memoizing and reusing
  654  p6  paragraph  [p6-l8]  precomputed intermediate results. The usefulness
  655  p6  paragraph  [p6-l9]  of this trade-off in practice depends on the specifics
  656  p6  paragraph  [p6-l10]  of the grammar, and while the complexity is strictly
  657  p6  paragraph  [p6-l11]  better in terms of non-terminals, it will be most
  658  p6  paragraph  [p6-l12]  noticeable for denser grammars with many non-
  659  p6  paragraph  [p6-l13]  terminals.
  660  p6  paragraph  [p6-l14]  Ethics Statement
  661  p6  paragraph  [p6-l15]  We do not foresee any ethical issues arising from
  662  p6  paragraph  [p6-l16]  this work.
