  808  p8  paragraph  [p8-l1]  A Proof of Lem. 1
  809  p8  paragraph  [p8-l2]  Lemma 1. Given a tight, trim PCFG in CNF and a string w = w1 · · · wN , the prefix probability of a
  810  p8  paragraph  [p8-l3]  substring wi+1 · · · wk of w being derived from X can be defined recursively as follows:
  811  p8  paragraph  [p8-l7]  k−1
  812  p8  paragraph  [p8-l4]  π(i, X, k) = X
  813  p8  paragraph  [p8-l6]  ξ(Y Z | X) · X
  814  p8  paragraph  [p8-l9]  β(i, Y, j) · π(j, Z, k)
  815  p8  paragraph  [p8-l10]  (10)
  816  p8  paragraph  [p8-l5]  Y,Z∈N
  817  p8  paragraph  [p8-l8]  j=i+1
  818  p8  paragraph  [p8-l11]  Base case (for all k ∈ 0 . . . N − 1 and all X ∈ N ):
  819  p8  paragraph  [p8-l12]  π(k, X, k+1) = X
  820  p8  paragraph  [p8-l14]  ξ(Y | X) · p(Y−→wk+1)
  821  p8  paragraph  [p8-l15]  (11)
  822  p8  paragraph  [p8-l13]  Y∈N
  823  p8  paragraph  [p8-l16]  Proof. Eq. (11): The base case, π(k, X, k+1), is the probability of wk+1 being the left-most terminal
  824  p8  paragraph  [p8-l17]  in the parse subtree under X. It is, therefore, simply the sum of probabilities of any non-terminal Y being
  825  p8  paragraph  [p8-l18]  on the left corner of the parse subtree under X multiplied by the corresponding probability of Y directly
  826  p8  paragraph  [p8-l19]  deriving wk+1.
  827  p8  paragraph  [p8-l20]  Eq. (10): Given the PCFG is in CNF and assuming k > i + 1, in order to derive the prefix wi+1 · · · wk
  828  p8  paragraph  [p8-l21]  we must first apply some rule X −→ Y Z, where the first part of the substring is then derived from Y and
  829  p8  paragraph  [p8-l22]  the remainder (and potentially more) from Z:
  830  p8  paragraph  [p8-l27]  k−1
  831  p8  paragraph  [p8-l23]  π(i, X, k) = X
  832  p8  paragraph  [p8-l25]  p(X −→ Y Z)
  833  p8  paragraph  [p8-l26]  X
  834  p8  paragraph  [p8-l29]  β(i, Y, j) · π(j, Z, k) + π(i, Y, k)
  835  p8  paragraph  [p8-l30]  (17)
  836  p8  paragraph  [p8-l24]  Y,Z∈N
  837  p8  paragraph  [p8-l28]  j=i+1
  838  p8  paragraph  [p8-l31]  where the last term, π(i, Y, k), handles the case where the whole prefix is derived from Y alone.
  839  p8  paragraph  [p8-l32]  This term is clearly recursively defined through Eq. (17), with X replaced by Y.
  840  p8  paragraph  [p8-l33]  Defining
  841  p8  paragraph  [p8-l34]  R(Y, Z) def
  842  p8  paragraph  [p8-l35]  = Pk−1
  843  p8  paragraph  [p8-l36]  j=i+1 β(i, Y, j)π(j, Z, k), we can rewrite Eq. (17) as:
  844  p8  paragraph  [p8-l37]  π(i, X, k) = X
  845  p8  paragraph  [p8-l39]  p(X −→ Y Z) · R(Y, Z) + X
  846  p8  paragraph  [p8-l41]  p(X −→ A B) · π(i, A, k)
  847  p8  paragraph  [p8-l42]  (18)
  848  p8  paragraph  [p8-l38]  Y,Z∈N
  849  p8  paragraph  [p8-l40]  A,B∈N
  850  p8  paragraph  [p8-l43]  After repeated substitutions ad infinitum, we get:
  851  p8  paragraph  [p8-l44]  π(i, X, k) = X
  852  p8  paragraph  [p8-l46]  p(X ∗⇒ A B) X
  853  p8  paragraph  [p8-l48]  p(A −→ Y Z) · R(Y, Z)
  854  p8  paragraph  [p8-l49]  (19)
  855  p8  paragraph  [p8-l45]  A,B∈N
  856  p8  paragraph  [p8-l47]  Y,Z∈N
  857  p8  paragraph  [p8-l50]  Note that, in the last step, infinite derivations do not carry any probability mass since we assumed the
  858  p8  paragraph  [p8-l51]  PCFG to be tight and trim. Hence, the final form of the equation is:
  859  p8  paragraph  [p8-l52]  π(i, X, k) = X
  860  p8  paragraph  [p8-l54]  p(X ∗⇒ A B) X
  861  p8  paragraph  [p8-l56]  p(A −→ Y Z) · R(Y, Z)
  862  p8  paragraph  [p8-l53]  A,B∈N
  863  p8  paragraph  [p8-l55]  Y,Z∈N
  864  p8  paragraph  [p8-l60]  k−1
  865  p8  paragraph  [p8-l63]  (20)
  866  p8  paragraph  [p8-l57]  = X
  867  p8  paragraph  [p8-l59]  ξ(Y Z | X) X
  868  p8  paragraph  [p8-l62]  β(i, Y, j) · π(j, Z, k)
  869  p8  paragraph  [p8-l58]  Y,Z∈N
  870  p8  paragraph  [p8-l61]  j=i+1
  871  p8  paragraph  [p8-l64]  ■
  872  p8  furniture  [p8-l65]  64
  873  p9  paragraph  [p9-l1]  B Extension of Alg. 3 to Semirings
  874  p9  paragraph  [p9-l2]  In the following, we give the necessary background on semirings and then show how the algorithms
  875  p9  paragraph  [p9-l3]  introduced above can be framed in terms of semirings.
  876  p9  paragraph  [p9-l4]  B.1
  877  p9  paragraph  [p9-l5]  Semirings
  878  p9  paragraph  [p9-l6]  We start by introducing the necessary definitions and notation.
  879  p9  paragraph  [p9-l7]  Definition 10. A monoid is a 3-tuple ⟨A, ◦, 1⟩ where:
  880  p9  paragraph  [p9-l8]  (i) A is a non-empty set;
  881  p9  paragraph  [p9-l9]  (ii) ◦ is an associative binary operation: ∀a, b, c ∈ A,(a ◦ b) ◦ c = a ◦ (b ◦ c);
  882  p9  paragraph  [p9-l10]  (iii) 1 is a left and right identity element: ∀a ∈ A, 1 ◦ a = a ◦ 1 = a
  883  p9  paragraph  [p9-l11]  (iv) A is closed under the operation ◦: ∀a, b ∈ A, a ◦ b ∈ A
  884  p9  paragraph  [p9-l12]  A monoid is commutative if ∀a, b ∈ A : a ◦ b = b ◦ a.
  885  p9  paragraph  [p9-l13]  Definition 11. A semiring is a 5-tuple W = ⟨A, ⊕, ⊗, 0, 1⟩, where
  886  p9  paragraph  [p9-l14]  (i) ⟨A, ⊕, 0⟩ is a commutative monoid over A with identity element 0 under the addition operation ⊕;
  887  p9  paragraph  [p9-l15]  (ii) ⟨A, ⊗, 1⟩ is a monoid over A with identity element 1 under the multiplication operation ⊗;
  888  p9  paragraph  [p9-l16]  (iii) Multiplication is distributive over addition, that is, ∀a, b, c ∈ A:
  889  p9  paragraph  [p9-l17]  • a ⊗ (b ⊕ c) = a ⊗ b ⊕ a ⊗ c;
  890  p9  paragraph  [p9-l18]  • (b ⊕ c) ⊗ a = b ⊗ a ⊕ c ⊗ a.
  891  p9  paragraph  [p9-l19]  (iv) 0 is an annihilator for A, that is, ∀a ∈ A, 0 ⊗ a = a ⊗ 0 = 0.
  892  p9  paragraph  [p9-l20]  A semiring is commutative if ⟨A, ⊗, 1⟩ is a commutative monoid. A semiring is idempotent if ∀a ∈ A :
  893  p9  paragraph  [p9-l21]  a ⊕ a = a.
  894  p9  paragraph  [p9-l22]  Definition 12. A semiring W = ⟨A, ⊕, ⊗, 0, 1⟩ is complete if it is possible to extend the addition operator
  895  p9  paragraph  [p9-l23]  ⊕ to infinite sums, maintaining the properties of associativity, commutativity, and distributivity from the
  896  p9  paragraph  [p9-l24]  finite case (Rozenberg and Salomaa, 1997, Chapter 9). In this case, we can define the unary operation of
  897  p9  paragraph  [p9-l25]  the Kleene star denoted by a superscript ∗ as the infinite sum over powers of its operand, that is, ∀a ∈ A:
  898  p9  paragraph  [p9-l28]  ∞
  899  p9  paragraph  [p9-l26]  a ∗ def
  900  p9  paragraph  [p9-l27]  = M
  901  p9  paragraph  [p9-l30]  a i
  902  p9  paragraph  [p9-l29]  i=0
  903  p9  paragraph  [p9-l32]  Analogously to Eq. (14), it then follows that:
  904  p9  paragraph  [p9-l34]  ∞
  905  p9  paragraph  [p9-l37]  ∞
  906  p9  paragraph  [p9-l33]  a ∗ = M
  907  p9  paragraph  [p9-l36]  a i = a 0 ⊕ M
  908  p9  paragraph  [p9-l31]  (21)
  909  p9  paragraph  [p9-l40]  ∞
  910  p9  paragraph  [p9-l42]  a i = 1 ⊕ a ⊗ a ∗
  911  p9  paragraph  [p9-l39]  a i = 1 ⊕ a ⊗ M
  912  p9  paragraph  [p9-l35]  i=0
  913  p9  paragraph  [p9-l38]  i=1
  914  p9  paragraph  [p9-l44]  and, similarly:
  915  p9  paragraph  [p9-l46]  ∞
  916  p9  paragraph  [p9-l49]  ∞
  917  p9  paragraph  [p9-l43]  (22)
  918  p9  paragraph  [p9-l41]  i=0
  919  p9  paragraph  [p9-l51]  a i ⊗ a = 1 ⊕ a ∗ ⊗ a
  920  p9  paragraph  [p9-l45]  a ∗ = a 0 ⊕ M
  921  p9  paragraph  [p9-l48]  a i = 1 ⊕ M
  922  p9  paragraph  [p9-l47]  i=1
  923  p9  paragraph  [p9-l50]  i=0
  924  p9  paragraph  [p9-l52]  (23)
  925  p9  paragraph  [p9-l53]  We now discuss how complete semirings can be lifted to square matrices. The definitions follow
  926  p9  paragraph  [p9-l54]  analogously to matrices over the real numbers.
  927  p9  paragraph  [p9-l55]  Definition 13. We define semiring matrix addition as follows. Let A and B be d × d matrices whose
  928  p9  paragraph  [p9-l56]  entries are elements from a complete semiring W = ⟨A, ⊕, ⊗, 0, 1⟩. Then the sum ("+") of A and B is
  929  p9  paragraph  [p9-l57]  defined as:
  930  p9  paragraph  [p9-l58]  (A + B)ij def
  931  p9  paragraph  [p9-l59]  = Aij ⊕ Bij
  932  p9  paragraph  [p9-l60]  i, j ∈ 1, . . . , d
  933  p9  paragraph  [p9-l61]  (24)
  934  p9  furniture  [p9-l62]  65
  935  p10  paragraph  [p10-l1]  Definition 14. We define semiring matrix multiplication as follows. Let A and B be d × d matrices
  936  p10  paragraph  [p10-l2]  whose entries are elements from a complete semiring W = ⟨A, ⊕, ⊗, 0, 1⟩. Then the product of A and B
  937  p10  paragraph  [p10-l3]  is defined as:
  938  p10  paragraph  [p10-l6]  d
  939  p10  paragraph  [p10-l4]  (AB)ij def
  940  p10  paragraph  [p10-l5]  = M
  941  p10  paragraph  [p10-l8]  Aik ⊗ Bkj
  942  p10  paragraph  [p10-l9]  i, j ∈ 1, . . . , d
  943  p10  paragraph  [p10-l10]  (25)
  944  p10  paragraph  [p10-l7]  k=1
  945  p10  paragraph  [p10-l11]  We also define the zero matrix, O, over the complete semiring W = ⟨A, ⊕, ⊗, 0, 1⟩, such that all
  946  p10  paragraph  [p10-l12]  entries are 0, and the unit matrix I as (I)ij = 1 iff i = j and 0 otherwise for all indices i, j ∈ 0, . . . , d.
  947  p10  paragraph  [p10-l13]  It is then straightforward to show that matrix addition is associative and commutative, while matrix
  948  p10  paragraph  [p10-l14]  multiplication is associative and distributive over matrix addition. Hence, the set of square matrices
  949  p10  paragraph  [p10-l15]  of dimension d over a semiring, with addition and multiplication as defined above, is itself a semiring.
  950  p10  paragraph  [p10-l16]  Furthermore, by the element-wise definition of its addition operation, it is also complete.
  951  p10  paragraph  [p10-l17]  B.2
  952  p10  paragraph  [p10-l18]  Semiring-weighted prefix algorithm
  953  p10  paragraph  [p10-l19]  We now consider a semiring-weighted CFG G = ⟨N , Σ, S, R, p, W⟩, where N , Σ, S, R are defined as
  954  p10  paragraph  [p10-l20]  before but the weighting function p: R → W now maps rules to elements of a commutative semiring
  955  p10  paragraph  [p10-l21]  W. 9 Note that we no longer require the rule weights to sum to one. As before, we define the matrix P
  956  p10  paragraph  [p10-l22]  as the square matrix of dimension |N | whose rows and columns are indexed by the non-terminals N in
  957  p10  paragraph  [p10-l23]  some fixed order so that the entry Pij corresponds to the weight of getting the non-terminal Xj on the left
  958  p10  paragraph  [p10-l24]  after one rule application to Xi . Since the rule weights are no longer locally normalized, however, we
  959  p10  paragraph  [p10-l25]  need to include the treesum under the right non-terminal of each rule as an additional term:10
  960  p10  paragraph  [p10-l26]  Pij = p(Xi −→ Xj _) def
  961  p10  paragraph  [p10-l27]  = M
  962  p10  paragraph  [p10-l29]  p(Xi −→ XjY) · treesum(Y)
  963  p10  paragraph  [p10-l30]  (26)
  964  p10  paragraph  [p10-l28]  Y∈N
  965  p10  paragraph  [p10-l31]  We can then calculate the weight of getting Xj from Xi at the leftmost non-terminal after exactly k
  966  p10  paragraph  [p10-l32]  derivation steps as (P k )ij , where P k def
  967  p10  paragraph  [p10-l33]  = P · · · P
  968  p10  paragraph  [p10-l36]  . Finally, to get the left-corner expectations, we then
  969  p10  paragraph  [p10-l34]  | {z }
