  971  p10  figure  [p10-d2]  
  972  p10  paragraph  [p10-l35]  k times
  973  p10  paragraph  [p10-l37]  need to calculate the Kleene closure over the matrix P, 11 that is, we want to find P ∗ = P∞ k=0 P k . To
  974  p10  paragraph  [p10-l38]  compute the Kleene closure over the transition matrix we can use an efficient algorithm by Lehmann
  975  p10  paragraph  [p10-l39]  (1977) which is a generalization of the well-known shortest-path algorithm usually attributed to Floyd
  976  p10  paragraph  [p10-l40]  (1962) and Warshall (1962), but introduced previously by Roy (1959).12 The algorithm works under the
  977  p10  paragraph  [p10-l41]  condition that the Kleene closure of all individual matrix entries from semiring W exists, which is true for
  978  p10  paragraph  [p10-l42]  our case since we assumed W to be complete. The algorithm is shown in Alg. 4.
  979  p10  figure  [p10-d3]  
  980  p10  paragraph  [p10-l43]  Algorithm 4 Lehmann’s algorithm for computing the Kleene closure over a transition matrix
  981  p10  figure  [p10-d4]  
