  1009  p10  paragraph  [p10-l69]  9We require that W be commutative because the order of rule applications does not affect string weight in a weighted CFG.
  1010  p10  paragraph  [p10-l70]  10For locally normalized semirings, the treesum of any non-terminal is 1. In the general case when the weights are not
  1011  p10  paragraph  [p10-l71]  normalized, the treesum of a semiring-weighted WCFG can be computed through fixed point iteration in a similar way to
  1012  p10  paragraph  [p10-l72]  Newton’s method (Esparza et al., 2007).
  1013  p10  paragraph  [p10-l73]  11Note that the Kleene closure exists since matrices with elements from a complete semiring are complete.
  1014  p10  paragraph  [p10-l74]  12The generalization is by way of choosing the appropriate semiring for the given problem. By the same token, Lehmann’s
  1015  p10  paragraph  [p10-l75]  algorithm can also be seen as a generalization of Kleene’s (1956) algorithm for converting finite-state automata to regular
  1016  p10  paragraph  [p10-l76]  expressions and the Gauss–Jordan algorithm for computing matrix inversion (see e.g. Althoen and McLaughlin (1987)).
