  arxiv2024-arsiwalla-qualia-meaning.pdf  language en  blocks 1114
  0  p1  paragraph  [p1-l1]  Qualia and the Formal Structure of Meaning
  1  p1  paragraph  [p1-l2]  Xerxes D. Arsiwalla1,2,∗
  2  p1  paragraph  [p1-l3]  1Pompeu Fabra University, Barcelona, Spain
  3  p1  paragraph  [p1-l4]  2Wolfram Institute, Illinois, USA
  4  p1  paragraph  [p1-l5]  Abstract
  5  p1  paragraph  [p1-l6]  This work explores the hypothesis that subjectively attributed meaning constitutes the
  6  p1  paragraph  [p1-l7]  phenomenal content of conscious experience. That is, phenomenal content is semantic. This
  7  p1  paragraph  [p1-l8]  form of subjective meaning manifests as an intrinsic and non-representational character of
  8  p1  paragraph  [p1-l9]  qualia. Empirically, subjective meaning is ubiquitous in conscious experiences. We point
  9  p1  paragraph  [p1-l10]  to phenomenological studies that lend evidence to support this. Furthermore, this notion
  10  p1  paragraph  [p1-l11]  of meaning closely relates to what Frege refers to as "sense", in metaphysics and philoso-
  11  p1  paragraph  [p1-l12]  phy of language. It also aligns with Peirce’s "interpretant", in semiotics. We discuss how
  12  p1  paragraph  [p1-l13]  Frege’s sense can also be extended to the raw feels of consciousness. Sense and reference
  13  p1  paragraph  [p1-l14]  both play a role in phenomenal experience. Moreover, within the context of the mind-matter
  14  p1  paragraph  [p1-l15]  relation, we provide a formalization of subjective meaning associated to one’s mental repre-
  15  p1  paragraph  [p1-l16]  sentations. Identifying the precise maps between the physical and mental domains, we argue
  16  p1  paragraph  [p1-l17]  that syntactic and semantic structures transcend language, and are realized within each of
  17  p1  paragraph  [p1-l18]  these domains. Formally, meaning is a relational attribute, realized via a map that interprets
  18  p1  paragraph  [p1-l19]  syntactic structures of a formal system within an appropriate semantic space. The image of
  19  p1  paragraph  [p1-l20]  this map within the mental domain is what is relevant for experience, and thus comprises
  20  p1  paragraph  [p1-l21]  the phenomenal content of qualia. We conclude with possible implications this may have for
  21  p1  paragraph  [p1-l22]  experience-based theories of consciousness.
  22  p1  paragraph  [p1-l23]  Keywords: Consciousness Science; Qualia; Mind-Matter Problem; Formal Structures; Lan-
  23  p1  paragraph  [p1-l24]  guage; Meaning; Semiotics.
  24  p1  figure  [p1-d1]  
  25  p1  paragraph  [p1-l25]  ∗x.d.arsiwalla@gmail.com
  26  p1  furniture  [p1-l27]  arXiv:2405.01148v1 [q-bio.NC] 2 May 2024
  27  p1  furniture  [p1-l26]  1
  28  p2  paragraph  [p2-l12]  1
  29  p2  paragraph  [p2-l13]  Introduction
  30  p2  paragraph  [p2-l14]  How should one formulate a mathematical or metaphysical framework that admits a formalization
  31  p2  paragraph  [p2-l15]  of the phenomenal contents of conscious experience? The phenomenal contents or the so-called
  32  p2  paragraph  [p2-l16]  qualia of consciousness are inherently subjective attributes of experience, and by most accounts,
  33  p2  paragraph  [p2-l17]  deemed non-representational [127]. They refer to the raw, immediate and phenomenal aspects
  34  p2  paragraph  [p2-l18]  of perception, sensation or mental states. Phenomenal content, by its very nature, is subjective;
  35  p2  paragraph  [p2-l19]  that is, available only through one’s first-person perspective. Qualia are at the heart of the "what
  36  p2  paragraph  [p2-l20]  it’s like to be" character of conscious experience, as elucidated by Thomas Nagel in 1974 [97]. If
  37  p2  paragraph  [p2-l21]  it were possible to provide a complete third-person description of phenomenal states, that would
  38  p2  paragraph  [p2-l22]  resolve the "what it’s like to be" problem of consciousness. Qualia cannot be fully described, com-
  39  p2  paragraph  [p2-l23]  municated or understood through any objective third-person account or computational routine.
  40  p2  paragraph  [p2-l24]  For instance, attempts to fully convey the subjective experience of tasting chocolate or perceiving
  41  p2  paragraph  [p2-l25]  the color red through objective descriptions or computational algorithms fall short of capturing
  42  p2  paragraph  [p2-l26]  the full qualitative nature of those experiences. These observations collectively indicate that cer-
  43  p2  paragraph  [p2-l27]  tain aspects of conscious experiences pose challenges for all attempts that seek to fully explain
  44  p2  paragraph  [p2-l28]  or replicate qualia through either exclusively computational, or objective descriptions, that is,
  45  p2  paragraph  [p2-l29]  potential third-person descriptions. Besides potential implications for philosophy of mind, this
  46  p2  paragraph  [p2-l30]  problem also suggests new frontiers for the philosophy of science itself; namely, how does one ex-
  47  p2  paragraph  [p2-l31]  tend the existing scientific paradigm based on third-person perspective to account for phenomena
  48  p2  paragraph  [p2-l32]  that are subjective and non-representational - that is, amenable only via first-person perspective?
  49  p2  paragraph  [p2-l33]  For these very reasons, a scientific description or theory elucidating the nature of qualia,
  50  p2  paragraph  [p2-l34]  or one explaining the phenomenology of conscious experiences continues to be an extremely chal-
  51  p2  paragraph  [p2-l35]  lenging problem at the heart of modern science. Attempts at any such theoretical description (of
  52  p2  paragraph  [p2-l36]  qualia) call for at least two essential ingredients:
  53  p2  paragraph  [p2-l37]  (i) A whole new scientific paradigm of subjectivity, one which describes phenomena from the
  54  p2  paragraph  [p2-l38]  intrinsic point-of-view, or "the view from the inside".
  55  p2  furniture  [p2-l1]  Contents
  56  p2  furniture  [p2-l2]  1 Introduction
  57  p2  furniture  [p2-l3]  2
  58  p2  furniture  [p2-l4]  2 Subjective Meaning in Phenomenology of Experience
  59  p2  furniture  [p2-l5]  4
  60  p2  furniture  [p2-l6]  3 Do Raw Feels Have Subjective Meaning?
  61  p2  furniture  [p2-l7]  9
  62  p2  furniture  [p2-l8]  4 The Formal Structure of Meaning
  63  p2  furniture  [p2-l9]  11
  64  p2  furniture  [p2-l10]  5 Discussion
  65  p2  furniture  [p2-l11]  18
  66  p2  furniture  [p2-l39]  2
  67  p3  paragraph  [p3-l1]  (ii) A metaphysical (and possibly mathematical) formalization of non-representational entities
  68  p3  paragraph  [p3-l2]  (potentially in the form of abstract objects and their relations).
  69  p3  paragraph  [p3-l3]  Currently, in the absence of a complete description of (or difficulty conceptualizing) the
  70  p3  paragraph  [p3-l4]  above two ingredients, one often relies on constructs using information, computation or causality
  71  p3  paragraph  [p3-l5]  [43, 99, 124]. These are certainly useful to quantify computational processes or identify states of
  72  p3  paragraph  [p3-l6]  complexity, but that is still rather far from capturing the very phenomenal character of qualia1 .
  73  p3  paragraph  [p3-l7]  Possibly, a metaphysical formalization of phenomenal properties is what is needed. Even though
  74  p3  paragraph  [p3-l8]  a satisfactory solution to this problem does not exist yet, here we put forth a proposal that
  75  p3  paragraph  [p3-l9]  identifies subjectively attributed meaning with the phenomenal content of conscious experience.
  76  p3  paragraph  [p3-l10]  We then discuss how subjective meaning of this kind can be formalized within the setting of the
  77  p3  paragraph  [p3-l11]  mind-matter relation.
  78  p3  paragraph  [p3-l12]  More broadly, within the cognitive sciences (inclusive of approaches focussing on cognition,
  79  p3  paragraph  [p3-l13]  consciousness, computation, and clinical practice), meaning has been discussed in one of two
  80  p3  paragraph  [p3-l14]  ways: either as a by-product of explicit neuronal or computational mechanisms [45], or as an
  81  p3  paragraph  [p3-l15]  intentionality relation between mental representations and what they represent [24, 89]. These
  82  p3  paragraph  [p3-l16]  two approaches differ in metaphysical ontology. The former assumes a purely physicalistic stance,
  83  p3  paragraph  [p3-l17]  whereas the latter presupposes a fundamental relationality between the physical and the mental.
  84  p3  paragraph  [p3-l18]  For instance, in the former scenario, the meaning associated to one’s experience is believed
  85  p3  paragraph  [p3-l19]  to arise from various cognitive processes and associations thereof [45, 58, 71]. Such cognitive
  86  p3  paragraph  [p3-l20]  processes may then serve as accessories to conscious experience. In such a view, when an individual
  87  p3  paragraph  [p3-l21]  experiences a scene, it triggers associations, memories, emotions and cognitive mechanisms that
  88  p3  paragraph  [p3-l22]  provide cognitive significance to that particular experience. For instance, the color red might
  89  p3  paragraph  [p3-l23]  evoke emotions, cultural associations or memories linked to the color. Here, meaning is not
  90  p3  paragraph  [p3-l24]  considered an inherent feature of qualia, but something that is constructed and attributed by
  91  p3  paragraph  [p3-l25]  an individual based upon their cognitive deliberations and psychological states. The significance
  92  p3  paragraph  [p3-l26]  and interpretation of any experience, is then shaped by one’s perceptions, memories, beliefs and
  93  p3  paragraph  [p3-l27]  also cultural influences. In other words, meaning construction is discussed through the lens of
  94  p3  paragraph  [p3-l28]  cognitive (and metacognitive) mechanistic explanations [45]; that is, as an interpretative layer
  95  p3  paragraph  [p3-l29]  imposed on top of experience, rather than an inherent feature of raw subjective experience itself.
  96  p3  paragraph  [p3-l30]  In contrast to meaning as a cognitive by-product, the second approach mentioned above,
  97  p3  paragraph  [p3-l31]  decrees a more prominent role for meaning with regard to consciousness. Here, meaning is dis-
  98  p3  paragraph  [p3-l32]  cussed in the sense of Brentano’s notion of "intentionality" [33]. In the present work, our discus-
  99  p3  paragraph  [p3-l33]  sions align more with the stance that meaning is indeed fundamental to consciousness. Further-
  100  p3  paragraph  [p3-l34]  more, we propose that subjective meaning constitutes the phenomenal content of all conscious
  101  p3  paragraph  [p3-l35]  experience. Compiling evidence from phenomenological studies, we demonstrate that this form
  102  p3  paragraph  [p3-l36]  of meaning is ubiquitous to the qualia of consciousness. As mentioned, it is also important to
  103  p3  paragraph  [p3-l37]  formalize the mathematical or metaphysical structure of this kind of meaning, if one is to make
  104  p3  paragraph  [p3-l38]  progress on the problem of qualia. Fortunately, seminal works in the philosophy of mind and
  105  p3  paragraph  [p3-l39]  language [52] (see also [48, 135]), as well as mathematical advances in natural language seman-
  106  p3  figure  [p3-d1]  
  107  p3  paragraph  [p3-l40]  1Roger Penrose has been a vociferous proponent of the view that consciousness necessarily involves a funda-
  108  p3  paragraph  [p3-l41]  mentally non-computational process [105] (see also [15] for a recent perspective on this point). An early remnant
  109  p3  paragraph  [p3-l42]  of this view, though not explicitly expressed in terms of consciousness, may be traced back to Alan Turing’s PhD
  110  p3  paragraph  [p3-l43]  thesis, where he contrasts between intuition and ingenuity [126].
  111  p3  furniture  [p3-l44]  3
  112  p4  paragraph  [p4-l1]  tics [40] equip us with the right conceptual groundwork for formalizing subjective meaning with
  113  p4  paragraph  [p4-l2]  regard to the problem of consciousness.
  114  p4  paragraph  [p4-l3]  The outline of this paper is as follows: in section 2, we provide phenomenological evidence
  115  p4  paragraph  [p4-l4]  to support the claim that meaning is omnipresent in qualia; in section 3, we discuss how Frege’s
  116  p4  paragraph  [p4-l5]  notion of sense extends to the raw feels of conscious experience; in section 4, we formalize sub-
  117  p4  paragraph  [p4-l6]  jective meaning of mental representations in the context of the mind-matter relation; lastly, we
  118  p4  paragraph  [p4-l7]  conclude in section 5 with final thoughts.
  119  p4  paragraph  [p4-l9]  Subjective Meaning in Phenomenology of Experience
  120  p4  paragraph  [p4-l8]  2
  121  p4  figure  [p4-d1..p4-d4 (4)]  
  122  p4  paragraph  [p4-l10]  Proposition 2.1. We hypothesize a notion of subjectively attributed meaning or subjective mean-
  123  p4  paragraph  [p4-l11]  ing as an inherent feature of the qualia of consciousness. This subjective meaning constitutes the
  124  p4  paragraph  [p4-l12]  phenomenal content of all conscious experiences.
  125  p4  paragraph  [p4-l13]  Meaning is not merely about language2 , nor is it merely a construct about the world "as
  126  p4  paragraph  [p4-l14]  is". The notion of meaning has been extensively discussed by several philosophers in the context of
  127  p4  paragraph  [p4-l15]  language, mind, metaphysics and epistemology [52,63,68,74]. How cognitive and artificial agents
  128  p4  paragraph  [p4-l16]  learn to make associations about things in the world, is deeply relevant to our understanding of
  129  p4  paragraph  [p4-l17]  biological and artificial intelligence.
  130  p4  paragraph  [p4-l18]  Discussions about meaning-making or meaning-generation broadly involve some kind of
  131  p4  paragraph  [p4-l19]  attribution of either significance, interpretation or semantic content to an agent’s actions, per-
  132  p4  paragraph  [p4-l20]  ceptions and thoughts [45,58,71]. Some of these attributions may be representable using compu-
  133  p4  paragraph  [p4-l21]  tational principles, and hence be realized as specific cognitive functions. Alternatively, there may
  134  p4  paragraph  [p4-l22]  also exist attributions that are non-representational. These, one might refer to as phenomenal,
  135  p4  paragraph  [p4-l23]  and hence intrinsic to the qualia of consciousness.
  136  p4  paragraph  [p4-l24]  Note that it is this latter class, of non-representational significance attributions, that we will refer
  137  p4  paragraph  [p4-l25]  to as subjectively attributed meaning or subjective meaning.
  138  p4  paragraph  [p4-l26]  Let us briefly compare the above two kinds of significance attributions. Computationally repre-
  139  p4  paragraph  [p4-l27]  sentable attributions are the ones that are particularly well-aligned with an embodied cognition
  140  p4  paragraph  [p4-l28]  perspective, where an agent has to learn the affordances of its environment for planning its ac-
  141  p4  paragraph  [p4-l29]  tions and directing itself towards its goals [95, 118]. By some accounts, this process also requires
  142  p4  paragraph  [p4-l30]  the agent to actively infer or at least conceptualize its own actions and perceptions, as well as
  143  p4  paragraph  [p4-l31]  that of other agents in the world [57, 87]. Meaning (in this context of cognitive functions) per-
  144  p4  paragraph  [p4-l32]  tains to the knowledge or significance that an experience acquires via mechanistic controls or
  145  p4  paragraph  [p4-l33]  computational processes, over and above its raw phenomenal content. In that sense, this is a
  146  p4  paragraph  [p4-l34]  deliberative meaning-making process. For example, meaning associated to perceptual processes
  147  p4  figure  [p4-d5]  
  148  p4  paragraph  [p4-l35]  2 It was David Mumford who first pointed out that "Grammar isn’t merely part of language" [96].
  149  p4  furniture  [p4-l36]  4
  150  p5  paragraph  [p5-l1]  involve the interpretation and understanding (often deliberative) of sensory stimuli, such as recog-
  151  p5  paragraph  [p5-l2]  nizing various representations of shapes, colors and patterns, or perceiving objects in the external
  152  p5  paragraph  [p5-l3]  world. In language and cognitive semiotics, meaning involves the understanding of language-
  153  p5  paragraph  [p5-l4]  based representations, concepts and symbols, when cognitive agents engage in understanding and
  154  p5  paragraph  [p5-l5]  communicating through language, signs and gestures [140]. Similarly, cognitive states involving
  155  p5  paragraph  [p5-l6]  beliefs, biases or intentions, involve some kind of value attribution by the individual (based on
  156  p5  paragraph  [p5-l7]  psychological conditioning) [73, 119].
  157  p5  paragraph  [p5-l8]  On the other hand, non-representational significance attributions are of the type that are
  158  p5  paragraph  [p5-l9]  immediate (as opposed to deliberative) and of a phenomenal character. They distinctly mean
  159  p5  paragraph  [p5-l10]  something to the experiencing subject. Examples include moments of intuitive insights, profound
  160  p5  paragraph  [p5-l11]  personal revelations, states of transcendence [28, 86, 113] (and more such examples discussed in
  161  p5  paragraph  [p5-l12]  Table 1 below). While the phenomenology of these experiences can certainly be investigated qual-
  162  p5  paragraph  [p5-l13]  itatively using introspective methods, the full scope of the meaning experienced in all of these
  163  p5  paragraph  [p5-l14]  instances is only available through first-person perspective.
  164  p5  paragraph  [p5-l15]  For these reasons, such subjectively attributed meaning is an observer-dependent construct: it lit-
  165  p5  paragraph  [p5-l16]  erally holds in the mind of the beholder!
  166  p5  paragraph  [p5-l17]  Meaning of this form is a genuinely subjective quality3 . In the discussion that follows, we will
  167  p5  paragraph  [p5-l18]  elaborate how this form of meaning might be directly tied to the qualia of consciousness, and
  168  p5  paragraph  [p5-l19]  how it may in fact manifest as the phenomenal content of all experience. We also point to
  169  p5  paragraph  [p5-l20]  phenomenological evidence in favor of this view.
  170  p5  paragraph  [p5-l21]  Remark 2.1. Earlier, we mentioned about meaning as discussed in the sense of Brentano’s notion
  171  p5  paragraph  [p5-l22]  of "intentionality" [33]. There, meaning is conceived as a relation between the physical and the
  172  p5  paragraph  [p5-l23]  mental (see also [23, 24]). Later, in section 4, we will indeed formalize such a relation between
  173  p5  paragraph  [p5-l24]  the physical and mental domains. How does this relational notion of meaning corroborate with
  174  p5  paragraph  [p5-l25]  the idea of subjective meaning being proposed here?
  175  p5  paragraph  [p5-l26]  Notice that, meaning, when conceptualized as a relation between the physical and the men-
  176  p5  paragraph  [p5-l27]  tal, is necessarily an extrinsically defined notion. Namely, extrinsic to any physical and mental
  177  p5  paragraph  [p5-l28]  observer. On the other hand, one may ask, from the intrinsic point of view of an observer within
  178  p5  paragraph  [p5-l29]  the mental domain, how does this physical to mental relation manifest? In other words, what
  179  p5  paragraph  [p5-l30]  does the mind experience of this relation? If one thinks of this relation as a formal map from a
  180  p5  paragraph  [p5-l31]  domain (physical) to a codomain (mental), then the answer is: the image of this map within the
  181  p5  paragraph  [p5-l32]  codomain. Subjective meaning is thus the mental image of Brentano’s intentionality. This is the
  182  p5  paragraph  [p5-l33]  facet of meaning that a conscious agent can experience with their mind. For this reason, we could
  183  p5  paragraph  [p5-l34]  have also referred to this notion of meaning as intrinsic meaning. The full relational map is only
  184  p5  paragraph  [p5-l35]  accessible to a metaphysical observer. Working from such an intrinsic point of view is of direct
  185  p5  paragraph  [p5-l36]  relevance to any experience-based theory of consciousness.
  186  p5  paragraph  [p5-l37]  Remark 2.2. Let us make a brief comment about qualia in more general modalities. Even though
  187  p5  paragraph  [p5-l38]  a sizable fraction of works discussing qualia often anchor around sensory experiences ("the redness
  188  p5  figure  [p5-d1]  
  189  p5  paragraph  [p5-l39]  3As such, the "objective" use of meaning in language and social communication is only a matter of standard-
  190  p5  paragraph  [p5-l40]  ization of what symbols may refer to, or formation of social norms and conventions within and between groups of
  191  p5  paragraph  [p5-l41]  agents [3, 55, 79, 110].
  192  p5  furniture  [p5-l42]  5
  193  p6  paragraph  [p6-l1]  of red", for instance), it is to be noted that qualia also refer to aspects of conscious experiences
  194  p6  paragraph  [p6-l2]  beyond the purely sensory [37, 88, 127]. Examples include; (a) qualia associated to subjective
  195  p6  paragraph  [p6-l3]  feelings and emotions, like the feeling of joy, sadness, or fear, which might not be directly tied
  196  p6  paragraph  [p6-l4]  (though indirectly related) to sensory input; (b) qualia associated to thought processes, such as
  197  p6  paragraph  [p6-l5]  the immediate awareness of having a thought, a mental image or an idea; (c) qualia associated to
  198  p6  paragraph  [p6-l6]  introspective states, such as the subjective sense of self-awareness, reflective thought, or experience
  199  p6  paragraph  [p6-l7]  of one’s own mental states; to name a few (see [37, 88, 127] and references therein). The above
  200  p6  paragraph  [p6-l8]  and other examples of qualia not exclusively tied to sensory perceptions will be relevant for us
  201  p6  paragraph  [p6-l9]  later.
  202  p6  paragraph  [p6-l10]  Empirically, how could subjective meaning manifest as the phenomenal content of qualia
  203  p6  paragraph  [p6-l11]  (in the broader sense of qualia mentioned in the remark above)? For instance, consider the case
  204  p6  paragraph  [p6-l12]  of qualia associated to emotions: one may ask whether emotional feelings might come endowed
  205  p6  paragraph  [p6-l13]  with some form of subjective meaning that is immediate to the experience? Now, emotions
  206  p6  paragraph  [p6-l14]  and feelings as intricate mental states certainly do carry a personal form of significance for the
  207  p6  paragraph  [p6-l15]  experiencing subject. Furthermore, this form of significance is highly subjective, contextual to
  208  p6  paragraph  [p6-l16]  the individual’s history, and intimately tied to their internal states. But significance of this kind
  209  p6  paragraph  [p6-l17]  is in fact a semantic quality, in that, it means something to the subject (in the next section
  210  p6  paragraph  [p6-l18]  we compare this notion of subjective meaning to Frege’s notion of sense). It is this aspect of
  211  p6  paragraph  [p6-l19]  meaning that we claim is raw and immediate to the emotion (as opposed to a form of deliberative
  212  p6  paragraph  [p6-l20]  interpretation of the emotion). Now, discussions about phenomenal properties usually refer to the
  213  p6  paragraph  [p6-l21]  immediate, intrinsic and non-representational qualities of conscious experiences that constitute
  214  p6  paragraph  [p6-l22]  the "what it is like" aspect of those experiences [97]. The form of meaning that we are referring
  215  p6  paragraph  [p6-l23]  to above, is intrinsically subjective (involving personal significance and value attribution) and
  216  p6  paragraph  [p6-l24]  non-representational in the same way that sensory qualia are. One may report one’s emotional
  217  p6  paragraph  [p6-l25]  experiences categorically, but there is no way to convey an objectively third-person perspective as
  218  p6  paragraph  [p6-l26]  to what that emotion means to the experiencing subject. This meaning or significance is closely
  219  p6  paragraph  [p6-l27]  associated to the raw feel of the emotion itself.
  220  p6  paragraph  [p6-l28]  First, let us discuss why meaning of the above kind may be closely entwined with the
  221  p6  paragraph  [p6-l29]  subject’s own self-states.
  222  p6  paragraph  [p6-l30]  Remark 2.3. Let us note that issues related to personal significance or personal value attribution
  223  p6  paragraph  [p6-l31]  within the realm of a subject’s experiences are features closely associated to their self-model.
  224  p6  paragraph  [p6-l32]  Therefore:
  225  p6  figure  [p6-d1..p6-d4 (4)]  
  226  p6  paragraph  [p6-l33]  Proposition 2.2. We posit that subjective meaning is phenomenologically relevant to all qualia
  227  p6  paragraph  [p6-l34]  associated with the subject’s self-states, or at least those involving any relation between self-states
  228  p6  paragraph  [p6-l35]  to other states within the subject’s experiential realm.
  229  p6  paragraph  [p6-l36]  From a systems theory perspective, a self-model is a construct that encompasses an in-
  230  p6  paragraph  [p6-l37]  dividual’s interpretations, value attributions and representations concerning their own identity,
  231  p6  paragraph  [p6-l38]  internal states, belief systems, emotional states and behaviors [89, 91]. This internal model con-
  232  p6  paragraph  [p6-l39]  tributes to the recognition of one’s own goals and value systems, and consequently, it influences
  233  p6  furniture  [p6-l40]  6
  234  p7  paragraph  [p7-l1]  all external models of the subject (such as those pertaining to the world, or models of other
  235  p7  paragraph  [p7-l2]  agents that the subject interacts with). In the more specific context of consciousness studies, the
  236  p7  paragraph  [p7-l3]  relevant kind of self-models are what are referred to as "phenomenal self-models". These models
  237  p7  paragraph  [p7-l4]  were proposed by Thomas Metzinger in 2004 [89]. They are internal dynamical representations
  238  p7  paragraph  [p7-l5]  of the agent, and carry phenomenal content. A feature of these self-models is that they not only
  239  p7  paragraph  [p7-l6]  constitute a representation of the agent, but they also co-represent the representational relation
  240  p7  paragraph  [p7-l7]  (between the agent and the world) itself [89, 91]. In other words, they are equipped with a phe-
  241  p7  paragraph  [p7-l8]  nomenal model of the intentionality relation4 . According to Metzinger, phenomenal self-models
  242  p7  paragraph  [p7-l9]  are characterized by phenomenal properties pertaining to ownership, perspectivalness and self-
  243  p7  paragraph  [p7-l10]  hood; all of which are essential for creating a first-person perspective [89, 91]. Presumably, these
  244  p7  paragraph  [p7-l11]  phenomenal properties realize what is referred to as sense of ownership, sense of situatedness
  245  p7  paragraph  [p7-l12]  and sense of self respectively. The "sense" in the above terms is indeed a form of subjective
  246  p7  paragraph  [p7-l13]  significance or knowingness, and hence a form of meaning (the link between sense and meaning is
  247  p7  paragraph  [p7-l14]  further discussed in the next section). For that reason, an experience of self, necessarily features
  248  p7  paragraph  [p7-l15]  subjective meaning of the kind we have noted above.
  249  p7  paragraph  [p7-l16]  Furthermore, one may extrapolate that the above stated properties of a phenomenal self-
  250  p7  paragraph  [p7-l17]  model would also ascribe subjective significance to the very manner in which an agent interacts
  251  p7  paragraph  [p7-l18]  with and perceives the world. In other words, the subjective meaning attached to (or in part,
  252  p7  paragraph  [p7-l19]  defining) one’s self attributes, will influence the way a subject experiences the world from their
  253  p7  paragraph  [p7-l20]  first-person perspective. Their meaning-attributes of self affect their meaning-attributes of the
  254  p7  paragraph  [p7-l21]  world (and vice-versa, owing to feedback from the environment).
  255  p7  paragraph  [p7-l22]  Let us now discuss literature examples which demonstrate the above. What we are sug-
  256  p7  paragraph  [p7-l23]  gesting here that subjective meaning, as the phenomenal content of experience, may well be
  257  p7  paragraph  [p7-l24]  ubiquitous to consciousness. Table 1 summarizes various examples of conscious experiences, typ-
  258  p7  paragraph  [p7-l25]  ically those besides the purely sensory ones, which explicitly evoke a personal form of meaning
  259  p7  paragraph  [p7-l26]  for the experiencing subject. The text thereafter, further elaborates on these.
  260  p7  figure  [p7-d1..p7-d61 (61)]  
  261  p7  paragraph  [p7-l27]  1.
  262  p7  paragraph  [p7-l28]  Spiritual realization and personal revelations
  263  p7  paragraph  [p7-l29]  2.
  264  p7  paragraph  [p7-l30]  Eureka moments and intuitive insights
  265  p7  paragraph  [p7-l31]  3.
  266  p7  paragraph  [p7-l32]  Experiences of aesthetic appreciation
  267  p7  paragraph  [p7-l33]  4.
  268  p7  paragraph  [p7-l34]  Feelings of love and empathy
  269  p7  paragraph  [p7-l35]  5.
  270  p7  paragraph  [p7-l36]  States of enlightenment and transcendence
  271  p7  paragraph  [p7-l37]  6.
  272  p7  paragraph  [p7-l38]  Altered states of consciousness
  273  p7  paragraph  [p7-l39]  7.
  274  p7  paragraph  [p7-l40]  Feelings of synchronicity
  275  p7  paragraph  [p7-l41]  8.
  276  p7  paragraph  [p7-l42]  Out-of-body experiences
  277  p7  paragraph  [p7-l43]  9.
  278  p7  paragraph  [p7-l44]  Near-death experiences
  279  p7  paragraph  [p7-l45]  10.
  280  p7  paragraph  [p7-l46]  States of flow
  281  p7  paragraph  [p7-l47]  Table 1: Examples of conscious experiences that constitute subjective meaning.
  282  p7  figure  [p7-d62]  
  283  p7  paragraph  [p7-l48]  4According to Metzinger, a system of "third-order embodiment" possess phenomenal self-models [91]. Such a
  284  p7  paragraph  [p7-l49]  system explicitly models itself as an embodied being, and maps some of the representational content generated in
  285  p7  paragraph  [p7-l50]  this process directly onto conscious experience. In other words, it consciously experiences itself as embodied.
  286  p7  furniture  [p7-l51]  7
  287  p8  paragraph  [p8-l1]  1. Moments of personal revelation, existential realization or sudden transformative life experi-
  288  p8  paragraph  [p8-l2]  ences like a spiritual realization, a meditative revelation or an epiphany have been discussed
  289  p8  paragraph  [p8-l3]  in [26, 61, 86, 93, 128]. These experiences have been reported to involve a deep and immedi-
  290  p8  paragraph  [p8-l4]  ate personal significance to the experiencing subject. For that reason, the aforementioned
  291  p8  paragraph  [p8-l5]  studies investigating these experiences lean towards consciousness, rather than seeking ex-
  292  p8  paragraph  [p8-l6]  planations based on purely cognitive control or processing.
  293  p8  paragraph  [p8-l7]  2. Sudden realizations such as "eureka" moments, intuitive insights, instinctive or gut feel-
  294  p8  paragraph  [p8-l8]  ings, that provide an immediate sense or perceived understanding to complex problems also
  295  p8  paragraph  [p8-l9]  possess subjective meaning within conscious experiences (see [113,131]). Likewise, for intro-
  296  p8  paragraph  [p8-l10]  spective awareness; that is, the immediate awareness of one’s own mental states, thoughts
  297  p8  paragraph  [p8-l11]  or introspective processes [129].
  298  p8  paragraph  [p8-l12]  3. Profound experiences of artistic or aesthetic appreciation, such as being deeply moved by
  299  p8  paragraph  [p8-l13]  a piece of music, art or even nature. Feelings of awe, wonder or personal connection when
  300  p8  paragraph  [p8-l14]  experiencing aesthetic perceptions. All of these constitute examples of conscious experiences
  301  p8  paragraph  [p8-l15]  that invoke an immediate kind of meaning in the form of personal significance [39, 49, 111].
  302  p8  paragraph  [p8-l16]  4. Intensely felt connections in personal relationships, profound moments of empathy or ex-
  303  p8  paragraph  [p8-l17]  periences of deep understanding and resonance with others, including feelings of love, are
  304  p8  paragraph  [p8-l18]  examples of experiences deeply tied to a purely subjective notion of meaning [35, 122, 138].
  305  p8  paragraph  [p8-l19]  5. Moments of transcendence (either through meditation, contemplation or religious practices),
  306  p8  paragraph  [p8-l20]  perceived states of enlightenment, metaphysical awareness, spiritual awakening, mystical
  307  p8  paragraph  [p8-l21]  encounters eliciting immediate, ineffable feelings of unity and connectedness either with a
  308  p8  paragraph  [p8-l22]  higher state of being or a divine presence [28,51,64,85,120]. These too, hold deep personal
  309  p8  paragraph  [p8-l23]  significance for the subject.
  310  p8  paragraph  [p8-l24]  6. Experiences involving altered states of consciousness, induced by meditation, trance or
  311  p8  paragraph  [p8-l25]  altered perceptions; such as those during lucid dreaming, mindfulness, heightened self-
  312  p8  paragraph  [p8-l26]  awareness, or those induced by hallucinogens [29, 94, 123].
  313  p8  paragraph  [p8-l27]  7. Following Carl Jung, there are several literature studies involving instances of synchronicity
  314  p8  paragraph  [p8-l28]  or meaningful coincidences that evoke immediate, ineffable feelings of interconnectedness
  315  p8  paragraph  [p8-l29]  for the experiencing subject [41, 46, 72, 101].
  316  p8  paragraph  [p8-l30]  8. Instances of transpersonal experiences, such as out-of-body experiences, which involve a
  317  p8  paragraph  [p8-l31]  sensation of being detached from one’s physical body and perceiving the world from a
  318  p8  paragraph  [p8-l32]  location outside the physical body [31, 32, 90]. These experiences are intimately concerned
  319  p8  paragraph  [p8-l33]  with sense of self.
  320  p8  paragraph  [p8-l34]  9. Also, near-death experiences such as experiences of vivid and panoramic life reviews, where
  321  p8  paragraph  [p8-l35]  subjects relive or review significant events or memories from their lives in a comprehensive
  322  p8  paragraph  [p8-l36]  manner (these are sometimes reported with feelings of peace or transcendence while seeming
  323  p8  paragraph  [p8-l37]  to enter into a different realm during the experience) [30, 31, 130]
  324  p8  paragraph  [p8-l38]  10. Lastly, experiences of being in a state of flow, where immediate, ineffable insights and
  325  p8  paragraph  [p8-l39]  intense focus arise during engaging activities like creating art, playing music or solving
  326  p8  paragraph  [p8-l40]  complex problems [70, 98, 133].
  327  p8  furniture  [p8-l41]  8
  328  p9  paragraph  [p9-l1]  The above are all examples of conscious experiences (although not of a typically sensory
  329  p9  paragraph  [p9-l2]  type) that constitute some form of personal meaning for the experiencing subject. We claim
  330  p9  paragraph  [p9-l3]  that the phenomenal content of these experiences is of a semantic type, that is, it is subjective
  331  p9  paragraph  [p9-l4]  meaning.
  332  p9  paragraph  [p9-l6]  Do Raw Feels Have Subjective Meaning?
  333  p9  paragraph  [p9-l5]  3
  334  p9  paragraph  [p9-l7]  The experiential phenomena enumerated in the section above, while predominantly of a non-
  335  p9  paragraph  [p9-l8]  sensory nature, have nonetheless been extensively studied in the consciousness literature (see
  336  p9  paragraph  [p9-l9]  references cited above). The important point of the above discussion was that a personal notion
  337  p9  paragraph  [p9-l10]  of meaning is ubiquitous to a wide variety of conscious experiences (particularly, those referring
  338  p9  paragraph  [p9-l11]  to non-sensory modalities). What about the raw feels of consciousness? Are they associated to
  339  p9  paragraph  [p9-l12]  meaning?
  340  p9  paragraph  [p9-l13]  Expositions concerning sensory qualia are often discussed in terms of raw feels [127]. In
  341  p9  paragraph  [p9-l14]  order to make the case that raw feels too, cannot be devoid of meaning that is intrinsic to the
  342  p9  paragraph  [p9-l15]  experiencing subject, let us consider a bridging concept between feels and meaning: namely, that
  343  p9  paragraph  [p9-l16]  of "sense". In philosophy of mind, the term sense has multiple connotations: it may refer to a
  344  p9  paragraph  [p9-l17]  subjective perception, an understanding or significance of something, or a form of knowledge [25,
  345  p9  paragraph  [p9-l18]  27,52]. Sense, perfectly well, applies also to experiences beyond those involving the purely sensory
  346  p9  paragraph  [p9-l19]  modalities (for instance, those involving internal states). Here we will anchor our discussion
  347  p9  paragraph  [p9-l20]  mainly on Frege’s use of the notion of sense [52].
  348  p9  paragraph  [p9-l21]  Remark 3.1. In his seminal 1892 paper, "On Sense and Reference", Gottlob Frege developed
  349  p9  paragraph  [p9-l22]  his ideas on sense and reference [52]. This laid the groundwork for the metaphysics of thought,
  350  p9  paragraph  [p9-l23]  theories of meaning in philosophy of mind, as well as discussions on semantics and language 5 .
  351  p9  paragraph  [p9-l24]  Frege elaborates on the distinction between sense and reference, and suggests that both, sense and
  352  p9  paragraph  [p9-l25]  reference contribute to meaning (with respect to both, expressions as well as thoughts).
  353  p9  paragraph  [p9-l26]  According to Frege, reference6 is concerned with naming objects or entities. It refers to, or
  354  p9  paragraph  [p9-l27]  is about, something. Sense, on the other hand, characterizes the mode in which something presents
  355  p9  paragraph  [p9-l28]  itself. In terms of expressions, this mode accounts for what the expression signifies (to the subject).
  356  p9  paragraph  [p9-l29]  It is the way by which one understands the expression via internal conceptual relations. Given the
  357  p9  paragraph  [p9-l30]  reference of words (as names that refer to something), the reference of a sentence as a whole can
  358  p9  paragraph  [p9-l31]  be described in terms of the references of the individual words and the way in which those words are
  359  p9  paragraph  [p9-l32]  arranged in the sentence. Based on this Frege identifies the reference of a sentence as a truth value
  360  p9  paragraph  [p9-l33]  (see also [48,135] for an interpretation of Frege to the philosophy of language). Correspondingly,
  361  p9  paragraph  [p9-l34]  the sense of a sentence is a "thought" ( [53] discusses the metaphysics of thought, as well as
  362  p9  paragraph  [p9-l35]  its linguistic realization). While the reference of a sentence can assume one of two truth values,
  363  p9  paragraph  [p9-l36]  the sense of a sentence can be acquired from a potentially infinite possibility of thoughts. Thus,
  364  p9  paragraph  [p9-l37]  sense and reference are both, compositional and hierarchical, existing at each level of the structure
  365  p9  figure  [p9-d1]  
  366  p9  paragraph  [p9-l38]  5Following Frege’s seminal essay, other works that have extensively addressed the relation between language,
  367  p9  paragraph  [p9-l39]  meaning and mind include those of Bertrand Russell, Ludwig Wittgenstein, Hilary Putnam and Saul Kripke, to
  368  p9  paragraph  [p9-l40]  name a few [75, 81, 109, 114].
  369  p9  paragraph  [p9-l41]  6Some authors also use the term "denotation" for "reference" [135].
  370  p9  furniture  [p9-l42]  9
  371  p10  paragraph  [p10-l1]  of the expression. Two syntactically distinct expressions, may have identical references (at each
  372  p10  paragraph  [p10-l2]  corresponding level of their structures), but distinct senses.
  373  p10  paragraph  [p10-l3]  The relevant question then is: Do raw feels have a subjective "sense" for that what is
  374  p10  paragraph  [p10-l4]  being experienced? Is this a form of meaning, after all? Let us now answer these questions. In
  375  p10  paragraph  [p10-l5]  doing so, we will make use of the following proposition:
  376  p10  figure  [p10-d1..p10-d4 (4)]  
  377  p10  paragraph  [p10-l6]  Proposition 3.1. Mental representations are to the mind, what symbolic expressions are to lan-
  378  p10  paragraph  [p10-l7]  guage.
  379  p10  paragraph  [p10-l8]  Building up on remark 3.1 above, let us analyze Frege’s notion of sense in the context of
  380  p10  paragraph  [p10-l9]  mind. In Frege’s view, the "sense of an expression" is a semantic attribute based on personal
  381  p10  paragraph  [p10-l10]  significance to the subject. This sense conveys a meaning of the expression to the subject. In
  382  p10  paragraph  [p10-l11]  this context, Frege even described thought as a semantic construct. In order to systematically
  383  p10  paragraph  [p10-l12]  lift this notion from the philosophy of language to the philosophy of mind; first, notice that just
  384  p10  paragraph  [p10-l13]  as an expression is a syntactic structure based on rules of composition and an architectural hi-
  385  p10  paragraph  [p10-l14]  erarchy; so is a mental representation of objects in the world (or of internal states), a syntactic
  386  p10  paragraph  [p10-l15]  construct based on rules of composition and structural hierarchy7 (in the following section, we
  387  p10  paragraph  [p10-l16]  elaborate further on how grammar extends beyond spoken language; see also [96, 139]). Hence,
  388  p10  paragraph  [p10-l17]  our proposition above: that mental representations are to the mind, what symbolic expressions
  389  p10  paragraph  [p10-l18]  are to language. By themselves, they are both syntactic constructs based on admissible structural
  390  p10  paragraph  [p10-l19]  compositions of linguistic and mental primitives respectively. Extrapolating Frege’s philosophy
  391  p10  paragraph  [p10-l20]  to all syntactic constructs, mental representations also have a reference and a sense. Their ref-
  392  p10  paragraph  [p10-l21]  erence is literally what they are about, or what they represent; whereas, the sense of a mental
  393  p10  paragraph  [p10-l22]  representation is simply its significance to the subject. The latter is thus a semantic attribute of
  394  p10  paragraph  [p10-l23]  the representation. Mental representations thus have a structural (syntactic) aspect, as well as a
  395  p10  paragraph  [p10-l24]  semantic aspect. In the phenomenology of consciousness, when one speaks about awareness, or
  396  p10  paragraph  [p10-l25]  being aware about "something", it is certainly not the "thing" itself8 that the subject is, or even
  397  p10  paragraph  [p10-l26]  possibly could be aware of9 . Furthermore, the fact that qualia are transparent, implies that one
  398  p10  paragraph  [p10-l27]  does not experience their mental representations as representations either. Rather, it is only cer-
  399  p10  paragraph  [p10-l28]  tain attributes of one’s mental representation (of the "thing" being "observed") that the subject
  400  p10  paragraph  [p10-l29]  gains awareness of. And, a prominent attribute of a representation is its subjective meaning.
  401  p10  paragraph  [p10-l30]  Based on the above, we surmise that it is subjective meaning of one’s mental representation of the
  402  p10  paragraph  [p10-l31]  "thing" being observed (either external or internal) that the subject is aware of during a conscious
  403  p10  paragraph  [p10-l32]  experience. The raw feel of a conscious experience, therefore, has a sense to it, coming from the
  404  p10  paragraph  [p10-l33]  mental representation of that what is being experienced.
  405  p10  figure  [p10-d5]  
  406  p10  paragraph  [p10-l34]  7Psychophysical studies involving preverbal human infants have demonstrated compelling evidence of the use
  407  p10  paragraph  [p10-l35]  of logical reasoning and probabilistic inference in the way humans learn and construct mental representations of
  408  p10  paragraph  [p10-l36]  objects in the world [36, 121]. These early representations are constructed using logical primitives and rules of
  409  p10  paragraph  [p10-l37]  composition [106].
  410  p10  paragraph  [p10-l38]  8See [47] for discussions on mathematical foundations of "what is a thing?"; and [134] for a discussion on the
  411  p10  paragraph  [p10-l39]  metaphysics of abstract and concrete objects.
  412  p10  paragraph  [p10-l40]  9Several philosophers including Husserl and Merleau-Ponty have emphasized this point [68, 88] .
  413  p10  furniture  [p10-l41]  10
  414  p11  paragraph  [p11-l1]  Two remarks are in order here:
  415  p11  paragraph  [p11-l2]  Remark 3.2. There is of course room to argue that there may be other attributes of mental rep-
  416  p11  paragraph  [p11-l3]  resentations such as its symbolic, computational or informational features that also partake in the
  417  p11  paragraph  [p11-l4]  realm of awareness. However, unlike meaning, these other features are not non-representational.
  418  p11  paragraph  [p11-l5]  Which is why, they do in fact admit a third-person description. However, qualia, having a phe-
  419  p11  paragraph  [p11-l6]  nomenal character, are deemed non-representational. For that reason, we maintain that subjective
  420  p11  paragraph  [p11-l7]  meaning is the more compelling candidate that captures the phenomenal character of qualia.
  421  p11  paragraph  [p11-l8]  Remark 3.3. Let us also briefly comment on empirical studies that also invoke the notion of
  422  p11  paragraph  [p11-l9]  sense in consciousness research. In empirical and clinical literature on consciousness, one often
  423  p11  paragraph  [p11-l10]  encounters sense via terms such as, "sense of agency", "sense of self", "sense of ownership",
  424  p11  paragraph  [p11-l11]  "sense of time", and so on [42,67,80,84,132]. In all of these contexts, sense is often described as
  425  p11  paragraph  [p11-l12]  a subjective perception or comprehension of something. Notice that such a description is precisely a
  426  p11  paragraph  [p11-l13]  semantic attribute based on personal significance to the experiencing subject. The same can be said
  427  p11  paragraph  [p11-l14]  for other examples of sense such as an intense sensation of pain, an abrupt sense of discomfort,
  428  p11  paragraph  [p11-l15]  or a sudden surge of pleasure. In fact, sense and feeling are sometimes used interchangeably in
  429  p11  paragraph  [p11-l16]  this literature.
  430  p11  paragraph  [p11-l18]  The Formal Structure of Meaning
  431  p11  paragraph  [p11-l17]  4
  432  p11  paragraph  [p11-l19]  So far, we have put forth, mostly on phenomenological grounds, that meaning, generated intrin-
  433  p11  paragraph  [p11-l20]  sically by the experiencing self, is an integral and inseparable characteristic of qualia, across the
  434  p11  paragraph  [p11-l21]  board. Furthermore, the subjectivity, non-representational and as well, the transparency of, what
  435  p11  paragraph  [p11-l22]  has been referred to as subjective meaning, suggests why it may be directly linked to the phenom-
  436  p11  paragraph  [p11-l23]  enal contents of conscious experience. However, to simply label meaning as a mental property,
  437  p11  paragraph  [p11-l24]  does not do full justice to the important role that meaning plays with regard to the mind-matter
  438  p11  paragraph  [p11-l25]  relation. This view has also been proposed in [23, 24], albeit, based on different motivations. To
  439  p11  paragraph  [p11-l26]  elucidate the character of meaning with respect to the nature of qualia, and also its relevance to
  440  p11  paragraph  [p11-l27]  the mind-matter relation, we first need to discuss the formal structure of meaning itself.
  441  p11  paragraph  [p11-l28]  Of course, the concept of meaning is not exclusive to the philosophy of mind. Rather,
  442  p11  paragraph  [p11-l29]  it has a shared history across disciplines, including linguistics, semiotics, programming language
  443  p11  paragraph  [p11-l30]  theory, type theory, category theory and metamathematics, to name a few. Based on the specific
  444  p11  paragraph  [p11-l31]  domain, one considers or constructs appropriate semantic frameworks for given syntactic systems
  445  p11  paragraph  [p11-l32]  (both, verbal or non-verbal). A semantic framework provides an interpretation space for syntactic
  446  p11  paragraph  [p11-l33]  expressions. For instance, a symbolic expression such as a sentence or a logical proof, by itself,
  447  p11  paragraph  [p11-l34]  is a purely syntactic construct. It is its semantics that elucidates what the sentence or proof
  448  p11  paragraph  [p11-l35]  refers to or how it can be interpreted. For this reason, one works with a formal interpretation
  449  p11  paragraph  [p11-l36]  space within which syntactic expressions may be evaluated. These evaluations may either yield
  450  p11  paragraph  [p11-l37]  truth values or, more generally, meaning vectors (see distributional models of meaning [40]).
  451  p11  paragraph  [p11-l38]  Semantic models thus enable value or categorical evaluations of the syntax. However, what is
  452  p11  paragraph  [p11-l39]  important are not the specific categories or values that a syntactic structure may acquire within
  453  p11  paragraph  [p11-l40]  a model (which often depend on model constraints); rather, it is that these evaluations establish
  454  p11  paragraph  [p11-l41]  relations between syntactic expressions. For example, relations of similarity. It is such relations
  455  p11  furniture  [p11-l42]  11
  456  p12  paragraph  [p12-l1]  that are hypothesized to give words and sentences their meanings, which is how the so-called
  457  p12  paragraph  [p12-l2]  "compositional distributional models of meaning" operate [38].
  458  p12  paragraph  [p12-l3]  Let us consider the case of natural language semantics. Here, one may want to study
  459  p12  paragraph  [p12-l4]  lexical semantics which refers to word meanings, and how meanings between similar words might
  460  p12  paragraph  [p12-l5]  relate. In that case, an often used dictum due to J. R. Firth goes as, "You shall know a word
  461  p12  paragraph  [p12-l6]  by the company it keeps" [50]. That is, words with similar linguistic distributional properties
  462  p12  paragraph  [p12-l7]  (in large corpus data) have similar meanings. This is known as the distributional hypothesis of
  463  p12  paragraph  [p12-l8]  meaning [62]. Additionally, besides word meanings, one may also want to investigate meanings
  464  p12  paragraph  [p12-l9]  of phrases and sentences (given the meanings of the words contained therein). In this case, one
  465  p12  paragraph  [p12-l10]  resorts to the "principle of semantic compositionality", which is also known as Frege’s principle
  466  p12  paragraph  [p12-l11]  [104]. According to this, the meaning of a complex expression is derived from the meanings of its
  467  p12  paragraph  [p12-l12]  constituent expressions and the syntactic rules used to combine them10. Hence, the meaning of a
  468  p12  paragraph  [p12-l13]  sentence is then obtained by putting together these two principles, which yields what is known as
  469  p12  paragraph  [p12-l14]  the "Compositional Distributional Semantics" framework [40]. The mathematical formalization of
  470  p12  paragraph  [p12-l15]  this framework in terms of monoidal categories of vector spaces and Lambek’s pregroup grammar
  471  p12  paragraph  [p12-l16]  enables one to compute the meaning of well-typed sentences from the meaning of its constituent
  472  p12  paragraph  [p12-l17]  words, by inducing the type reduction mechanisms of the pregroup grammar to the whole category.
  473  p12  paragraph  [p12-l18]  These sentence meanings live in a single space, realized via monoidal (tensor) products of the
  474  p12  paragraph  [p12-l19]  category. Meaning is thus evaluated via a category-theoretic functor from a syntactic space
  475  p12  paragraph  [p12-l20]  governed by rules of grammar, to a semantic space, functioning as an interpretation space for
  476  p12  paragraph  [p12-l21]  syntactic constructs.
  477  p12  paragraph  [p12-l22]  Interestingly, such a functorial perspective (as elucidated above) turns out to be much
  478  p12  paragraph  [p12-l23]  more general than its particular instantiation in linguistics alone. In fact, in mathematical logic
  479  p12  paragraph  [p12-l24]  and abstract algebra this goes by the name of "Functorial Semantics" [78]. For example, an
  480  p12  paragraph  [p12-l25]  association map from a given syntactic system to a semantic system, such as:
  481  p12  paragraph  [p12-l26]  Association Map : Syntactic Space → Semantic Space
  482  p12  paragraph  [p12-l27]  (1)
  483  p12  paragraph  [p12-l28]  may realize how abstract algebraic systems yield a geometric representation within an appropriate
  484  p12  paragraph  [p12-l29]  space (for instance, the representation theory of groups). In this case, the "intuition" or physical
  485  p12  paragraph  [p12-l30]  interpretation of the algebraic system is a geometric one.
  486  p12  paragraph  [p12-l31]  Remark 4.1. It is interesting to see how eq.(1) compares to Charles S. Peirce’s Theory of Signs,
  487  p12  paragraph  [p12-l32]  or Semiotics [102, 103]. Semiotics refers to a formal theory of signification of entities (across
  488  p12  paragraph  [p12-l33]  science and philosophy), their representation, in terms of suitable signs, and the meaning of those
  489  p12  paragraph  [p12-l34]  signs [22]. The essential elements of Peirce’s theory include a triad consisting of a sign S, the
  490  p12  paragraph  [p12-l35]  object O, and the interpretant I. The sign can be thought of as a symbol or a signifier of the thing
  491  p12  paragraph  [p12-l36]  (the object) being signified. Whereas, the interpretant is the understanding (sometimes described
  492  p12  paragraph  [p12-l37]  as a "translation") that the user of the sign has of the object-sign relation [22, 117].
  493  p12  figure  [p12-d1]  
  494  p12  paragraph  [p12-l38]  10Of course, compositionality is not the only guiding principle for semantics of complex expressions or conceptual
  495  p12  paragraph  [p12-l39]  combinations. One also has to take into account contributions from non-compositional semantics, as illustrated
  496  p12  paragraph  [p12-l40]  in [34]. These authors emphasize that the latter is markedly distinct from the former in terms of contextual
  497  p12  paragraph  [p12-l41]  dependence. This additional contribution is necessary for a more comprehensive treatment of natural language
  498  p12  paragraph  [p12-l42]  semantics and pragmatics. However, for our purposes here, compositional models will suffice for what follows.
  499  p12  paragraph  [p12-l43]  Additional contextual contributions can be included thereafter.
  500  p12  furniture  [p12-l44]  12
  501  p13  paragraph  [p13-l1]  The object determines the sign, and in turn, the sign, via the specific representation of the
  502  p13  paragraph  [p13-l2]  object, influences and shapes the interpretant. The order of these relations is so:
  503  p13  paragraph  [p13-l3]  O → S → I
  504  p13  paragraph  [p13-l4]  One can think of the relation O → S as a Representation of the object, while the relation S → I
  505  p13  paragraph  [p13-l5]  can be viewed as the Interpretation of the sign (or an Association that maps signs to what they
  506  p13  paragraph  [p13-l6]  refer to). The meaning of the sign, then manifests via the interpretation of the representation of
  507  p13  paragraph  [p13-l7]  the object being signified.
  508  p13  paragraph  [p13-l8]  The comparison to eq.(1) is now straightforward: the sign S belongs to the syntactic do-
  509  p13  paragraph  [p13-l9]  main, whereas the interpretant I is semantic. The interpretation map S → I, then corresponds
  510  p13  paragraph  [p13-l10]  to the association map in eq.(1).
  511  p13  paragraph  [p13-l11]  Now, let us take the above formal perspective of meaning, as a map from syntax to
  512  p13  paragraph  [p13-l12]  semantics (eq.(1)), more seriously. How does one apply this to the mind-matter problem? To do
  513  p13  paragraph  [p13-l13]  so, we propose the following maps of relations between the physical and mental domains:
  514  p13  paragraph  [p13-l19]  AP
  515  p13  paragraph  [p13-l14]  ObjP
  516  p13  figure  [p13-d3..p13-d4 (2)]  
  517  p13  paragraph  [p13-l15]  RelP
  518  p13  paragraph  [p13-l18]  RObj
  519  p13  figure  [p13-d1..p13-d2 (2)]  
  520  p13  figure  [p13-d5..p13-d6 (2)]  
  521  p13  paragraph  [p13-l20]  RRel
  522  p13  paragraph  [p13-l22]  (2)
  523  p13  paragraph  [p13-l16]  ObjM
  524  p13  figure  [p13-d7..p13-d8 (2)]  
  525  p13  paragraph  [p13-l17]  RelM
  526  p13  paragraph  [p13-l21]  AM
  527  p13  paragraph  [p13-l23]  This diagram requires some unpacking. Let us first motivate the relations shown therein,
  528  p13  paragraph  [p13-l24]  and then examine the structure of meaning appearing here and how that relates to qualia, dis-
  529  p13  paragraph  [p13-l25]  cussed above in earlier sections. After that, we indicate how this system of maps weighs on the
  530  p13  paragraph  [p13-l26]  mind-matter relation.
  531  p13  paragraph  [p13-l27]  The top row maps collections of physical objects ObjP to relations between these, indicated
  532  p13  paragraph  [p13-l28]  by RelP , via the association map
  533  p13  paragraph  [p13-l29]  AP : ObjP → RelP
  534  p13  paragraph  [p13-l30]  In fact, the association map AP defines relations contained within RelP . Note that these relations
  535  p13  paragraph  [p13-l31]  can be of general arity (in which case, the objects in RelP may be diagrammatically presented
  536  p13  paragraph  [p13-l32]  as hyperedges, and the AP denote multi-source / target maps for constructing hyperedges).
  537  p13  paragraph  [p13-l33]  Together, the collection {ObjP , AP (ObjP )} forms the physical domain, denoted P.
  538  p13  paragraph  [p13-l34]  Likewise, ObjM denotes collections of mental objects, and RelM contains relations (unary,
  539  p13  paragraph  [p13-l35]  binary or any higher arity) between mental objects. Here again, the association map
  540  p13  paragraph  [p13-l36]  AM : ObjM → RelM
  541  p13  furniture  [p13-l37]  13
  542  p14  paragraph  [p14-l1]  defines relations contained within RelM. We will refer to the collection {ObjM, AM(ObjM)} as
  543  p14  paragraph  [p14-l2]  the mental domain M.
  544  p14  paragraph  [p14-l3]  Furthermore, the two representation maps
  545  p14  paragraph  [p14-l4]  RObj : ObjP → ObjM
  546  p14  paragraph  [p14-l5]  and
  547  p14  paragraph  [p14-l6]  RRel : RelP → RelM
  548  p14  paragraph  [p14-l7]  (3)
  549  p14  paragraph  [p14-l8]  take objects and relations in the physical domain and construct models of these objects and
  550  p14  paragraph  [p14-l9]  relations respectively within the mental domain. At times, one interchangeably uses the term
  551  p14  paragraph  [p14-l10]  "mental representation" to refer to these models, rather than the maps themselves11. While
  552  p14  paragraph  [p14-l11]  mental models may well supervene upon the brain’s neuronal circuitry (and feedback loops with
  553  p14  paragraph  [p14-l12]  the environment, through the body), strictly speaking, these models are directly accessible only
  554  p14  paragraph  [p14-l13]  to the cognitive / conscious agent. For that reason, these models lie solely within the mental
  555  p14  paragraph  [p14-l14]  domain12, while the representations RObj and RRel themselves, are maps from the physical to the
  556  p14  paragraph  [p14-l15]  mental domain.
  557  p14  paragraph  [p14-l16]  Now, how does one realize meaning (in the sense of eq.(1)) within the framework of eq.(2)?
  558  p14  paragraph  [p14-l17]  In order to answer this, first let us note that the spaces of relations RelP and RelM above, precisely
  559  p14  paragraph  [p14-l18]  function as semantic spaces. In linguistics, and more specifically in natural language semantics,
  560  p14  paragraph  [p14-l19]  words (and sentences) that are deemed to carry similar meanings, are hypothesized to occur in
  561  p14  paragraph  [p14-l20]  similar linguistic distributions [62]. Semantic spaces are then constructed as vector spaces (or more
  562  p14  paragraph  [p14-l21]  generally, as metric spaces) endowed with a similarity metric, such that words or sentences with
  563  p14  paragraph  [p14-l22]  similar linguistic distributions are clustered together, and thus exhibit similar meanings. In other
  564  p14  paragraph  [p14-l23]  words, the meaning of a syntactic construct is a relational attribute that depends on how that
  565  p14  paragraph  [p14-l24]  construct is related (with respect to the similarity metric) to other constructs within that space.
  566  p14  paragraph  [p14-l25]  Meanings of concepts can be extracted from its network of relations with other concepts. Examples
  567  p14  paragraph  [p14-l26]  of such models include word embedding models in computational linguistics [92], and Gardenfors’
  568  p14  paragraph  [p14-l27]  "conceptual spaces" in cognitive science [59]. Furthermore, there is compelling evidence from
  569  p14  paragraph  [p14-l28]  neurophysiological studies demonstrating how semantic maps are encoded in the human brain as
  570  p14  paragraph  [p14-l29]  a network of (encoded) concepts, via similarity distance in encoding space [69, 100, 108]. Despite
  571  p14  paragraph  [p14-l30]  different choices in implementation, all the examples above suggest that relational maps between
  572  p14  paragraph  [p14-l31]  concepts provide the building blocks of semantic spaces.
  573  p14  paragraph  [p14-l32]  What about the space of syntax? Where does that appear within our framework? Syn-
  574  p14  paragraph  [p14-l33]  tactic structures refer to admissible compositions of symbols of a formal language, that generate
  575  p14  paragraph  [p14-l34]  the words or sentences of that language. What determines admissibility of symbolic compositions
  576  p14  paragraph  [p14-l35]  is the formal grammar specifying the language. In recent years, what one means by language
  577  p14  paragraph  [p14-l36]  and grammar has vastly generalized beyond traditional forms of written language. Examples
  578  p14  paragraph  [p14-l37]  include graph grammars in graphical languages [5–7, 83], pregroup grammars in diagrammatic
  579  p14  paragraph  [p14-l38]  languages involving string diagrams [40, 77], visual grammars corresponding to compositions in-
  580  p14  paragraph  [p14-l39]  volving visual scenes and actions [96,139], and hypergraph / operator algebras that extend graph
  581  p14  paragraph  [p14-l40]  grammars [4, 136, 137], just to name a few. In our case here, the collections of objects in both,
  582  p14  paragraph  [p14-l41]  ObjP and ObjM are not arbitrary collections. How objects interact and compose in the physical
  583  p14  paragraph  [p14-l42]  world, and the ensuing physical properties of these compositions, is highly constrained by the laws
  584  p14  figure  [p14-d1]  
  585  p14  paragraph  [p14-l43]  11More generally, mental representations may also encompass models of internal states, such as one’s self model
  586  p14  paragraph  [p14-l44]  (see [107] for an overview).
  587  p14  paragraph  [p14-l45]  12The neuroscientist experimenting upon the agent, is merely inferring what the model may be about.
  588  p14  furniture  [p14-l46]  14
  589  p15  paragraph  [p15-l1]  of physics; including laws of dynamical evolution, order effects such as causal ordering, kinemat-
  590  p15  paragraph  [p15-l2]  ical constraints such as conservation laws, and geometrical constraints due to the spaces within
  591  p15  paragraph  [p15-l3]  which these objects live. Furthermore, hierarchical structures of physical systems have their own
  592  p15  paragraph  [p15-l4]  macro-level laws in the form of statistical mechanics and thermodynamics. Consequently, rep-
  593  p15  paragraph  [p15-l5]  resentations of the physical world in ObjM are also modeled accordingly. The visual grammar
  594  p15  paragraph  [p15-l6]  discussed in [139], the "physics engine" in [76], and the "impossible figures" in [82], all lend strong
  595  p15  paragraph  [p15-l7]  evidence towards a grammar underlying mental representations13. In other words, structures that
  596  p15  paragraph  [p15-l8]  "live" in ObjP and ObjM, realize syntactically well-defined structures following a broader notion
  597  p15  paragraph  [p15-l9]  of grammar, as indicated in the examples and references above.
  598  p15  paragraph  [p15-l10]  Therefore, on one hand, we have rules constraining the space ObjP (and its representation
  599  p15  paragraph  [p15-l11]  in ObjM), that allow for only very specific compositions of physical (respectively, mental) objects
  600  p15  paragraph  [p15-l12]  corresponding to existing constructs in the world (mind). On the other hand, we have an associ-
  601  p15  paragraph  [p15-l13]  ation map AP , that defines relations between physically permissible objects, leading to the space
  602  p15  paragraph  [p15-l14]  of physical relations RelP , which in turn, functions as a semantic space for the physical domain.
  603  p15  paragraph  [p15-l15]  Correspondingly, we have the association map AM, defining relations between permissible mental
  604  p15  paragraph  [p15-l16]  objects (obtained as representations of physical objects or of internal states of the agent), and
  605  p15  paragraph  [p15-l17]  leading to the space of mental relations RelM, which now functions as a semantic space for the
  606  p15  paragraph  [p15-l18]  mental domain. As per eq.(1), in the system in eq.(2) we now have two possible levels at which
  607  p15  paragraph  [p15-l19]  meaning may manifest as a distributional and compositional attribute of these mappings from
  608  p15  paragraph  [p15-l20]  syntactic to semantic spaces: the physical level, as well as the mental level. However, these levels
  609  p15  paragraph  [p15-l21]  are not independent, and neither is the kind of meaning associated to these domains.
  610  p15  paragraph  [p15-l22]  Our central question is how does subjectively attributed meaning arise in the mind, and
  611  p15  paragraph  [p15-l23]  how does that associate to the broader mind-matter relation? Our map AP suggests that a
  612  p15  paragraph  [p15-l24]  formal notion of meaning also ought to exist within the physical domain (in terms of relations
  613  p15  paragraph  [p15-l25]  and interactions between physical structures, constrained by causal and statistical laws). However,
  614  p15  paragraph  [p15-l26]  there needs to be something to read-off this meaning associated to the physical world14. Now this
  615  p15  paragraph  [p15-l27]  is where mental states come into the picture. They serve as a means to read-off associations in
  616  p15  paragraph  [p15-l28]  the world. This is how that may be so: through the representation maps RObj and RRel, models
  617  p15  paragraph  [p15-l29]  of physical objects and their relations are constructed within the mental domain. Then, following
  618  p15  paragraph  [p15-l30]  simply the commutativity of the diagram in eq.(2), the association map AP (within the physical
  619  p15  paragraph  [p15-l31]  domain) induces the association map AM (within the mental domain). Thus, meanings about
  620  p15  paragraph  [p15-l32]  things in the physical world can be read-off via mental states, following this relation between the
  621  p15  paragraph  [p15-l33]  physical and mental domains.
  622  p15  paragraph  [p15-l34]  Remark 4.2. Once again, notice the parallels to Peirce’s object-sign-interpretant triad in semi-
  623  p15  paragraph  [p15-l35]  otics. The O → S → I relations in Remark 4.1 are precisely captured by the following sequence
  624  p15  paragraph  [p15-l36]  of maps in eq.(2):
  625  p15  paragraph  [p15-l37]  ObjP → ObjM → RelM
  626  p15  paragraph  [p15-l38]  where elements of ObjM play the role of signs with respect to the mind-matter relation, and RelM
  627  p15  paragraph  [p15-l39]  give rise to Peirce’s interpretant, corresponding to mental objects. Of course, the above is only
  628  p15  figure  [p15-d1]  
  629  p15  paragraph  [p15-l40]  13Needless to say, there have been numerous debates in the literature as to whether grammar, in some form,
  630  p15  paragraph  [p15-l41]  may be innate to cognitive agents [60].
  631  p15  paragraph  [p15-l42]  14One may well call such an entity an "Observer". Though this is by no means a sufficient condition to define an
  632  p15  paragraph  [p15-l43]  observer. Hence, to read-off any kind of meaning, an observer is imperative. A discussion about observer theory
  633  p15  paragraph  [p15-l44]  from the perspective of second-order cybernetics can be found in [112].
  634  p15  furniture  [p15-l45]  15
  635  p16  paragraph  [p16-l1]  a particular application of Peirce’s theory of signs, which otherwise, was intended for broader
  636  p16  paragraph  [p16-l2]  applications across the sciences.
  637  p16  paragraph  [p16-l3]  Returning to our discussion about the meaning of things in the physical world, the astute
  638  p16  paragraph  [p16-l4]  reader will immediately notice that this is not the only instantiation of meaning available to one’s
  639  p16  paragraph  [p16-l5]  mental faculties. That is indeed so. And this is where internal states, as well as perceptual
  640  p16  paragraph  [p16-l6]  limitations of the subject, matter. Let us discuss this now:
  641  p16  paragraph  [p16-l7]  (i) First, as has been well documented in the literature, mental representations of objects and
  642  p16  paragraph  [p16-l8]  relations in the physical world, need not always be complete or accurate descriptions of those
  643  p16  paragraph  [p16-l9]  physical entities [107]. In practice, the cognitive / conscious agent has to build models of both,
  644  p16  paragraph  [p16-l10]  the world as well as that of other agents from incomplete social, behavioral and sensorimotor data
  645  p16  paragraph  [p16-l11]  (either due to stimulus uncertainty, hidden states of other agents, or the agent’s own limitations
  646  p16  paragraph  [p16-l12]  in processing and parsing all that information), about objects and agents in its environment,
  647  p16  paragraph  [p16-l13]  obtained in real-time [8–10, 16, 44, 54, 56, 115].
  648  p16  paragraph  [p16-l14]  (ii) Second, and more importantly, minds can also model their own internal states. The self-model
  649  p16  paragraph  [p16-l15]  being a classic example. And these internal models within the mental domain also influence each
  650  p16  paragraph  [p16-l16]  other. Again, the example being how the self-model modulates the way the agent probes its
  651  p16  paragraph  [p16-l17]  environment to construct a world model, and how through its self-model, the agent perceives its
  652  p16  paragraph  [p16-l18]  situated and embodied self, immersed within the world. What this means is that there is a self-
  653  p16  paragraph  [p16-l19]  referential aspect within the design of mental models, which is arguably a peculiar feature of the
  654  p16  paragraph  [p16-l20]  mental domain. This self-referential feature, or circular feedback, can be modeled via self-loops
  655  p16  paragraph  [p16-l21]  within the mental domain. This gives us the following maps15:
  656  p16  paragraph  [p16-l22]  SObj : ObjM → ObjM
  657  p16  paragraph  [p16-l23]  and
  658  p16  paragraph  [p16-l24]  SRel : RelM → RelM
  659  p16  paragraph  [p16-l25]  which extend the diagram in eq.(2) by:
  660  p16  figure  [p16-i1]  
  661  p16  paragraph  [p16-l26]  (4)
  662  p16  paragraph  [p16-l27]  In other words, in ObjM we have additional syntactic rules (compared to those in the
  663  p16  paragraph  [p16-l28]  purely physical domain) governing how mental models interact with each other. For instance,
  664  p16  paragraph  [p16-l29]  how the self-model (which includes models of the agent’s psychological as well as bodily faculties)
  665  p16  paragraph  [p16-l30]  may interact with or influence parameters within the subject’s world-model in order to generate
  666  p16  paragraph  [p16-l31]  anticipatory and adaptive control responses to a planned action [12, 65, 66, 91]. Correspondingly,
  667  p16  paragraph  [p16-l32]  the semantic space RelM also captures relations within and between all internal models.
  668  p16  figure  [p16-d1]  
  669  p16  paragraph  [p16-l33]  15Note that these are not identity morphisms, but non-trivial mappings within the mental domain.
  670  p16  furniture  [p16-l34]  16
  671  p17  paragraph  [p17-l1]  Remark 4.3. The maps, in eqns.(2) and (4) above, demonstrate how meaning involves relations
  672  p17  paragraph  [p17-l2]  between the physical and mental domains. The mind-matter relation thus plays a central role in
  673  p17  paragraph  [p17-l3]  realizing subjective meaning within mental states. How does one realize sense and reference within
  674  p17  paragraph  [p17-l4]  this framework?
  675  p17  paragraph  [p17-l5]  The map from ObjP to RelM following the composition16 AP ◦RRel suggests how reference
  676  p17  paragraph  [p17-l6]  (as a relation between the physical and the mental) about things in the world, as perceived by an
  677  p17  paragraph  [p17-l7]  agent with mental states, may be formalized:- As a map from structure in the world to relations in
  678  p17  paragraph  [p17-l8]  the mind, that factors through physical relations actually found in the physical world, and hence
  679  p17  paragraph  [p17-l9]  relies on the association map AP .
  680  p17  paragraph  [p17-l10]  On the other hand, the composition RObj◦AM suggests how sense (as a relation between the
  681  p17  paragraph  [p17-l11]  physical and the mental), as perceived by the observing agent, may be formalized:- As a map from
  682  p17  paragraph  [p17-l12]  structure in the world to relations in the mind, that now factors through various mental models
  683  p17  paragraph  [p17-l13]  that the agent may have, and hence relies on the association map AM. However, to fully account
  684  p17  paragraph  [p17-l14]  for sense as this composition of maps, one has to admit a slight generalization of the diagram in
  685  p17  paragraph  [p17-l15]  eq.(2) such that it is commutative up to higher homotopy (in this case, a 2-morphism)17 .
  686  p17  paragraph  [p17-l16]  As an example, consider a physical object; say, the planet Venus. Its reference map passes
  687  p17  paragraph  [p17-l17]  through physical relations in the world (such as the planet between Mercury and Earth) which
  688  p17  paragraph  [p17-l18]  constrain its concept within the mental domain; and that yields its meaning as reference. On
  689  p17  paragraph  [p17-l19]  the other hand, its sense map passes through mental objects (which are mental models that the
  690  p17  paragraph  [p17-l20]  mind uses to know about these objects). These mental models are based on how the subject has
  691  p17  paragraph  [p17-l21]  experienced the planet Venus, either as the morning star, or the evening star. Hence with respect
  692  p17  paragraph  [p17-l22]  to sense as a relation, there are more than one possible paths that could be followed; which explains
  693  p17  paragraph  [p17-l23]  why there can be different senses for the same object.
  694  p17  paragraph  [p17-l24]  Remark 4.4. Based on the above, sense and reference are both crucial to a subject’s experience.
  695  p17  paragraph  [p17-l25]  Major dissociations or disruptions between pathways related to sense and those to reference, would
  696  p17  paragraph  [p17-l26]  arguably be implicated in aberrant psychological conditions.
  697  p17  paragraph  [p17-l27]  For example, sense without reference would suggest domination of internal biases over
  698  p17  paragraph  [p17-l28]  external evidence, as in cases of hallucination or delusion. Reference without sense corresponds
  699  p17  paragraph  [p17-l29]  to a state of being unable to contextualize what is being observed. Therefore, both sense and
  700  p17  paragraph  [p17-l30]  reference play a vital role in the phenomenology of consciousness.
  701  p17  paragraph  [p17-l31]  Also, in future technology aiming to achieve Artificial General Intelligence (AGI), we posit
  702  p17  paragraph  [p17-l32]  that such a corroboration between sense and reference would, at the very least, be necessary (if
  703  p17  paragraph  [p17-l33]  not sufficient).
  704  p17  figure  [p17-d1]  
  705  p17  paragraph  [p17-l34]  16In mathematical literature such a composition of maps is often written in right-to-left notation, but here we
  706  p17  paragraph  [p17-l35]  prefer the left-to-right presentation for the sake of better readibility.
  707  p17  paragraph  [p17-l36]  17We thank Giulio Katis for bringing this point to our attention. A thorough category-theoretic treatment of
  708  p17  paragraph  [p17-l37]  this diagram along with admissible higher morphisms will be reported in a forthcoming article.
  709  p17  furniture  [p17-l38]  17
  710  p18  paragraph  [p18-l1]  5
  711  p18  paragraph  [p18-l2]  Discussion
  712  p18  paragraph  [p18-l3]  This work posits that subjectively attributed meaning is ubiquitous in conscious experiences.
  713  p18  paragraph  [p18-l4]  Our hypothesis is that subjective meaning (or, one may also refer to it as intrinsic meaning)
  714  p18  paragraph  [p18-l5]  is intrinsic to the qualia of conscious experience, in that, the phenomenal content of conscious
  715  p18  paragraph  [p18-l6]  experience is precisely this type of meaning. In other words, we claim that the content of conscious
  716  p18  paragraph  [p18-l7]  experience is semantic. To support this proposal, we have discussed literature examples from the
  717  p18  paragraph  [p18-l8]  phenomenology of consciousness, which demonstrate the close connection between meaning and
  718  p18  paragraph  [p18-l9]  experience.
  719  p18  paragraph  [p18-l10]  Our notion of subjective meaning closely relates to what Frege has referred to as "sense".
  720  p18  paragraph  [p18-l11]  Furthermore, we have noted out how subjective meaning also parallels Peirce’s "interpretant" in
  721  p18  paragraph  [p18-l12]  semiotics. As a special case of Peirce’s theory of signs, Peirce’s object-sign-interpretant triad is
  722  p18  paragraph  [p18-l13]  captured following the sequence of maps ObjP → ObjM → RelM, in eq.(2).
  723  p18  paragraph  [p18-l14]  Building upon the above, we have argued that raw feels can not be devoid of subjective
  724  p18  paragraph  [p18-l15]  meaning. It is this form of meaning of one’s mental representation (of the stimuli) that the subject
  725  p18  paragraph  [p18-l16]  is aware of during a conscious experience. The raw feel of a conscious experience has a sense to
  726  p18  paragraph  [p18-l17]  it (à la Frege), one that comes from the mental representation of that what is being experienced.
  727  p18  paragraph  [p18-l18]  Furthermore, we have presented a formal structure of maps that elucidates the role of
  728  p18  paragraph  [p18-l19]  subjective meaning in the context of the mind-matter relation. Exploiting the mapping between
  729  p18  paragraph  [p18-l20]  the physical and mental domains, we show how syntactic and semantic structures are realized
  730  p18  paragraph  [p18-l21]  within both, the physical and the mental domain. In this sense, grammar and meaning transcend
  731  p18  paragraph  [p18-l22]  conventional written language. Meaning is realized as a relational attribute arising from a map
  732  p18  paragraph  [p18-l23]  that interprets syntactic structures of a formal system within an appropriate semantic space.
  733  p18  paragraph  [p18-l24]  Based on this, we have provided an explanation of how sense and reference may be formally
  734  p18  paragraph  [p18-l25]  realized within the mind-matter relation.
  735  p18  paragraph  [p18-l26]  Our notion of subjective meaning is the mental image of Brentano’s intentionality. It is this
  736  p18  paragraph  [p18-l27]  subjective facet of meaning that a conscious agent experiences with their mind. For this reason,
  737  p18  paragraph  [p18-l28]  we could have also referred to this notion of meaning as intrinsic meaning. The full relational
  738  p18  paragraph  [p18-l29]  map of meaning (linking the physical to the mental) is only accessible to a metaphysical observer.
  739  p18  paragraph  [p18-l30]  Working from such an intrinsic point of view is of direct relevance to any experience-based theory
  740  p18  paragraph  [p18-l31]  of consciousness18 .
  741  p18  paragraph  [p18-l32]  Given that this work places meaning of an intrinsic kind at the heart of the problem of
  742  p18  paragraph  [p18-l33]  consciousness, or the qualia of it thereof, it is reasonable to ask whether or not conscious aware-
  743  p18  paragraph  [p18-l34]  ness itself is a guise of subjective meaning, as structurally defined in eq.(2) above? Though this
  744  p18  paragraph  [p18-l35]  view may appear to be in contrast to other proposals, such as consciousness being integrated
  745  p18  paragraph  [p18-l36]  information [125], or that, consciousness being identical to a specific maximally irreducible causal
  746  p18  paragraph  [p18-l37]  structure [99], or that, consciousness being a form of computation [43]; it may well be that these
  747  p18  paragraph  [p18-l38]  are different pieces of the larger puzzle, and perhaps one may find convergence of some of these
  748  p18  paragraph  [p18-l39]  different ideas in future theories of consciousness. For instance, a suggestion of how Integrated
  749  p18  figure  [p18-d1]  
  750  p18  paragraph  [p18-l40]  18Of course, one may certainly choose to consider other points of view, other than the one that is intrinsic to
  751  p18  paragraph  [p18-l41]  experience, as considered here. Each such case will have its own metaphysical considerations to take into account,
  752  p18  paragraph  [p18-l42]  as well as how that may translate to experience.
  753  p18  furniture  [p18-l43]  18
  754  p19  paragraph  [p19-l1]  Information Theory itself may be extended to include compositional meaning was already pro-
  755  p19  paragraph  [p19-l2]  posed19 in [2]. However, note that any such synthesis of ideas does not imply that information,
  756  p19  paragraph  [p19-l3]  causality, computation or meaning can simply be used interchangeably as a theoretical basis for
  757  p19  paragraph  [p19-l4]  consciousness science. Each of these are conceptually distinct (and also differ in the way they
  758  p19  paragraph  [p19-l5]  could be used as a basis of a scientific theory). However, different ways of looking at the problem
  759  p19  paragraph  [p19-l6]  (of consciousness) may certainly be a very fruitful exercise. What this work attempts to highlight
  760  p19  paragraph  [p19-l7]  is that meaning, of the kind that is subjectively attributed, seems to account for the phenomenal
  761  p19  paragraph  [p19-l8]  and non-representational aspects of conscious experience.
  762  p19  paragraph  [p19-l9]  Taking Frege’s philosophy of language and mind seriously, we surmise that that syntax
  763  p19  paragraph  [p19-l10]  and semantics are also relevant to a theory of consciousness. In particular, mental representations
  764  p19  paragraph  [p19-l11]  are to the mind, what symbolic expressions are to language. Within such a philosophical framing,
  765  p19  paragraph  [p19-l12]  when one speaks about awareness about "something", one may then ask: precisely what attribute
  766  p19  paragraph  [p19-l13]  of the "thing" is it, that the subject is actually aware of? The answer, subjective meaning "of"
  767  p19  paragraph  [p19-l14]  the subject’s mental representation of the "thing", seems better suited than alternatives such
  768  p19  paragraph  [p19-l15]  as information, causality or computation. That is not to say that information, causality or
  769  p19  paragraph  [p19-l16]  computation are not useful to study how consciousness may work. In fact, these are absolutely
  770  p19  paragraph  [p19-l17]  indispensable for quantifying neuronal processes in brain networks (or for that matter, artificial
  771  p19  paragraph  [p19-l18]  neural networks), that may correlate to empirical states of consciousness [1, 11, 13, 14, 17–21,
  772  p19  paragraph  [p19-l19]  116]. However, if one wants to explain what may constitute the non-representational content of
  773  p19  paragraph  [p19-l20]  phenomenal experience, then, the proposal of subjective meaning seems to offer a promising new
  774  p19  paragraph  [p19-l21]  avenue for conceptualizing theories of consciousness. Such a notion of meaning is the attribute
  775  p19  paragraph  [p19-l22]  (of the thing observed) that the subject may well be aware of, or may sense (à la Frege) during
  776  p19  paragraph  [p19-l23]  conscious experience. This hypothesis, of subjective meaning as the phenomenal constituent of
  777  p19  paragraph  [p19-l24]  qualia of conscious experience, also suggests that one take ideas from the philosophy of language
  778  p19  paragraph  [p19-l25]  and semiotics more seriously towards the advancement of a comprehensive theoretical framework
  779  p19  paragraph  [p19-l26]  that satisfactorily addresses the phenomenal character of consciousness.
  780  p19  paragraph  [p19-l27]  Acknowledgments
