  arxiv2024-liu-orbital-hall.pdf  language en  blocks 805
  0  p1  paragraph  [p1-l1]  Dominance of extrinsic scattering mechanisms in the orbital Hall effect: graphene,
  1  p1  paragraph  [p1-l2]  transition metal dichalcogenides and topological antiferromagnets
  2  p1  paragraph  [p1-l3]  Hong Liu1 and Dimitrie Culcer1
  3  p1  paragraph  [p1-l4]  1School of Physics and Australian Research Council Centre of Excellence in Low-Energy Electronics Technologies,
  4  p1  paragraph  [p1-l5]  UNSW Node, The University of New South Wales, Sydney 2052, Australia
  5  p1  paragraph  [p1-l6]  (Dated: April 5, 2024)
  6  p1  paragraph  [p1-l7]  The theory of the orbital Hall effect (OHE), a transverse flow of orbital angular momentum
  7  p1  paragraph  [p1-l8]  (OAM) in response to an electric field, has concentrated on intrinsic mechanisms. Here, using a
  8  p1  paragraph  [p1-l9]  quantum kinetic formulation, we determine the full OHE in the presence of short-range disorder
  9  p1  paragraph  [p1-l10]  using 2D massive Dirac fermions as a prototype. We find that, in doped systems, extrinsic effects
  10  p1  paragraph  [p1-l11]  associated with the Fermi surface (skew scattering and side jump) provide ≈ 95% of the OHE. This
  11  p1  paragraph  [p1-l12]  suggests that, at experimentally relevant transport densities, the OHE is primarily extrinsic.
  12  p1  paragraph  [p1-l13]  Introduction. The electrical manipulation of magnetic
  13  p1  paragraph  [p1-l14]  degrees of freedom has been continuously investigated
  14  p1  paragraph  [p1-l15]  since Øersted discovered the deflection of a compass nee-
  15  p1  paragraph  [p1-l16]  dle by a current-carrying wire. Modern research has fo-
  16  p1  paragraph  [p1-l17]  cused on the magneto-electric coupling provided by the
  17  p1  paragraph  [p1-l18]  spin-orbit interaction [1–4], while an energetic recent ef-
  18  p1  paragraph  [p1-l19]  fort has been geared towards the electrical operation of
  19  p1  paragraph  [p1-l20]  orbital degrees of freedom in systems without spin-orbit
  20  p1  paragraph  [p1-l21]  coupling [5–12]. This effort centres on the realization
  21  p1  paragraph  [p1-l22]  that Bloch electrons, possess an orbital angular momen-
  22  p1  paragraph  [p1-l23]  tum (OAM) about their centre of mass [13], which is in
  23  p1  paragraph  [p1-l24]  part related to the Berry curvature [14–18]. The OAM
  24  p1  paragraph  [p1-l25]  affects semiclassical quantization [15, 17, 18], contributes
  25  p1  paragraph  [p1-l26]  to the magnetization in certain materials [19–21], affects
  26  p1  paragraph  [p1-l27]  the Zeeman splitting of Dirac materials [22, 23] and con-
  27  p1  paragraph  [p1-l28]  tributes to the non-linear magneto-resistance, valley-Hall
  28  p1  paragraph  [p1-l29]  effect [19, 24, 25], and anomalous Nernst effect [26].
  29  p1  paragraph  [p1-l30]  In a time-reversal symmetric system a finite OAM den-
  30  p1  paragraph  [p1-l31]  sity can be generated by an electric field via the orbital
  31  p1  paragraph  [p1-l32]  Edelstein effect [27–31] or by separating electrons with
  32  p1  paragraph  [p1-l33]  different OAMs on different sides of the sample using an
  33  p1  paragraph  [p1-l34]  electric current. This is the orbital Hall effect (OHE)
  34  p1  paragraph  [p1-l35]  [7, 10, 32–39], which has received considerable attention
  35  p1  paragraph  [p1-l36]  recently [11, 12, 20, 34, 40–43]. The OHE has been pro-
  36  p1  paragraph  [p1-l37]  posed as the mechanism behind the valley Hall effect ob-
  37  p1  paragraph  [p1-l38]  served in graphene and transition metal dichalcogenides
  38  p1  paragraph  [p1-l39]  (TMDs) [33, 44]. Injection of an orbital current into a fer-
  39  p1  paragraph  [p1-l40]  romagnet generates an orbital torque on local magnetic
  40  p1  paragraph  [p1-l41]  moments [11, 12, 20, 34, 40–43], and the OHE may like-
  41  p1  paragraph  [p1-l42]  wise be responsible for the large spin Hall effect observed
  42  p1  paragraph  [p1-l43]  recently [10, 35–39]. Remarkably, all recent theoretical
  43  p1  paragraph  [p1-l44]  work has focused on intrinsic OHE mechanisms [45–49],
  44  p1  paragraph  [p1-l45]  while neglecting extrinsic disorder contributions. This
  45  p1  paragraph  [p1-l46]  absence is puzzling since extrinsic scattering mechanisms
  46  p1  paragraph  [p1-l47]  such as skew scattering and side jump are known to con-
  47  p1  paragraph  [p1-l48]  tribute to the anomalous and spin-Hall effects at the same
  48  p1  paragraph  [p1-l49]  order in the disorder strength as the intrinsic contribu-
  49  p1  paragraph  [p1-l50]  tion [37, 50–57]. It is natural to expect a substantial
  50  p1  paragraph  [p1-l51]  disorder contribution in the OHE. This has enormous
  51  p1  paragraph  [p1-l52]  implications for experiment: a signal may appear to be
  52  p1  paragraph  [p1-l53]  intrinsic, that is, independent of the disorder strength,
  53  p1  paragraph  [p1-l54]  yet our work shows it can still be almost entirely due to
  54  p1  paragraph  [p1-l55]  disorder.
