  75  p1  paragraph  [p1-l72]  Figure 1. The total, extrinsic and intrinsic OH-conductance
  76  p1  paragraph  [p1-l73]  σOH vs the Fermi energy EF for the 2H-phase monolayer
  77  p1  paragraph  [p1-l74]  MoS2. The parameters are Fermi velocity vF = 3.6eV˚A and
  78  p1  paragraph  [p1-l75]  energy gap 2∆ = 1.766eV.
  79  p1  paragraph  [p1-l76]  In this article, we calculate the full OHE, including in-
  80  p1  paragraph  [p1-l77]  trinsic and extrinsic contributions, for two-dimensional
  81  p1  paragraph  [p1-l78]  (2D) massive Dirac fermions, using graphene, TMDs,
  82  p1  paragraph  [p1-l79]  and topological antiferromagnets as prototype systems.
  83  p1  paragraph  [p1-l80]  We employ a quantum kinetic equation for the density
  84  p1  paragraph  [p1-l81]  matrix ˆρ, derived from the quantum Liouville equation,
  85  p1  paragraph  [p1-l82]  following the blueprint of Refs. [53, 58]. Our main find-
  86  p1  paragraph  [p1-l83]  ing is that, remarkably, the extrinsic OH-conductance
  87  p1  paragraph  [p1-l84]  overwhelms the intrinsic OH-conductance in doped sys-
  88  p1  paragraph  [p1-l85]  tems and provides the dominant contribution to the OHE
  89  p1  paragraph  [p1-l86]  when the Fermi energy lies in the conduction or valence
  90  p1  paragraph  [p1-l87]  bands. This finding overhauls the conventional interpre-
  91  p1  paragraph  [p1-l88]  tation of experimental data and suggests that what is
  92  p1  paragraph  [p1-l89]  measured experimentally is overwhelmingly the extrin-
  93  p1  paragraph  [p1-l90]  sic contribution. For the model studied here of massive
  94  p1  paragraph  [p1-l91]  Dirac fermions, and assuming short-range impurities, the
  95  p1  paragraph  [p1-l92]  extrinsic contribution is 21 times of the intrinsic one.
  96  p1  paragraph  [p1-l93]  Our central result is summarized in Fig. 1 with the to-
  97  p1  paragraph  [p1-l94]  tal, intrinsic and extrinsic OH-conductance σOH vs Fermi
  98  p1  paragraph  [p1-l95]  energy plotted separately. The orbital Hall current op-
  99  p1  paragraph  [p1-l96]  erator ˆj = 1 2 {Lˆ, vˆ} where Lˆ is the OAM operator and
  100  p1  figure  [p1-d97]  
  101  p1  paragraph  [p1-l97]  vˆ the velocity operator. With the applied electric field
