  401  p3  paragraph  [p3-l106]  The individual contributions are plotted in Fig. 3.
  402  p3  paragraph  [p3-l107]  Topological antiferromagnets. These are important for
  403  p3  paragraph  [p3-l108]  orbital torque applications: i. an external electric field
  404  p3  paragraph  [p3-l109]  can interact directly with orbital degrees of freedom with-
  405  p3  paragraph  [p3-l110]  out requiring the spin-orbit interaction, electrical gener-
  406  p3  paragraph  [p3-l111]  ation of OAM can rely on light atomic elements [60–63],
  407  p3  paragraph  [p3-l112]  widening the material choice for the electrical control of
  408  p3  paragraph  [p3-l113]  magnetism [20, 64, 65]. ii. the absence of spin-orbit cou-
  409  p3  paragraph  [p3-l114]  pling could result in long-lived magnetic information [5],
  410  p3  paragraph  [p3-l115]  the orbital torque is investigated as an alternative to the
  411  p3  paragraph  [p3-l116]  spin torque, which has applications in data storage and
  412  p3  paragraph  [p3-l117]  nonvolatile logic [4, 66, 67]. Topological antiferromagnets
  413  p3  paragraph  [p3-l118]  are described by a tilted Dirac Hamiltonian [19]:
  414  p3  paragraph  [p3-l119]  H0(q) = νvtqxτ0 + vF (qxτx − νqyτy) + ∆τz,
  415  p3  paragraph  [p3-l120]  (8)
  416  p3  paragraph  [p3-l121]  where vt introduces a tilt along qx-axis. This Hamilto-
  417  p3  paragraph  [p3-l122]  nian breaks both T - and P- symmetry. With ν = 1,
  418  p3  paragraph  [p3-l123]  the dispersion is E± q = vtqx ± Eq. The tilt will get
  419  p3  paragraph  [p3-l124]  into the energy conservation function in the scattering
  420  p3  paragraph  [p3-l125]  integral and Fermi distribution function, while the tilt
  421  p3  paragraph  [p3-l126]  doesn’t get into the eigenstates. To determine the OHE
  422  p3  paragraph  [p3-l127]  for the tilted 2D massive Dirac system one can expand
  423  p3  paragraph  [p3-l128]  the tilt up to first order in the energy function by assum-
  424  p3  paragraph  [p3-l129]  ing vt/vF ≪ 1. So in the scattering process, the energy
  425  p3  paragraph  [p3-l130]  conservation function can be expanded as δ(Em q −Em q′ ) ≈
  426  p3  paragraph  [p3-l131]  δ(Eq − Eq ′ ) + vtq � cos ϕ − cos ϕ ′ ∂δ(Eq−Eq′ )
  427  p3  figure  [p3-d210]  
  428  p3  paragraph  [p3-l133]  . The Fermi
  429  p3  paragraph  [p3-l132]  ∂Eq
  430  p3  paragraph  [p3-l134]  distribution function in equilibrium and at the absolute
  431  p3  paragraph  [p3-l135]  zero of temperature can also be expanded f (0)(E+ q ) ≈
  432  p3  paragraph  [p3-l136]  Θ(EF − E+ q ) = Θ(EF − Eq) + vtq cos ϕ ∂Θ(EF −Eq)
  433  p3  figure  [p3-d211]  
  434  p3  paragraph  [p3-l138]  . Then
  435  p3  paragraph  [p3-l137]  ∂Eq
  436  p3  paragraph  [p3-l139]  the density matrix can be decomposed as ρE +ρE,vt with
  437  p3  paragraph  [p3-l140]  ρE,vt the correction to first order in the tilt. The expan-
  438  p3  paragraph  [p3-l141]  sion involves an angular factor, which, in the operator
  439  p3  paragraph  [p3-l142]  trace, is further multiplied by angular factors in the den-
  440  p3  paragraph  [p3-l143]  sity matrix and velocity operator. The product of these
  441  p3  paragraph  [p3-l144]  three angular factors causes the correction to the OHE to
  442  p3  paragraph  [p3-l145]  first order in the tilt to vanish. Therefore, our calculation
  443  p3  paragraph  [p3-l146]  of the OHE in the 2D massive Dirac model applies to the
  444  p3  paragraph  [p3-l147]  tilted 2D massive Dirac model as well, up to terms of sec-
  445  p3  paragraph  [p3-l148]  ond order in the tilt. Based on the values in Refs. [68, 69],
  446  p3  paragraph  [p3-l149]  we expect such terms to account for < 5% of the total.
  447  p3  paragraph  [p3-l150]  Experimental observation. In systems with topological
  448  p3  paragraph  [p3-l151]  textures disorder yields contributions formally of zeroth
  449  p3  paragraph  [p3-l152]  order in the disorder strength, which are hard to distin-
  450  p3  paragraph  [p3-l153]  guish experimentally from the intrinsic terms in the DC
  451  p3  paragraph  [p3-l154]  regime. However, it should be possible to distinguish the
  452  p3  paragraph  [p3-l155]  intrinsic and extrinsic contributions to the OHE using
  453  p3  paragraph  [p3-l156]  AC techniques. This would be the orbital transport ana-
  454  p3  paragraph  [p3-l157]  logue of the magneto-optical Kerr effect [70], which was
  455  p3  paragraph  [p3-l158]  shown to be a strong probe of intrinsic transport in the
  456  p3  paragraph  [p3-l159]  anomalous Hall effect of topological insulators. Since dis-
  457  p3  paragraph  [p3-l160]  order effects are suppressed as 1/(ωτ ), with ω the light
  458  p3  paragraph  [p3-l161]  frequency, one requires ωτ ≫ 1. Taking τ ∼ 0.1ps places
  459  p3  paragraph  [p3-l162]  the wavelength in the ultraviolet range.
  460  p3  paragraph  [p3-l163]  Comparison with previous work.
  461  p3  paragraph  [p3-l164]  The total OH-
  462  p3  paragraph  [p3-l165]  conductance calculated here also reflects knowledge built
  463  p3  paragraph  [p3-l166]  up during earlier studies of the anomalous and spin-Hall
  464  p3  paragraph  [p3-l167]  effects [50–53]. In the spin Hall effect disorder corrections
  465  p3  paragraph  [p3-l168]  cancel the intrinsic contribution altogether, while in the
  466  p4  furniture  [p4-l1]  4
  467  p4  paragraph  [p4-l2]  anomalous Hall effect they overwhelm the intrinsic con-
  468  p4  paragraph  [p4-l3]  tribution by a factor of 7 [52, 58]. Here the extrinsic OHE
  469  p4  paragraph  [p4-l4]  is 21 times the intrinsic contribution. Recalling that for
  470  p4  paragraph  [p4-l5]  Dirac fermions the velocity and spin operators coincide,
  471  p4  paragraph  [p4-l6]  these factors are understandable in light of the qualita-
  472  p4  paragraph  [p4-l7]  tive difference between the orbital and spin angular mo-
  473  p4  paragraph  [p4-l8]  mentum operators [71]. The intrinsic and extrinsic terms
  474  p4  paragraph  [p4-l9]  discussed here form the main contributions to the OHE
  475  p4  paragraph  [p4-l10]  in the good metal regime, in the sense used in Ref. [1] for
  476  p4  paragraph  [p4-l11]  the anomalous Hall effect. Disorder effects beyond the
  477  p4  paragraph  [p4-l12]  first Born approximation, including correlations, may be
  478  p4  paragraph  [p4-l13]  important in certain regimes [72–74]. In particular, an
  479  p4  paragraph  [p4-l14]  additional skew scattering term appears in the second
  480  p4  paragraph  [p4-l15]  Born approximation, yielding a contribution to the OH-
  481  p4  paragraph  [p4-l16]  conductance ∝
  482  p4  figure  [p4-d1..p4-d2 (2)]  
  483  p4  paragraph  [p4-l17]  U 3 1
  484  p4  paragraph  [p4-l20]  (q 2 F v 2 F +∆2) , which dominates as the
  485  p4  paragraph  [p4-l19]  mev 2 F ∆2
  486  p4  paragraph  [p4-l18]  2πniU4 0
  487  p4  paragraph  [p4-l21]  ballistic limit is approached. The large extrinsic OHE in
  488  p4  paragraph  [p4-l22]  graphene and monolayer TMDs systems is due to their
  489  p4  paragraph  [p4-l23]  Dirac dispersion [75] and inversion symmetry breaking.
  490  p4  paragraph  [p4-l24]  The extrinsic OHE, including skew-scattering and side-
  491  p4  paragraph  [p4-l25]  jump, has been considered theoretically by Bernevig et al.
  492  p4  paragraph  [p4-l26]  in hole-doped Si [32]. The effect of impurities in the Kubo
  493  p4  paragraph  [p4-l27]  linear response theory of Ref. [32] is twofold: through the
  494  p4  paragraph  [p4-l28]  vertex correction to the current operator and the self-
  495  p4  paragraph  [p4-l29]  energy. Bernevig et al. showed that vertex corrections
  496  p4  paragraph  [p4-l30]  from impurity scattering vanish for a low-energy model
  497  p4  paragraph  [p4-l31]  for hole-doped Si. However, a recent work [76] shows this
  498  p4  paragraph  [p4-l32]  is not the case in generic inversion symmetric systems.
  499  p4  paragraph  [p4-l33]  Application of the theory. Some words are in order re-
  500  p4  paragraph  [p4-l34]  garding the range of applicability of our theory. Firstly,
  501  p4  paragraph  [p4-l35]  since most of the orbital moment contribution comes
  502  p4  paragraph  [p4-l36]  from the valley points [77], we considered the OHE from
  503  p4  paragraph  [p4-l37]  the K and K′ points, without including the contribu-
  504  p4  paragraph  [p4-l38]  tion from the remainder of the Brillouin zone. Secondly,
  505  p4  paragraph  [p4-l39]  we approximate the Fermi-Dirac function as a step func-
  506  p4  paragraph  [p4-l40]  tion which is the zero-temperature limit, whereas orbital
  507  p4  paragraph  [p4-l41]  Hall measurements are performed at room temperature
  508  p4  paragraph  [p4-l42]  i.e. 25 meV [7, 41]. Our main assumption is that the
  509  p4  paragraph  [p4-l43]  fermion system is degenerate, i.e. EF ≫ kBT. How-
  510  p4  paragraph  [p4-l44]  ever, based on realistic values for τ , our theory requires
  511  p4  paragraph  [p4-l45]  the Fermi energy to be away from the band edge by at
  512  p4  paragraph  [p4-l46]  least 50 meV in order to satisfy EF τ /ℏ ≫ 1. This is
  513  p4  paragraph  [p4-l47]  twice room temperature: whereas the assumption of de-
  514  p4  paragraph  [p4-l48]  generacy is not perfect, the system is still approximately
  515  p4  paragraph  [p4-l49]  degenerate. Thirdly, we have assumed short-range disor-
  516  p4  paragraph  [p4-l50]  der. For a more generic disorder model, although we do
  517  p4  paragraph  [p4-l51]  not expect the results to change qualitatively, the RHS of
  518  p4  paragraph  [p4-l52]  Eqs. (6) and (7) will depend on the angular characteris-
  519  p4  paragraph  [p4-l53]  tics of the scattering potential. Finally but most impor-
  520  p4  paragraph  [p4-l54]  tantly, the theory requires EF τ /ℏ ≫ 1, which excludes
  521  p4  paragraph  [p4-l55]  the low-density regions in the vicinity of the conduction
  522  p4  paragraph  [p4-l56]  and valence band edges. These regions are virtually im-
  523  p4  paragraph  [p4-l57]  possible to capture by most theoretical approaches. This
  524  p4  paragraph  [p4-l58]  is one of the fundamental paradoxes of transport theory:
  525  p4  paragraph  [p4-l59]  disorder is needed on physical grounds, but it needs to
  526  p4  paragraph  [p4-l60]  satisfy EF τ ≫ 1 (alternatively qF l ≫ 1, where l is the
  527  p4  paragraph  [p4-l61]  mean free path) for the theory to be applicable. The
  528  p4  paragraph  [p4-l62]  appearance of a term ∝ τ (i.e. ∝ 1/ni) is understood
  529  p4  paragraph  [p4-l63]  on physical grounds: it represents the shift in the Fermi
  530  p4  paragraph  [p4-l64]  surface induced by the electric field. The fact that this
  531  p4  paragraph  [p4-l65]  shift is ∝ τ reflects the need for disorder to be present so
  532  p4  paragraph  [p4-l66]  as to keep the Fermi surface near equilibrium. If disorder
  533  p4  paragraph  [p4-l67]  is removed entirely and τ formally tends to infinity this
  534  p4  paragraph  [p4-l68]  would imply that an infinitesimally small electric field
  535  p4  paragraph  [p4-l69]  can shift the Fermi surface infinitely far away from equi-
  536  p4  paragraph  [p4-l70]  librium. Such a process is unphysical, therefore the limit
  537  p4  paragraph  [p4-l71]  τ → ∞ is not captured in this formulation of transport
  538  p4  paragraph  [p4-l72]  theory. The resolution is that the size of the sample now
  539  p4  paragraph  [p4-l73]  needs to be considered and it is assumed that the mean
  540  p4  paragraph  [p4-l74]  free path is larger than the size of the sample, so the sys-
  541  p4  paragraph  [p4-l75]  tem transitions into the Landauer-Buttiker regime. This
  542  p4  paragraph  [p4-l76]  regime can also be captured in our density matrix lan-
  543  p4  paragraph  [p4-l77]  guage [78], but is unlikely to be relevant to the materials
  544  p4  paragraph  [p4-l78]  studied in the present work, where transport is diffusive.
  545  p4  paragraph  [p4-l79]  The opposite limit, qF → 0, is equivalent to EF τ ≪ 1,
  546  p4  paragraph  [p4-l80]  which represents the transition to the localised regime.
  547  p4  paragraph  [p4-l81]  The transport theory first needs to be augmented with
  548  p4  paragraph  [p4-l82]  the Cooperon in order to describe weak localisation [79].
  549  p4  paragraph  [p4-l83]  Conclusions. We have determined the disorder con-
  550  p4  paragraph  [p4-l84]  tribution to the OHE of 2D massive Dirac fermions and
  551  p4  paragraph  [p4-l85]  shown that it exceeds the intrinsic contribution by an
  552  p4  paragraph  [p4-l86]  order of magnitude, using graphene, transition metal
  553  p4  paragraph  [p4-l87]  dichalcogenides and topological antiferromagnets as pro-
  554  p4  paragraph  [p4-l88]  totypes. The calculation offers an approach to tuning
  555  p4  paragraph  [p4-l89]  and maximizing the orbital torque and can be extended
  556  p4  paragraph  [p4-l90]  to other classes of materials, such as Weyl and Dirac
  557  p4  paragraph  [p4-l91]  semimetals, topological insulators, and van der Waals
  558  p4  paragraph  [p4-l92]  heterostructures, as well as opening future perspectives
  559  p4  paragraph  [p4-l93]  on graphene-based orbitronics and twistronics [80].
  560  p4  paragraph  [p4-l94]  Acknowledgments. This work is supported by the Aus-
  561  p4  paragraph  [p4-l95]  tralian Research Council Centre of Excellence in Future
