  0  p1  figure  [p1-i1]  
  1  p1  paragraph  [p1-l1]  seaborn: statistical data visualization
  2  p1  paragraph  [p1-l2]  Michael L. Waskom1
  3  p1  paragraph  [p1-l3]  1 Center for Neural Science, New York University
  4  p1  figure  [p1-d1..p1-d3 (3)]  
  5  p1  paragraph  [p1-l21]  Summary
  6  p1  figure  [p1-d4..p1-d6 (3)]  
  7  p1  figure  [p1-d7..p1-d9 (3)]  
  8  p1  paragraph  [p1-l22]  seaborn is a library for making statistical graphics in Python. It provides a high-level interface
  9  p1  paragraph  [p1-l23]  to matplotlib and integrates closely with pandas data structures. Functions in the seaborn
  10  p1  figure  [p1-d10]  
  11  p1  paragraph  [p1-l24]  library expose a declarative, dataset-oriented API that makes it easy to translate questions
  12  p1  figure  [p1-d11..p1-d13 (3)]  
  13  p1  paragraph  [p1-l25]  about data into graphics that can answer them. When given a dataset and a specification
  14  p1  paragraph  [p1-l26]  of the plot to make, seaborn automatically maps the data values to visual attributes such
  15  p1  paragraph  [p1-l27]  as color, size, or style, internally computes statistical transformations, and decorates the plot
  16  p1  paragraph  [p1-l28]  with informative axis labels and a legend. Many seaborn functions can generate figures with
  17  p1  paragraph  [p1-l29]  multiple panels that elicit comparisons between conditional subsets of data or across different
  18  p1  paragraph  [p1-l30]  pairings of variables in a dataset. seaborn is designed to be useful throughout the lifecycle of
  19  p1  paragraph  [p1-l31]  a scientific project. By producing complete graphics from a single function call with minimal
  20  p1  paragraph  [p1-l32]  arguments, seaborn facilitates rapid prototyping and exploratory data analysis. And by
  21  p1  paragraph  [p1-l33]  offering extensive options for customization, along with exposing the underlying matplotlib
  22  p1  paragraph  [p1-l34]  objects, it can be used to create polished, publication-quality figures.
  23  p1  paragraph  [p1-l35]  Statement of need
  24  p1  paragraph  [p1-l36]  Data visualization is an indispensable part of the scientific process. Effective visualizations
  25  p1  paragraph  [p1-l37]  will allow a scientist both to understand their own data and to communicate their insights to
  26  p1  paragraph  [p1-l38]  others (Tukey, 1977). These goals can be furthered by tools for specifying a graph that provide
  27  p1  paragraph  [p1-l39]  a good balance between efficiency and flexibility. Within the scientific Python ecosystem, the
  28  p1  paragraph  [p1-l40]  matplotlib (Hunter, 2007) project is very well established, having been under continuous
  29  p1  paragraph  [p1-l41]  development for nearly two decades. It is highly flexible, offering fine-grained control over the
  30  p1  paragraph  [p1-l42]  placement and visual appearance of objects in a plot. It can be used interactively through GUI
  31  p1  paragraph  [p1-l43]  applications, and it can output graphics to a wide range of static formats. Yet its relatively
  32  p1  paragraph  [p1-l44]  low-level API can make some common tasks cumbersome to perform. For example, creating
  33  p1  paragraph  [p1-l45]  a scatter plot where the marker size represents a numeric variable and the marker shape
  34  p1  paragraph  [p1-l46]  represents a categorical variable requires one to transform the size values to graphical units
  35  p1  paragraph  [p1-l47]  and to loop over the categorical levels, separately invoking a plotting function for each marker
  36  p1  paragraph  [p1-l48]  type.
  37  p1  paragraph  [p1-l49]  The seaborn library offers an interface to matplotlib that permits rapid data exploration
  38  p1  paragraph  [p1-l50]  and prototyping of visualizations while retaining much of the flexibility and stability that are
  39  p1  paragraph  [p1-l51]  necessary to produce publication-quality graphics. It is domain-general and can be used to
  40  p1  paragraph  [p1-l52]  visualize a wide range of datasets that are well-represented within a tabular format.
  41  p1  paragraph  [p1-l53]  Example
  42  p1  paragraph  [p1-l54]  The following example demonstrates the creation of a figure with seaborn. The example
  43  p1  paragraph  [p1-l55]  makes use of one of the built-in datasets that are provided for documentation and generation of
  44  p1  figure  [p1-d14]  
