  100  p2  paragraph  [p2-l30]  timepoint
  101  p2  paragraph  [p2-l35]  Figure 1: An example seaborn figure demonstrating some of its key features. The image was
  102  p2  paragraph  [p2-l36]  generated using seaborn v0.11.1.
  103  p2  paragraph  [p2-l37]  Overview
  104  p2  paragraph  [p2-l38]  Users interface with seaborn through a collection of plotting functions that share a common
  105  p2  paragraph  [p2-l39]  API for plot specification and offer many more specific options for customization. These
  106  p2  paragraph  [p2-l40]  functions range from basic plot types such as scatter and line plots to functions that apply
  107  p2  paragraph  [p2-l41]  various transformations and abstractions, such as histogram binning, kernel density estimation,
  108  p2  paragraph  [p2-l42]  and regression model fitting. Functions in seaborn are classified as either “axes-level” or
  109  p2  paragraph  [p2-l43]  “figure-level.” Axes-level functions behave like most plotting functions in the matplotlib.
  110  p2  paragraph  [p2-l44]  pyplot namespace. By default, they hook into the state machine that tracks a “current”
  111  p2  paragraph  [p2-l45]  figure and add a layer to it, but they can also accept a matplotlib axes object to control
  112  p2  paragraph  [p2-l46]  where the plot is drawn, similar to using the matplotlib “object-oriented” interface. Figure-
  113  p2  paragraph  [p2-l47]  level functions create their own figure when invoked, allowing them to “facet” the dataset
  114  p2  paragraph  [p2-l48]  by creating multiple conditional subplots, along with adding conveniences such as putting
  115  p2  paragraph  [p2-l49]  the legend outside the space of the plot by default. Each figure-level function corresponds
  116  p2  paragraph  [p2-l50]  to several axes-level functions that serve similar purposes, with a single parameter selecting
  117  p2  figure  [p2-d37]  
  118  p2  furniture  [p2-l51]  Waskom, M. L., (2021). seaborn: statistical data visualization. Journal of Open Source Software, 6(60), 3021. https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.
  119  p2  furniture  [p2-l53]  2
  120  p2  furniture  [p2-l52]  03021
  121  p3  figure  [p3-i1]  
  122  p3  paragraph  [p3-l1]  the kind of plot to make. For example, the displot function can produce several different
  123  p3  paragraph  [p3-l2]  representations of a distribution, including a histogram, kernel density estimate, or empirical
  124  p3  paragraph  [p3-l3]  cumulative distribution function. The figure-level functions make use of a seaborn class that
  125  p3  paragraph  [p3-l4]  controls the layout of the figure, mediating between the axes-level functions and matplotlib.
  126  p3  paragraph  [p3-l5]  These classes are part of the public API and can be used directly for advanced applications.
  127  p3  paragraph  [p3-l6]  One of the key features in seaborn is that variables in a dataset can be automatically
  128  p3  paragraph  [p3-l7]  “mapped” to visual attributes of the graph. These transformations are referred to as “se-
  129  p3  paragraph  [p3-l8]  mantic” mappings because they endow the attributes with meaning vis a vis the dataset. By
  130  p3  paragraph  [p3-l9]  freeing the user from manually specifying the transformations – which often requires looping
  131  p3  paragraph  [p3-l10]  and multiple function invocations when using matplotlib directly – seaborn allows rapid
  132  p3  paragraph  [p3-l11]  exploration of multidimensional relationships. To further aid efficiency, the default parameters
  133  p3  paragraph  [p3-l12]  of the mappings are opinionated. For example, when mapping the color of the elements in a
  134  p3  paragraph  [p3-l13]  plot, seaborn infers whether to use a qualitative or quantitative mapping based on whether
  135  p3  paragraph  [p3-l14]  the input data are categorical or numeric. This behavior can be further configured or even
  136  p3  paragraph  [p3-l15]  overridden by setting additional parameters of each plotting function.
  137  p3  paragraph  [p3-l16]  Several seaborn functions also apply statistical transformations to the input data before
  138  p3  paragraph  [p3-l17]  plotting, ranging from estimating the mean or median to fitting a general linear model. When
  139  p3  paragraph  [p3-l18]  data are transformed in this way, seaborn automatically computes and shows error bars to
  140  p3  paragraph  [p3-l19]  provide a visual cue about the uncertainty of the estimate. Unlike many graphical libraries,
  141  p3  paragraph  [p3-l20]  seaborn shows 95% confidence interval error bars by default, rather than standard errors. The
  142  p3  paragraph  [p3-l21]  confidence intervals are computed with a bootstrap algorithm, allowing them to generalize over
  143  p3  paragraph  [p3-l22]  many different statistics, and the default level allows the user to perform “inference by eye”
  144  p3  paragraph  [p3-l23]  (Cumming & Finch, 2005). Historically, error bar specification has been relatively limited, but
  145  p3  paragraph  [p3-l24]  a forthcoming release (v0.12) will introduce a new configuration system that makes it possible
  146  p3  paragraph  [p3-l25]  to show nonparametric percentile intervals and scaled analytic estimates of standard error or
  147  p3  paragraph  [p3-l26]  standard deviation statistics.
  148  p3  paragraph  [p3-l27]  seaborn aims to be flexible about the format of its input data. The most convenient usage
  149  p3  paragraph  [p3-l28]  pattern provides a pandas (McKinney, 2010) dataframe with variables encoded in a long-
  150  p3  paragraph  [p3-l29]  form or “tidy” (Wickham, 2014) format. With this format, columns in the dataframe can
  151  p3  paragraph  [p3-l30]  be explicitly assigned to roles in the plot, such as specifying the x and y positions of a
  152  p3  paragraph  [p3-l31]  scatterplot along with size and shape semantics. Long-form data supports efficient exploration
  153  p3  paragraph  [p3-l32]  and prototyping because variables can be assigned different roles in the plot without modifying
  154  p3  paragraph  [p3-l33]  anything about the original dataset. But most seaborn functions can also consume and
  155  p3  paragraph  [p3-l34]  visualize “wide-form” data, typically producing similar output to how the analogous matplot
  156  p3  paragraph  [p3-l35]  lib function would interpret a 2D array (e.g., producing a boxplot where each box represents a
  157  p3  paragraph  [p3-l36]  column in the dataframe) while making use of the index and column names to label the graph.
  158  p3  paragraph  [p3-l37]  Using the label information in a pandas object can help make plots that are interpretable
  159  p3  paragraph  [p3-l38]  without further tweaking – reducing the chance of interpretive errors – but seaborn also
  160  p3  paragraph  [p3-l39]  accepts data from a variety of more basic formats, including numpy (Harris et al., 2020) arrays
  161  p3  paragraph  [p3-l40]  and simple Python collection types.
  162  p3  paragraph  [p3-l41]  seaborn also offers multiple built-in themes that users can select to modify the visual appear-
  163  p3  paragraph  [p3-l42]  ance of their graphs. The themes make use of the matplotlib rcParams system, meaning
  164  p3  paragraph  [p3-l43]  that they will take effect for any figure created using matplotlib, not just those made by
  165  p3  paragraph  [p3-l44]  seaborn. The themes are defined by two disjoint sets of parameters that separately control
  166  p3  paragraph  [p3-l45]  the style of the figure and the scaling of its elements (such as line widths and font sizes). This
  167  p3  paragraph  [p3-l46]  separation makes it easy to generate multiple versions of a figure that are scaled for different
  168  p3  paragraph  [p3-l47]  contexts, such as written reports and slide presentations. The theming system can also be
  169  p3  paragraph  [p3-l48]  used to set a default color palette. As color is particularly important in data visualization and
  170  p3  paragraph  [p3-l49]  no single set of defaults is universally appropriate, every plotting function makes it easy to
  171  p3  paragraph  [p3-l50]  choose an alternate categorical palette or continuous gradient mapping that is well-suited for
  172  p3  paragraph  [p3-l51]  the particular dataset and plot type. The seaborn documentation contains a tutorial on the
  173  p3  paragraph  [p3-l52]  use of color in data visualization to help users make this important decision.
  174  p3  paragraph  [p3-l53]  seaborn does not aim to completely encapsulate or replace matplotlib. Many useful
  175  p3  figure  [p3-d1]  
  176  p3  furniture  [p3-l54]  Waskom, M. L., (2021). seaborn: statistical data visualization. Journal of Open Source Software, 6(60), 3021. https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.
  177  p3  furniture  [p3-l56]  3
  178  p3  furniture  [p3-l55]  03021
  179  p4  figure  [p4-i1]  
  180  p4  paragraph  [p4-l1]  graphs can be created through the seaborn interface, but more advanced applications –
  181  p4  paragraph  [p4-l2]  such as defining composite figures with multiple arbitrary plot types – will require importing
  182  p4  paragraph  [p4-l3]  and using matplotlib as well. Even when calling only seaborn functions, deeper cus-
  183  p4  paragraph  [p4-l4]  tomization of the plot appearance is achieved by specifying parameters that are passed-
  184  p4  paragraph  [p4-l5]  through to the underlying matplotlib functions, and tweaks to the default axis limits,
  185  p4  paragraph  [p4-l6]  ticks, and labels are made by calling methods on the matplotlib object that axes-level
  186  p4  paragraph  [p4-l7]  seaborn functions return. This approach is distinct from other statistical graphing sys-
  187  p4  paragraph  [p4-l8]  tems, such as ggplot2 (Wickham, 2016). While seaborn offers some similar features
  188  p4  paragraph  [p4-l9]  and, in some cases, uses similar terminology to ggplot2, it does not implement the for-
  189  p4  paragraph  [p4-l10]  mal Grammar of Graphics and cannot be used to produce arbitrary visualizations. Rather, its
  190  p4  paragraph  [p4-l11]  aim is to facilitate rapid exploration and prototyping through named functions and opinion-
  191  p4  paragraph  [p4-l12]  ated defaults while allowing the user to leverage the considerable flexibility of matplotlib
  192  p4  paragraph  [p4-l13]  to create more domain-specific graphics and to polish figures for publication. An exam-
  193  p4  paragraph  [p4-l14]  ple of a successful use of this approach to produce reproducible figures can be found at
  194  p4  paragraph  [p4-l15]  https://github.com/WagnerLabPapers/Waskom_PNAS_2017 (Waskom & Wagner, 2017).
