  39  p1  paragraph  [p1-l43]  Summary
  40  p1  figure  [p1-d26..p1-d28 (3)]  
  41  p1  paragraph  [p1-l44]  PyMedPhys is an open-source medical physics library built for Python by a diverse community
  42  p1  figure  [p1-d29..p1-d31 (3)]  
  43  p1  paragraph  [p1-l45]  that values and prioritizes code sharing, review, continuous improvement, and peer development.
  44  p1  paragraph  [p1-l46]  PyMedPhys aims to simplify and enhance both research and clinical work related to medical
  45  p1  figure  [p1-d32]  
  46  p1  paragraph  [p1-l47]  physics. It is inspired by the Astropy Project (Astropy Collaboration, 2013); a highly successful
  47  p1  figure  [p1-d33..p1-d35 (3)]  
  48  p1  figure  [p1-d36..p1-d37 (2)]  
  49  p1  paragraph  [p1-l48]  collaborative work of our physics peers in astronomy.
  50  p1  paragraph  [p1-l49]  Statement of need
  51  p1  paragraph  [p1-l50]  Medical radiation applications are subject to fast-paced technological advancements. This is
  52  p1  paragraph  [p1-l51]  particularly true in the field of radiation oncology, where the implementation of increasingly
  53  p1  paragraph  [p1-l52]  sophisticated technologies requires increasingly complex processes to maintain the improving
  54  p1  paragraph  [p1-l53]  standard of care. To help address this challenge, software tools that improve the quality, safety
  55  p1  paragraph  [p1-l54]  and efficiency of clinical tasks are increasingly being developed in-house (Arumugam et al.,
  56  p1  paragraph  [p1-l55]  2016; Bakhtiari et al., 2011; Bhagroo et al., 2019; Chan et al., 2015; Edvardsson et al., 2018;
  57  p1  paragraph  [p1-l56]  Huang et al., 2021; Inaniwa & Kanematsu, 2018; Keall et al., 2014; Kimura et al., 2021; Kuo
  58  p1  paragraph  [p1-l57]  et al., 2020; Latala et al., 2020; Li et al., 2010; Maughan et al., 2019; Skouboe et al., 2019).
  59  p1  paragraph  [p1-l58]  Commercial options are often prohibitively expensive or insufficiently tailored to an individual
  60  p1  paragraph  [p1-l59]  clinic’s needs. On the other hand, in-house development efforts are often limited to a single
  61  p1  paragraph  [p1-l60]  institution. Similar tools that could otherwise be shared are instead “reinvented” in clinics
  62  p1  paragraph  [p1-l61]  worldwide on a routine basis. Moreover, individual institutions typically lack the personnel and
  63  p1  paragraph  [p1-l62]  resources to incorporate simple aspects of good development practice or to properly maintain
  64  p1  paragraph  [p1-l63]  in-house software.
  65  p1  paragraph  [p1-l64]  By creating and promoting an open-source repository, PyMedPhys aims to improve the quality
  66  p1  paragraph  [p1-l65]  and accessibility of existing software solutions to problems faced across a range of medical
  67  p1  paragraph  [p1-l66]  radiation applications, especially those traditionally within the remit of medical physicists. These
  68  p1  paragraph  [p1-l67]  solutions can be broadly categorised in two areas: data extraction/conversion of proprietary
  69  p1  figure  [p1-d38]  
  70  p1  furniture  [p1-l27]  DOI: 10.21105/joss.04555
  71  p1  furniture  [p1-l28]  Software
  72  p1  furniture  [p1-l29]  • Review
  73  p1  furniture  [p1-l30]  • Repository
