  86  p1  furniture  [p1-l68]  Biggs et al. (2022). PyMedPhys: A community effort to develop an open, Python-based standard library for medical physics applications. Journal
  87  p1  furniture  [p1-l70]  1
  88  p1  furniture  [p1-l69]  of Open Source Software, 7(78), 4555. https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.04555.
  89  p2  figure  [p2-i1]  
  90  p2  paragraph  [p2-l1]  formats from a variety of radiotherapy systems, and manipulation of standard radiotherapy data
  91  p2  paragraph  [p2-l2]  to perform quality assurance (QA) tasks that are otherwise time-consuming or lack commercial
  92  p2  paragraph  [p2-l3]  solutions with the desired flexibility or true function.
  93  p2  paragraph  [p2-l4]  Data extraction and conversion currently includes: two treatment planning systems, an oncology
  94  p2  paragraph  [p2-l5]  information system, and a linear accelerator vendor family of systems. Data in proprietary
  95  p2  paragraph  [p2-l6]  formats from these systems are extracted and converted to allow for integration in a myriad of
  96  p2  paragraph  [p2-l7]  applications. Applications that use planning system information include: electron cut-out factor
  97  p2  paragraph  [p2-l8]  determination, CT extension, and extraction of dose information for patient QA purposes.
  98  p2  paragraph  [p2-l9]  Applications that use the oncology information systems include: clinical dashboards that
  99  p2  paragraph  [p2-l10]  summarise data, quality task tracking, and comparison of dose information to planning systems.
  100  p2  paragraph  [p2-l11]  Applications that use the linear accelerator data include: patient specific QA analysis against
  101  p2  paragraph  [p2-l12]  planning data, and analysis of machine performance such as the Winston-Lutz test.
  102  p2  paragraph  [p2-l13]  QA tasks using standard radiotherapy data include: anonymisation, extraction of dose data
  103  p2  paragraph  [p2-l14]  for analysis, manipulation of contour files to allow merging or adjustments/scaling of relative
  104  p2  paragraph  [p2-l15]  electron density, modifying machine names in plans, and most frequently used, the calculation
  105  p2  paragraph  [p2-l16]  of a Gamma index, a widely recognised metric in radiotherapy analysis that quantifies the
  106  p2  paragraph  [p2-l17]  difference between measured and calculated dose distributions on a point-by-point basis in
  107  p2  paragraph  [p2-l18]  terms of both dose and distance to agreement (DTA) differences.
  108  p2  paragraph  [p2-l19]  Many of these tools are in use clinically at affiliated sites, and additionally, aspects of PyMedPhys
  109  p2  paragraph  [p2-l20]  are implemented around the world for some applications. Many parties have embraced the
  110  p2  paragraph  [p2-l21]  gamma analysis module (Castle et al., 2022; Cronholm et al., 2020; Gajewski et al., 2021;
  111  p2  paragraph  [p2-l22]  Galić et al., 2020; Lysakovski et al., 2021; Milan et al., 2019; Pastor-Serrano & Perkó, 2021;
  112  p2  paragraph  [p2-l23]  Rodrı́guez et al., 2020; Spezialetti et al., 2021; Tsuneda et al., 2021; Yang et al., 2022), while
  113  p2  paragraph  [p2-l24]  implementations of the electron cutout factor module and others (Baltz & Kirsner, 2021;
  114  p2  paragraph  [p2-l25]  Douglass & Keal, 2021; Rembish, 2021) have also been reported. Additionally, the work
  115  p2  paragraph  [p2-l26]  has been recognized by the European Society for Radiotherapy and Oncology (ESTRO) and
  116  p2  paragraph  [p2-l27]  referenced as recommended literature in their 3rd Edition of Core Curriculum for Medical
  117  p2  paragraph  [p2-l28]  Physics Experts in Radiotherapy (Bert et al., 2021).
  118  p2  paragraph  [p2-l29]  Acknowledgements
  119  p2  paragraph  [p2-l30]  We acknowledge the support of all who have contributed to the development of PyMedPhys
  120  p2  paragraph  [p2-l31]  along the way.
